00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.520
Every so often, I come across
a study or set of studies

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:33.870
that I get so excited about

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.420
that I start telling
everybody in my immediate life

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.360
and I insist on also sharing it with you,

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.680
the listeners of this podcast,

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.540
because I find the information

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:45.360
to be so incredibly
interesting and actionable.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.130
The two studies that I'm going to discuss

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.620
both relate to sleep and sleep states

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.660
and how to access better sleep.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:54.600
The first one was published
in the journal Cell Reports,

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.340
Cell Press journal, excellent journal.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:57.427
And the title of this paper is

00:04:57.427 --> 00:05:00.307
"Rapid and Reversible
Control of Human Metabolism

00:05:00.307 --> 00:05:01.920
"by Individual Sleep States".

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.710
We will provide a link to this
study in the show captions.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:08.850
The first author is Nora Nowak, N-O-W-A-K.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:11.880
And basically what they
did is they measured

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.130
the different forms of
metabolism that occur

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.720
while humans sleep.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:16.553
As far as I know,

00:05:16.553 --> 00:05:19.320
this is one of the first
studies of this kind.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.120
There are many studies of metabolism.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.650
There are many studies of sleep.

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:25.710
This study focused on how
different states of sleep

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:27.450
such as rapid eye movement sleep,

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:28.830
which is associated with dreaming

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:30.870
and high emotional content dreams

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:32.250
versus slow wave sleep,

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:34.380
which tends to be more
focused on physical repair

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.270
of the body, more mundane dreams,

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.910
how those different states
of mind during sleep

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:41.610
relate to different aspects of metabolism

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:43.740
and what they found was
absolutely fascinating.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.030
First of all,

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:48.810
they found that sleep states
regulate more than 50%,

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:52.290
half of all the metabolite
features detected

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:54.060
in human breath.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:54.930
What does that mean?

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:56.460
Well, it turns out that you can figure out

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.800
what humans are
metabolizing in particular,

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.540
more lipids or more carbohydrate,

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.700
whether or not they're relying
more on glucose metabolism

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.470
based on the contents of their breath.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.327
This is true during
waking and during sleep.

00:06:06.327 --> 00:06:07.530
And this is what allowed them

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:08.820
to do these incredible measurements

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.130
of what's being metabolized during sleep.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:14.430
They measured close to
2000 metabolites in breath,

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:17.340
every 10 seconds across
the entire night's sleep.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.320
And what they found was that

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:22.590
there are major pathways
related to lipid metabolism,

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:23.670
fat metabolism,

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:25.560
or to carbohydrate metabolism

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.240
or other forms of metabolism

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:30.840
that are up or down regulated
as human beings transition

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:33.060
between slow wave sleep,

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:34.620
rapid eye movement sleep

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:35.453
and waking.

00:06:35.453 --> 00:06:36.286
And you might say waking?

00:06:36.286 --> 00:06:38.100
Well, yes, they also looked
as people fell asleep

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.180
and as they emerged from sleep.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:41.280
And believe it or not every
so often during sleep,

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:42.900
you wake up, you didn't know this,

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:43.890
but you wake up in the
middle of the night,

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:46.050
you look around and you go back to sleep.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:47.640
You're not aware of it
because you're still

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:52.080
in a rather sleep like state,
although you are awake.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:53.430
What they found was that sleep

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:54.870
and the various states of sleep

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:57.870
regulated individual metabolic pathways.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:06:59.340
They found for instance,

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.040
that the switch from sleep to wakefulness

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.230
reduces fatty acid oxidation.

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:05.520
So that means while you're asleep,

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.680
you're oxidating more fatty acids.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.040
And as you wake up, that
becomes less the case.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:12.990
And there's a switch in slow wave sleep

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:16.140
that increases fatty acid oxidation.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:19.080
And there's this transition
from rapid eye movement sleep

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:20.550
to other aspects of sleep

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:23.490
that brings about things
like the so-called TCA cycle.

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:24.990
Some of you familiar with metabolism

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:26.610
will be familiar with the TCA cycle,

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:28.800
the so-called tricarboxylic acid cycle,

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.170
intermediates, that's fancy nerd speak

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:35.070
for specific aspects of
metabolism being regulated

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:38.430
during this rapid eye
movement sleep transition.

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:39.810
What does all this mean?

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:41.070
And how is this actionable?

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:44.250
Well, on many episodes of
the Huberman Lab Podcast

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:46.620
such as the master sleep episode

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:47.940
and the episode that we're going

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:49.950
into in further depth today,

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:52.710
we're going to talk about sleep
and how to optimize sleep.

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:54.570
It's been thought of,

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:57.150
but not really tamped down

00:07:57.150 --> 00:08:00.360
that quality and depth of
sleep and duration of sleep

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.820
is important for metabolism
during the daytime.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:03.990
And indeed that's the case.

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:06.780
If people are sleep deprived
or they're not sleeping enough,

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:08.640
things like glucose metabolism, et cetera,

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:10.680
get really disrupted during the daytime.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:12.750
But what this current study shows

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:15.990
is that the metabolism that
you experience during sleep,

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:17.220
or to be more specific,

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:19.860
the range of different types of metabolism

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:21.420
that you experience during sleep

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:23.370
may serve to tune up

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:26.220
or to ensure that the
specific aspects of metabolism

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:29.550
that you require during
wakefulness are working properly.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:30.840
In addition to that,

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:33.870
this study clearly shows
that getting enough sleep

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:35.160
allows you to transition

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:37.770
through all the various
forms of metabolism

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.450
and use all those different forms

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:41.580
of metabolites during sleep

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:43.620
in a way that's immensely beneficial

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:45.810
for the systems of your brain and body.

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:47.880
So the take home message here is that,

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:49.980
as the author state,

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:53.670
sleep and experiencing the
different states of sleep,

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:55.890
slow wave sleep early in
the night, predominantly,

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:59.130
plus rapid eye movement sleep
toward the end of the night

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:02.580
is extremely important for
optimizing metabolic circuits

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:04.560
for human performance and health.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:07.200
In other words, by not getting
sufficient duration sleep,

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:08.760
you're not allowing your body

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.340
and brain to transition through
all the different aspects

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.140
of fuel utilization

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:16.320
and you're not teaching
your brain and body

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:19.110
how to use similar types of
fuels during wakefulness.

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:20.820
So again, all of this points to the fact

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:23.160
that we need to be
getting sufficient quality

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:24.300
and duration of sleep.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:27.180
So if you're sleep deprived,
even by an hour or so,

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:29.460
you're going to get far less
rapid eye movement sleep

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:31.200
because rapid eye movement
sleep is what occurs

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.540
toward the end of a sleep night.

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:34.590
During the early part of the night,

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:36.780
far more slow wave sleep.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:39.480
In getting less rapid eye movement sleep,

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:41.280
we know it makes you
more emotionally labile,

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:44.130
but now we know it's also
going to alter certain forms

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:46.260
of glucose metabolism

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:47.760
during the night

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:49.770
and during wakefulness.

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:52.800
So that all underscores
the need to get sleep.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:54.750
But then the question is
how to get enough sleep

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:55.680
and how to make sure you get

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:57.450
into all these different sleep states.

00:09:57.450 --> 00:09:58.860
And this is particularly important

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:01.140
for you so-called night owls.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:02.940
There's a lot of controversy out there

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:05.760
as to whether or not different
so-called chronotypes exist,

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:09.030
that is people who just
naturally or genetically

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:10.710
want to be an early bird,

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:12.090
wake up early and go to bed early.

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:14.580
So these people that wake up at 4:00 AM

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:16.770
and would be most comfortable going to bed

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:19.530
by seven or 8:00 PM or 9:00 PM.

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:21.420
Then they are so called night owls,

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:24.870
people that would feel
best or tend to feel best

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:28.740
when they go to sleep at 1:00
AM, 2:00 AM, even 3:00 AM,

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:31.050
and like to wake up
later 8:00, 9:00, 10:00,

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:33.960
or even 11:00 AM or noon.

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:34.793
And then of course,

00:10:34.793 --> 00:10:36.240
most people go to sleep somewhere

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:37.920
between 10:00 PM and midnight

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:41.220
and wake up somewhere
between 5:00 AM and 7:00 AM,

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:45.060
or I suppose more typically
6:00 AM and 8:00 AM.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:47.160
Now whether or not real chronotypes exist

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:50.130
or whether or not people
simply select schedules

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:52.380
for sleep and wakefulness that they like

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:53.730
because of their social schedules

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:55.050
or the activities they enjoy.

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:57.180
For instance, some people
like to really go out,

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:59.250
they like to go out dancing or hear music,

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:02.070
or spend time in venues that
are only open late at night,

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:04.500
and don't even open until noon or after.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:07.470
Other people like myself
rarely go out at night

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:08.700
but I like to get up early.

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:09.600
I like to exercise.

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:11.700
I like to see the sunrise, et cetera.

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:13.560
So I don't know if I'm a morning person

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:14.393
or an evening person.

00:11:14.393 --> 00:11:15.780
I just know the things I enjoy

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:17.520
tend to happen in the
early part of the day.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:19.800
And the things that I
don't enjoy quite as much

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:21.690
tend to happen late at night.

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:22.800
Regardless of whether or not

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:24.870
there are real genetic propensities

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:27.390
to be a night owl or an early bird

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:30.900
or a sort of typical person
right there in the middle,

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:32.010
it's very important that people

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:33.870
have some control over
their sleep schedule

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:35.130
and even more important,

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:37.740
that people are able to
get sufficient amount

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:40.170
of REM sleep and slow wave sleep,

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:41.070
for many reasons,

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:42.720
but including the reasons I discussed

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:45.153
in the previous study
related to metabolism.

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:47.520
I'm very excited, therefore,

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:49.620
about a study that came
out in sleep medicine.

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:51.840
This was a few years ago, but
somehow I missed this one.

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:53.370
It was published in 2019.

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:54.937
And the title of this article is

00:11:54.937 --> 00:11:57.007
"Resetting the Late Timing of 'Night Owls'

00:11:57.007 --> 00:11:58.927
"Has a Positive Impact on Mental Health,

00:11:58.927 --> 00:12:01.020
"Physical Health and Performance".

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:02.880
This is a study done in humans,

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:04.710
focusing specifically on people

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:07.530
that like to stay up late and sleep in,

00:12:07.530 --> 00:12:10.920
but who desire to be able
to get up and feel alert

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:12.570
in order to go to work or study

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:14.490
and they want to go to
sleep a bit earlier.

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:15.990
And so there are a lot of questions

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:18.090
embedded in this study in particular,

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:20.130
whether or not people can
actually shift their schedule

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:21.870
by a few hours or more.

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:24.060
Some people out there contend
that if you're a night owl,

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:25.680
that's just going to be impossible

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:27.660
or very, very challenging to do.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:29.280
Turns out it's not impossible

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:30.720
and it's not even that challenging to do

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:32.130
provided you do the right things.

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:33.630
Just a brief overview of the study

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:36.060
and then I'll give you the key takeaways.

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:37.950
It was a randomized control trial.

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:39.660
It involved a number of different people,

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:41.010
both male and female.

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:44.220
And what they did was they
used non-pharmacological,

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:47.190
practical interventions
in a real world setting,

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:48.510
here I'm paraphrasing.

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:50.880
They used targeted light exposure.

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:53.640
They used consistent sleep, wake times.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:55.650
They used fixed meal times,

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:57.780
caffeine intake and exercise.

00:12:57.780 --> 00:12:59.610
And this is one of the reasons
I love this study so much

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:00.540
because I've done episodes

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:01.920
where I've talked about temperature,

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:04.320
exercise, feeding, and most importantly,

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:06.600
light exposure as a way to control

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:08.010
and shift your sleep wake cycles,

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:11.610
your so-called circadian
timing and entrainment.

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:12.720
What did they find?

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:16.357
Well, they found "Significant improvements

00:13:16.357 --> 00:13:19.950
"in terms of mood so far
less depression and stress",

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:20.947
subjectively measured,

00:13:20.947 --> 00:13:23.070
"as well as improved
cognitive performance",

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:24.180
that was objectively measured.

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:25.620
So improved reaction times,

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:27.750
improved physical grip strength,

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:30.570
which is actually a measure
not just of strength per se,

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:32.670
but also of nervous system function

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:35.490
and a number of things
that people could do

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:37.560
in order to optimize their morning hours,

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:40.080
even though they were
night owls previously.

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:41.040
What did they have people do?

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:43.350
Well, I'm going to just
going to list this off

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:45.480
sort of rapid fire succession.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:46.890
Then we'll provide a link to the study

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:47.910
if you want to learn more.

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:50.070
First of all, they told participants

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:52.290
to try and wake up two to three hours

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:53.910
before their typical wake up time,

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:54.750
two to three hours.

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:56.340
That seems brutal to me

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:57.660
and probably seems brutal to you

00:13:57.660 --> 00:14:00.060
if you're somebody who
typically wakes up at 10:00 AM

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:04.140
to try and get up at eight
or even 7:00 AM consistently,

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:06.630
but they were also asked to maximize

00:14:06.630 --> 00:14:08.610
outdoor light exposure during the mornings

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:10.950
for reasons that if you've
listened to this podcast before,

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:12.840
if you've heard me talk about before,

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:14.400
you know, that I'm
constantly talking about,

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:15.960
I'll probably go into the grave shouting,

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:18.630
please get as much light exposure

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:20.610
from sunlight early in the day as possible

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:21.750
because it sets in motion

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:23.490
a huge number of things
that are beneficial

00:14:23.490 --> 00:14:25.170
for your mental health
and physical health,

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:26.880
including dopamine production,

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:29.220
timing melatonin production correctly,

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:31.170
reducing cortisol peaks late in the day,

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:32.820
et cetera, et cetera.

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:35.550
So they asked them to get a
lot of outdoor light exposure.

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:37.170
They didn't give them a specific amount.

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:38.003
What they said,

00:14:38.003 --> 00:14:40.380
maximize outdoor light
exposure during the mornings,

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:41.550
the time before noon.

00:14:41.550 --> 00:14:43.410
And again, they had them
waking up two to three hours

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:45.300
before their habitual wake up time.

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:46.440
They were also told,

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:47.460
and this is very important

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:49.230
if you're going to shift
your schedule earlier,

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:52.260
to try and keep sleep wake times fixed

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:54.720
between their work days
and their weekends.

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:56.400
So not sleeping in on the weekends

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:58.140
or not having any sleep in days,

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:00.900
regardless of how well they
slept the night before.

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:01.800
How fixed?

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:06.450
Within 15 to 30 minutes of
their pre-designated time.

00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:09.060
So if they were waking up
at seven o'clock one day,

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:12.030
they set their alarm and they
made sure they got out of bed

00:15:12.030 --> 00:15:15.420
at seven o'clock every day
plus or minus 30 minutes,

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:17.850
but never later than 7:30,

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:19.593
never earlier than 6:30.

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:23.220
Participants were also
asked to try and go to sleep

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:26.220
two to three hours before
their habitual bedtime.

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:28.110
So again, these are people
that want to stay up late,

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:30.150
like 11:00 PM, perhaps,

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:33.060
but even as late as 1:00 AM or 2:00 AM.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:35.220
And now they are asked to go to sleep

00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:37.800
two to three hours before
their habitual bedtime

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:40.440
and to wake up two to three hours earlier,

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:41.590
as I mentioned earlier.

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:43.560
They were also told,

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:45.660
and I love this because it
fits with many of the things

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:47.760
we've talked about on this podcast before,

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:50.850
to try and limit light
exposure during the evenings,

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:54.810
dim the lights or limit
altogether artificial lights.

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:55.890
Lot of reasons for that,

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:57.600
I covered that in the
Master Sleep episode,

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:00.750
I covered that in the Optimized
Health Using Light episode.

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:02.880
You can find those at HubermanLab.com.

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:04.530
They're asking them to do that here.

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:06.960
And they asked participants
to keep a regular schedule

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:08.220
for their daily meals,

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:11.220
not eating on the hour consistently,

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:14.160
you know, at 9:00 AM,
noon, 3:00 PM, exactly.

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:17.250
But within again, about 15 to 30 minutes,

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:19.350
they're always eating at the same times.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:20.610
That was also important.

00:16:20.610 --> 00:16:22.710
And again, that's because
we have these so-called

00:16:22.710 --> 00:16:24.510
food in trained circadian clocks.

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:27.510
When you eat, tells your
body when to be alert

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:30.270
and when you're not
eating, when to be asleep.

00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:33.150
And they were told to
not drink any caffeine

00:16:33.150 --> 00:16:35.250
after 3:00 PM in the afternoon.

00:16:35.250 --> 00:16:37.590
Another theme that we've
talked about on this podcast.

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:41.880
They were also told not to
take naps after 4:00 PM.

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:45.180
Naps are an interesting feature
of the sleep wake cycle.

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:47.010
To be very brief about this

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:48.330
and to pull from the episode

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:50.130
that I did with world sleep expert

00:16:50.130 --> 00:16:53.010
from the University of
California Berkeley, Matt Walker,

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:55.170
naps are great for many people,

00:16:55.170 --> 00:16:58.020
but don't nap if it interferes
with your nighttime sleep.

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:01.590
And in this study, they told
them don't nap after 4:00 PM.

00:17:01.590 --> 00:17:04.980
And if you are a napper, don't
nap for more than 90 minutes.

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:05.940
10 minute naps are fine.

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:07.170
20 minute naps are fine.

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:08.850
Zero minute naps are fine,

00:17:08.850 --> 00:17:10.740
but don't nap for more than 90 minutes.

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:12.180
And don't nap after 4:00 PM.

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:15.870
And to exercise during the morning.

00:17:15.870 --> 00:17:17.460
Now this one can be a bit controversial

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:19.290
because I know a lot of the PTs out there

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:22.410
and a lot of the online, you know,

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:23.940
gym rats and people who,

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:25.710
and runners too, for that matter,

00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:29.310
will say, well according to
body temperature and research,

00:17:29.310 --> 00:17:31.140
it's best to exercise in the afternoon.

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:32.880
Look, it's better to exercise sometime

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:34.200
as opposed to no time,

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:37.440
but if you're focused on how
to shift your schedule earlier,

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:39.270
meaning get up earlier
and go to sleep earlier,

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:42.810
this study had people exercise
in the early part of the day,

00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:45.720
certainly before 2:00 PM
and ideally before noon.

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:48.240
So again, this is a really important study

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:51.420
because it combines a lot
of different variables

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:53.640
to arrive at this very impressive shift

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:55.920
where people can get up
two to three hours earlier

00:17:55.920 --> 00:17:57.720
and then pretty
consistently and reflexively

00:17:57.720 --> 00:17:59.490
start going to bed two
to three hours earlier,

00:17:59.490 --> 00:18:00.810
feeling more alert during the day,

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:02.490
again, improvements in
cognitive performance,

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:06.090
mood and physical performance,
grip, strength, et cetera.

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:08.550
Very few studies are able to

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:11.100
or are willing to tackle so many variables

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:12.960
and combine them in one study.

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:15.570
This paper, I think, does a
marvelous job of doing this

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:18.000
and is incorporating
things that individually,

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:19.710
each have some support for them

00:18:19.710 --> 00:18:22.050
in animal studies and
previous human studies.

00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:22.883
But as far as I know,

00:18:22.883 --> 00:18:24.270
this is one of the few studies

00:18:24.270 --> 00:18:27.120
that really combines all
these different features

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:28.920
in one place,

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:31.260
eating times, keeping those consistent,

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:35.220
getting maximal sunlight
exposure earlier in the day,

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:36.660
getting up at a consistent time,

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:40.200
going to sleep at a
consistent time and on and on.

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:41.910
It's a really marvelous
study for that reason.

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:44.310
And I think for any of
you that are night owls

00:18:44.310 --> 00:18:47.760
and any of you that want to
reinforce your early waking

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:49.500
and early to bed times,

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:51.360
and I think for most all of you

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:53.550
who fall into that general middle category

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:54.930
of tend to go to sleep somewhere

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:56.250
between 10:00 PM and midnight,

00:18:56.250 --> 00:18:57.120
'cause that's most people

00:18:57.120 --> 00:19:00.630
and tend to wake up sometime
between 6:00 AM and 8:00 AM,

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:02.580
well, maybe you want to
become more of an early riser

00:19:02.580 --> 00:19:04.200
or maybe you're going to travel

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:07.170
or the seasons are changing
and you want to shift your time

00:19:07.170 --> 00:19:08.940
or you have a new job, et cetera,

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:10.800
or something that's actually very common

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:12.030
in terms of relationship struggle.

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:14.580
You want to match your wake sleep times,

00:19:14.580 --> 00:19:17.070
or maybe you want to offset
your wake sleep times

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:19.140
from a significant other,

00:19:19.140 --> 00:19:21.300
these sorts of approaches
that I described here

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:23.940
and that are supported
by the data in this paper

00:19:23.940 --> 00:19:26.730
are absolutely powerful
and science supported.

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:28.230
And I'm certain that if
you were to apply them,

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:30.270
that you would see
essentially the same effects

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:31.230
that were observed here.

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:32.070
Before we begin,

