00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:05.310
Let's talk about how to optimize

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:08.400
and indeed how to control
your brain chemistry

00:24:08.400 --> 00:24:10.860
for a sake of health and performance.

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:12.660
Now in order to do that,

00:24:12.660 --> 00:24:14.130
we all need to be on the same page

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:16.050
about some basic facts.

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:17.190
Some of those basic facts

00:24:17.190 --> 00:24:19.467
involve learning some basic biology.

00:24:19.467 --> 00:24:21.960
And I promise that even if
you don't have a biology

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:23.340
or chemistry background,

00:24:23.340 --> 00:24:25.500
everything I'm about to
say should be accessible

00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:26.673
and clear to you.

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:28.740
The important thing to know

00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:31.830
is that your brain and your spinal cord

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:35.160
and the rest of your
so-called nervous system

00:24:35.160 --> 00:24:37.230
control all the organs of your body

00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:40.170
and that all the organs
of your body feed back,

00:24:40.170 --> 00:24:43.140
meaning they communicate through
chemicals and other means,

00:24:43.140 --> 00:24:44.910
to your nervous system.

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:48.150
Now your nervous system plays
a particularly important role

00:24:48.150 --> 00:24:50.130
in generating everything from sleep

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:52.020
to wakefulness, creativity,

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:54.690
stress, calm, et cetera,

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:59.430
by way of a particular type
of cell-cell interaction.

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:02.880
And that's called synaptic communication.

00:25:02.880 --> 00:25:04.590
What is synaptic communication?

00:25:04.590 --> 00:25:06.810
Well, in order to understand that

00:25:06.810 --> 00:25:08.670
let's dial back a little bit further

00:25:08.670 --> 00:25:10.830
and try and understand for a moment

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:13.260
what makes up your nervous system.

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:14.220
In its simplest form,

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:16.650
your nervous system is made of nerve cells

00:25:16.650 --> 00:25:18.600
that we call neurons.

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:21.840
Neurons communicate with one
another through chemicals.

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:25.290
They release certain chemicals
that make other neurons

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:28.530
more or less likely to
be electrically active.

00:25:28.530 --> 00:25:30.270
What do we mean by electrically active?

00:25:30.270 --> 00:25:31.830
We mean,

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:32.850
as it sounds,

00:25:32.850 --> 00:25:36.330
electricity passing down through cells

00:25:36.330 --> 00:25:40.320
and then literally causing
electricity in other cells.

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:41.850
The simplest way to think about this

00:25:41.850 --> 00:25:44.340
is maybe when you were a
kid or maybe even still now,

00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:46.830
you would wear socks and
you'd shuffle along the floor

00:25:46.830 --> 00:25:47.910
to generate some static electricity,

00:25:47.910 --> 00:25:49.680
and then you'd touch
someone [playful whirring]

00:25:49.680 --> 00:25:51.840
and you'd shock them with your finger.

00:25:51.840 --> 00:25:52.680
I'm a younger brother

00:25:52.680 --> 00:25:54.720
so I'd occasionally do that to my sister.

00:25:54.720 --> 00:25:56.400
I had friends, we would
do that to one another.

00:25:56.400 --> 00:25:58.620
I know it's kind of silly and childish,

00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:00.240
yet, it illustrates the principle

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:03.870
that we can generate
electricity and pass electricity

00:26:03.870 --> 00:26:06.540
to other beings or in the case of neurons,

00:26:06.540 --> 00:26:08.130
from one neuron to the next.

00:26:08.130 --> 00:26:09.570
The way neurons do that,

00:26:09.570 --> 00:26:11.580
is that in between the neurons,

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:12.450
they're little spaces,

00:26:12.450 --> 00:26:15.030
those little spaces are called synapses

00:26:15.030 --> 00:26:18.060
and neurons literally vomit,

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:19.380
well, they don't literally vomit,

00:26:19.380 --> 00:26:21.630
but they release little packets

00:26:21.630 --> 00:26:24.660
of so-called transmitter chemical

00:26:24.660 --> 00:26:26.670
into that space we call a synapse.

00:26:26.670 --> 00:26:28.710
It travels across the synapse.

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:31.290
It attaches to the cell on the other side,

00:26:31.290 --> 00:26:32.123
the other neuron.

00:26:32.123 --> 00:26:34.220
And then depending on
what that chemical is,

00:26:34.220 --> 00:26:37.860
it either makes that next
neuron more electrically active

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:39.150
or less electrically active,

00:26:39.150 --> 00:26:40.770
so called excitation,

00:26:40.770 --> 00:26:42.690
it either excites the next neuron

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:44.880
to be electrically active also,

00:26:44.880 --> 00:26:48.240
or it inhibits, it
prevents the next neuron

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:50.430
from being electrically active.

00:26:50.430 --> 00:26:51.390
So again, very simply,

00:26:51.390 --> 00:26:53.490
we have nerve cells that
communicate with one another

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:55.620
through electricity and chemicals

00:26:55.620 --> 00:26:57.570
that inspire that electricity

00:26:57.570 --> 00:27:01.590
and the little gaps between
neurons are called synapses.

00:27:01.590 --> 00:27:02.790
If you can understand that,

00:27:02.790 --> 00:27:04.770
I'm certain you can make it
through the rest of the episode

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:07.860
and that you'll get all the
depth and important detail

00:27:07.860 --> 00:27:09.510
that you need to know.

00:27:09.510 --> 00:27:11.580
But I want to go just a little bit further

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:15.210
and explain that neurons
don't just talk one-to-one.

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:17.940
There are trillions of
neurons in your nervous system

00:27:17.940 --> 00:27:20.310
that allow you to be happy, to be in love,

00:27:20.310 --> 00:27:22.140
to be sad, to be in grief,

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:24.930
to remember things and so on.

00:27:24.930 --> 00:27:27.780
And what you do at any moment,

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:30.180
what you feel and what you think

00:27:30.180 --> 00:27:34.890
relates to which so-called
neural circuits are active.

00:27:34.890 --> 00:27:36.780
So a lot of times we
think about brain areas

00:27:36.780 --> 00:27:38.280
and we've all seen these
pictures of the brain

00:27:38.280 --> 00:27:42.150
where, you know, someone
was in a fMRI scanner,

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:44.430
or they were in a brain
scanner of some sort,

00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:46.380
and they saw a picture of something

00:27:46.380 --> 00:27:49.620
and a certain area of the
brain lights up as it's called.

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:51.480
That lighting up of the
brain really reflects

00:27:51.480 --> 00:27:53.580
the activity of hundreds,
if not thousands,

00:27:53.580 --> 00:27:55.953
maybe even millions of
neurons in that region.

00:27:57.090 --> 00:27:59.430
Those images of brain areas lighting up

00:27:59.430 --> 00:28:01.740
and indeed talking about
brain areas lighting up,

00:28:01.740 --> 00:28:03.540
can be a little bit or a lot misleading

00:28:03.540 --> 00:28:05.850
because in fact, no single brain area

00:28:05.850 --> 00:28:08.580
controls any one single perception

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:10.560
or behavior or feeling state.

00:28:10.560 --> 00:28:13.020
Rather, we have so-called neuro circuits,

00:28:13.020 --> 00:28:14.760
chains of neurons,

00:28:14.760 --> 00:28:17.760
chains of specific neurons that is,

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:20.190
that create different states of mind

00:28:20.190 --> 00:28:22.380
that lead to specific behaviors

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:25.200
that lead to specific emotional states.

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:26.970
And those neural circuits are made up

00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:29.130
of lots of different
brain areas that light up

00:28:29.130 --> 00:28:30.360
in particular sequences.

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:32.760
And when I say light up, excuse me,

00:28:32.760 --> 00:28:37.050
what I mean is that particular
brain areas either excite

00:28:37.050 --> 00:28:38.790
or prevent the excitation,

00:28:38.790 --> 00:28:40.950
that is they inhibit other brain areas

00:28:40.950 --> 00:28:42.570
in a particular sequence.

00:28:42.570 --> 00:28:46.260
Much like keys on a piano
played in a particular sequence,

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:48.150
makes up a particular song,

00:28:48.150 --> 00:28:52.080
particular brain areas
activated or made silent

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:53.430
in a particular sequence

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:55.980
leads to a particular behavior

00:28:55.980 --> 00:28:57.660
like getting up out of a chair

00:28:57.660 --> 00:28:59.520
or a particular feeling state

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:02.220
like being particularly happy
one day when you wake up

00:29:02.220 --> 00:29:03.960
or particularly depressed,

00:29:03.960 --> 00:29:07.080
whether or not that depression
is caused by a life event

00:29:07.080 --> 00:29:09.600
or whether not it arises spontaneously.

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:11.550
So we have neurons, we have synapses

00:29:11.550 --> 00:29:14.160
and we have neural circuits.

00:29:14.160 --> 00:29:17.130
And vitally important is the fact that

00:29:17.130 --> 00:29:19.950
which neural circuits are active

00:29:19.950 --> 00:29:23.190
and which neural circuits
are likely to be less active

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:24.900
at any given moment,

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:28.740
depends on two major
categories of chemicals.

00:29:28.740 --> 00:29:30.720
It depends on hormones

00:29:30.720 --> 00:29:34.440
and it depends on
so-called neuromodulators.

00:29:34.440 --> 00:29:36.870
Now we're mainly going to
focus on neuromodulators today

00:29:36.870 --> 00:29:38.310
because those are the things

00:29:38.310 --> 00:29:40.200
that if you can learn to control them,

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:42.510
and indeed there are
tools to control them,

00:29:42.510 --> 00:29:45.960
then you can control which neural circuits

00:29:45.960 --> 00:29:48.330
are more likely or less
likely to be active

00:29:48.330 --> 00:29:50.100
in you at any given moment.

00:29:50.100 --> 00:29:51.877
And in doing so,

00:29:51.877 --> 00:29:53.481
you can control whether or not

00:29:53.481 --> 00:29:57.192
you are going to be alert
and focused or deeply asleep.

00:29:57.192 --> 00:29:58.603
You can control whether or not

00:29:58.603 --> 00:30:00.404
you are going to be in a creative state

00:30:00.404 --> 00:30:02.580
or whether or not you're
going to be in a state of mind

00:30:02.580 --> 00:30:05.430
more fit, more capable that is

00:30:05.430 --> 00:30:07.950
of doing focused work or math

00:30:07.950 --> 00:30:09.750
or more so-called linear types of work

00:30:09.750 --> 00:30:10.950
where there's a correct answer.

00:30:10.950 --> 00:30:13.140
There's a specific thing to follow

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:15.810
and you're simply going to
plug and chug as it were

00:30:15.810 --> 00:30:18.180
through a particular set of steps

00:30:18.180 --> 00:30:19.410
in order to accomplish something.

00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:20.640
Or for instance,

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:22.740
whether or not you're going
to be in a more relaxed

00:30:22.740 --> 00:30:25.740
and creative state where
you're thinking about new ideas

00:30:25.740 --> 00:30:29.820
or new ideas just seem to be
spontaneously coming to mind.

00:30:29.820 --> 00:30:32.730
All of that can be controlled

00:30:32.730 --> 00:30:34.860
to a considerable extent

00:30:34.860 --> 00:30:38.130
by leveraging these
so-called neuromodulators.

00:30:38.130 --> 00:30:39.870
What are neuromodulators?

00:30:39.870 --> 00:30:44.100
Neuromodulators are particular chemicals

00:30:44.100 --> 00:30:45.720
that make it likely

00:30:45.720 --> 00:30:48.780
that certain neuro circuits
will be active and not others.

00:30:48.780 --> 00:30:50.520
And the four neuromodulators

00:30:50.520 --> 00:30:51.840
that we're going to talk about today

00:30:51.840 --> 00:30:55.590
that are of the utmost
importance for your goals

00:30:55.590 --> 00:30:57.960
are dopamine,

00:30:57.960 --> 00:31:00.390
epinephrine also called adrenaline,

00:31:00.390 --> 00:31:03.360
serotonin and acetylcholine.

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:05.280
That's dopamine, epinephrine,

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:07.233
serotonin and acetylcholine.

00:31:08.070 --> 00:31:09.270
Today, I'm going to teach you

00:31:09.270 --> 00:31:11.160
how each of those different categories

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:13.410
of neuromodulators work

00:31:13.410 --> 00:31:15.300
and the things that you can do

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:18.240
to control those neuromodulators,

00:31:18.240 --> 00:31:21.390
that is increase them or decrease them

00:31:21.390 --> 00:31:24.630
through behavioral tools
and supplementation,

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:26.850
in ways that allow you to access

00:31:26.850 --> 00:31:29.040
the brain and body states that you want

00:31:29.040 --> 00:31:30.750
at the times that you want.

00:31:30.750 --> 00:31:31.710
Just very quickly.

00:31:31.710 --> 00:31:35.100
I want to talk about how
neuromodulators are able to work,

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:36.810
regardless of whether or not it's dopamine

00:31:36.810 --> 00:31:39.483
or serotonin or epinephrine, et cetera.

00:31:40.590 --> 00:31:43.380
There are many features of
how neuromodulators work,

00:31:43.380 --> 00:31:45.600
but for sake of today's discussion,

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:48.090
we only need to focus on
two of those features.

00:31:48.090 --> 00:31:50.520
And those are fast-acting features

00:31:50.520 --> 00:31:52.380
and longer slower features

00:31:52.380 --> 00:31:55.020
or what we call baseline features.

00:31:55.020 --> 00:31:58.740
What am I talking about when
I say faster or baseline?

00:31:58.740 --> 00:32:01.800
Well, consider that at any given moment,

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:03.420
whether or not you're asleep or awake,

00:32:03.420 --> 00:32:05.910
whether or not it's morning
or afternoon or night,

00:32:05.910 --> 00:32:08.460
you have some amount of dopamine

00:32:08.460 --> 00:32:10.500
being released in your brain and body,

00:32:10.500 --> 00:32:12.090
some amount of serotonin,

00:32:12.090 --> 00:32:13.680
some amount of epinephrine

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:16.590
and some amount of acetylcholine.

00:32:16.590 --> 00:32:19.290
It is rarely, if ever the case,

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:22.833
that you have zero
dopamine or zero serotonin.

00:32:22.833 --> 00:32:24.090
You know, so often we hear about someone

00:32:24.090 --> 00:32:25.470
being dopamine depleted

00:32:25.470 --> 00:32:27.720
or these days, you hear
a lot about that anyways.

00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:31.680
Or you'll hear that people's
serotonin is bottomed out.

00:32:31.680 --> 00:32:33.870
In reality, none of these neuromodulators

00:32:33.870 --> 00:32:36.210
ever disappear completely,

00:32:36.210 --> 00:32:39.090
but they tend to be
present at different levels

00:32:39.090 --> 00:32:40.950
or different relative levels.

00:32:40.950 --> 00:32:42.660
Another important thing to point out

00:32:42.660 --> 00:32:45.090
is that they don't work alone.

00:32:45.090 --> 00:32:46.710
In fact, as you'll soon learn,

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:50.040
dopamine and epinephrine are close cousins

00:32:50.040 --> 00:32:53.100
that collaborate in
terms of creating states

00:32:53.100 --> 00:32:55.410
of focus and motivation, for instance,

00:32:55.410 --> 00:32:58.080
or in creating states of energy

00:32:58.080 --> 00:32:59.913
and the pursuit of particular goals.

00:32:59.913 --> 00:33:01.380
When I say they're close cousins,

00:33:01.380 --> 00:33:04.830
what I mean is that they tend to impact

00:33:04.830 --> 00:33:07.170
some of the same neural circuits.

00:33:07.170 --> 00:33:08.310
And believe it or not,

00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:11.790
dopamine and epinephrine
are chemically related, too.

00:33:11.790 --> 00:33:12.690
I'll just tell you right now

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:16.200
that epinephrine is actually
derived from dopamine,

00:33:16.200 --> 00:33:19.890
chemically epinephrine,
that is adrenaline,

00:33:19.890 --> 00:33:22.740
is made from the molecule dopamine.

00:33:22.740 --> 00:33:26.280
Now dopamine and serotonin
can also work together

00:33:26.280 --> 00:33:28.290
to impact certain circuits in the brain.

00:33:28.290 --> 00:33:29.850
But in large part,

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:32.100
they operate on separate circuits.

00:33:32.100 --> 00:33:34.440
And acetylcholine,

00:33:34.440 --> 00:33:35.790
which you'll soon learn is involved

00:33:35.790 --> 00:33:38.210
in states of focus and
can actually open up,

00:33:38.210 --> 00:33:41.520
it can literally create states in the mind

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:43.740
in which your brain is more plastic

00:33:43.740 --> 00:33:46.350
and able to change and learn more quickly.

00:33:46.350 --> 00:33:48.750
Well, acetylcholine
can do that on its own,

00:33:48.750 --> 00:33:51.210
but rarely does it do it on its own.

00:33:51.210 --> 00:33:53.400
More typically, it gets assistance

00:33:53.400 --> 00:33:55.290
from some of the other neuromodulators.

00:33:55.290 --> 00:33:57.060
Now that might seem like
it complicates the picture,

00:33:57.060 --> 00:33:59.340
but it actually makes
the picture far simpler

00:33:59.340 --> 00:34:01.410
because what we can say for sure

00:34:01.410 --> 00:34:04.860
is that the fast actions of dopamine

00:34:04.860 --> 00:34:06.600
or the fast actions of epinephrine,

00:34:06.600 --> 00:34:08.460
serotonin or acetylcholine

00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:11.970
are actions that occur on the
order of seconds or minutes

00:34:11.970 --> 00:34:14.010
or up to about an hour or so.

00:34:14.010 --> 00:34:17.250
Whereas the slower actions
of those neuromodulators

00:34:17.250 --> 00:34:21.330
tend to occur on the order of
hours, days, or even weeks.

00:34:21.330 --> 00:34:22.650
Now perhaps surprisingly,

00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:24.510
I'd like to focus on the slow actions

00:34:24.510 --> 00:34:26.400
of the neuromodulators first,

00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:29.310
because those slow actions
of the neuromodulators

00:34:29.310 --> 00:34:33.240
are happening in you and in
me and in everyone right now,

00:34:33.240 --> 00:34:34.860
and they set the backdrop,

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:37.890
the context in which the various tools

00:34:37.890 --> 00:34:40.710
to manipulate dopamine,
epinephrine, serotonin,

00:34:40.710 --> 00:34:43.680
or acetylcholine will work.

00:34:43.680 --> 00:34:45.030
What do I mean by the context

00:34:45.030 --> 00:34:47.070
or the backdrop or the baseline?

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:50.550
Well, it's fair to say that most people

00:34:50.550 --> 00:34:52.710
are awake during the
daytime and asleep at night.

00:34:52.710 --> 00:34:54.330
I do realize that there are people

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:57.360
who are going to be doing shift work

00:34:57.360 --> 00:34:59.220
or they're raising young children,

00:34:59.220 --> 00:35:01.950
or that might have a sick person at home

00:35:01.950 --> 00:35:03.240
that they're tending to, et cetera,

00:35:03.240 --> 00:35:05.820
or even have insomnia,
they're tending to them.

00:35:05.820 --> 00:35:09.120
So schedules of sleep and
wakefulness will vary,

00:35:09.120 --> 00:35:11.130
but in general, everybody,

00:35:11.130 --> 00:35:13.290
regardless of whether
or not you're nocturnal

00:35:13.290 --> 00:35:16.530
or you're, so-called, diurnal,
your awake during the day,

00:35:16.530 --> 00:35:19.500
pretty much everybody
follows a schedule in which

00:35:19.500 --> 00:35:22.893
from zero to nine hours after waking,

00:35:23.730 --> 00:35:25.080
that is from the time you wake up

00:35:25.080 --> 00:35:28.290
until about nine hours later,

00:35:28.290 --> 00:35:30.120
the neuromodulators,

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:32.400
dopamine and epinephrine,

00:35:32.400 --> 00:35:35.280
tend to be at their highest
levels that they will be

00:35:35.280 --> 00:35:39.090
at any point in the 24-hour period,

00:35:39.090 --> 00:35:41.070
in any period of the day.

00:35:41.070 --> 00:35:43.980
So we can call this
zero to nine hour period

00:35:43.980 --> 00:35:46.620
phase one of the day, just for simplicity.

00:35:46.620 --> 00:35:49.800
And I've referred to this
before in a previous episode,

00:35:49.800 --> 00:35:52.233
but not in this exact context.

00:35:53.490 --> 00:35:57.060
From nine to about 16 hours is
what we would call phase two.

00:35:57.060 --> 00:36:00.330
And that's when dopamine
and epinephrine levels

00:36:00.330 --> 00:36:01.650
tend to subside a bit

00:36:01.650 --> 00:36:05.400
compared to the earlier
phase one part of the day

00:36:05.400 --> 00:36:08.850
and serotonin levels start to increase.

00:36:08.850 --> 00:36:11.940
And then phase three of the 24-hour cycle,

00:36:11.940 --> 00:36:13.200
which is from about,

00:36:13.200 --> 00:36:15.420
and again about the zero approximates,

00:36:15.420 --> 00:36:17.430
from about 17 hours after waking

00:36:17.430 --> 00:36:20.040
until about 24 hours after waking

00:36:20.040 --> 00:36:22.440
is phase three of the day.

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:24.510
And during that time,

00:36:24.510 --> 00:36:27.990
there is chaos in terms of which

00:36:27.990 --> 00:36:30.330
neuromodulators are most
present in the brain.

00:36:30.330 --> 00:36:33.570
And by chaos, what I mean
is that during sleep,

00:36:33.570 --> 00:36:35.880
you have incredible peaks in acetylcholine

00:36:35.880 --> 00:36:37.920
and drops in acetylcholine.

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:39.660
You have incredible peaks in dopamine

00:36:39.660 --> 00:36:41.550
and drops in dopamine.

00:36:41.550 --> 00:36:43.260
You have incredible peaks in serotonin

00:36:43.260 --> 00:36:45.150
and drops in serotonin.

00:36:45.150 --> 00:36:47.730
Most often, you are not going to see

00:36:47.730 --> 00:36:51.390
much if any release of
epinephrine, adrenaline,

00:36:51.390 --> 00:36:53.280
and that's because epinephrine,

00:36:53.280 --> 00:36:54.870
also called adrenaline,

00:36:54.870 --> 00:36:59.100
tends to wake us up and put
us into action mode behaviors

00:36:59.100 --> 00:37:01.200
and that's simply not
happening during sleep.

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:03.510
But for the other three neuromodulators,

00:37:03.510 --> 00:37:05.070
across the night,

00:37:05.070 --> 00:37:06.630
it's sort of chaos.

00:37:06.630 --> 00:37:08.610
You've got peaks and
drops and peaks and drops

00:37:08.610 --> 00:37:09.720
in different combinations

00:37:09.720 --> 00:37:11.400
than you would ever see in wakefulness.

00:37:11.400 --> 00:37:13.290
And this plays important
roles in dreaming,

00:37:13.290 --> 00:37:14.123
important roles

00:37:14.123 --> 00:37:16.830
in some of the reparative
functions of sleep.

00:37:16.830 --> 00:37:20.190
The point is that during that phase three,

00:37:20.190 --> 00:37:22.590
the levels of neuromodulators
are all over the place,

00:37:22.590 --> 00:37:24.810
but it's not random, right?

00:37:24.810 --> 00:37:26.940
I say it's chaos, but it's organized

00:37:26.940 --> 00:37:29.670
according to the specific
reparative goals of sleep,

00:37:29.670 --> 00:37:32.610
the specific metabolic
roles of sleep, et cetera.

00:37:32.610 --> 00:37:35.520
We're not going to focus too
much on phase three today

00:37:35.520 --> 00:37:38.670
because phase three of the 24-hour cycle,

00:37:38.670 --> 00:37:40.530
that 17 to 24-hour period,

00:37:40.530 --> 00:37:42.900
is one in which you ought
to be deeply asleep,

00:37:42.900 --> 00:37:44.880
whether or not you're
nocturnal or diurnal, right?

00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:47.940
17 hours after waking,
you ought to be asleep.

00:37:47.940 --> 00:37:50.430
And there are a lot of
episodes of this podcast.

00:37:50.430 --> 00:37:52.740
And indeed, today, I started talking about

00:37:52.740 --> 00:37:55.074
two particular studies related to sleep,

00:37:55.074 --> 00:37:57.810
and there are a lot of tools
to enhance sleep, et cetera.

00:37:57.810 --> 00:37:59.340
And of course there are
things that you can do

00:37:59.340 --> 00:38:03.030
in the late portion of
phase two of the day

00:38:03.030 --> 00:38:05.730
in order to enhance your transition time

00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:07.470
into and depth of sleep.

00:38:07.470 --> 00:38:10.110
But you can't really do
much during sleep, right?

00:38:10.110 --> 00:38:11.250
You're not taking supplements.

00:38:11.250 --> 00:38:13.260
You're not doing breathing practices.

00:38:13.260 --> 00:38:14.247
There are things to fall back asleep,

00:38:14.247 --> 00:38:15.960
but you're not really
doing much during sleep.

00:38:15.960 --> 00:38:17.580
So we're mainly going to focus

00:38:17.580 --> 00:38:20.610
on what we're calling
phase one and phase two.

00:38:20.610 --> 00:38:22.500
Phase one, being this dopamine

00:38:22.500 --> 00:38:24.720
epinephrine dominated phase of our day.

00:38:24.720 --> 00:38:27.750
And phase two being this more serotonergic

00:38:27.750 --> 00:38:30.030
or serotonin dominated portion of the day.

00:38:30.030 --> 00:38:30.870
And then you might say,

00:38:30.870 --> 00:38:33.030
well, what about acetylcholine?

00:38:33.030 --> 00:38:34.140
You forgot about acetylcholine?

00:38:34.140 --> 00:38:35.730
Well, we didn't forget
about acetylcholine.

00:38:35.730 --> 00:38:38.730
Acetylcholine is under control

00:38:38.730 --> 00:38:42.060
more in terms of what
we happen to be doing

00:38:42.060 --> 00:38:42.930
at any given moment,

00:38:42.930 --> 00:38:44.760
whether or not we're
focusing or not focusing,

00:38:44.760 --> 00:38:47.190
whether or not we're
learning or not learning.

00:38:47.190 --> 00:38:48.720
And here I'm referring to acetylcholine

00:38:48.720 --> 00:38:51.750
specifically in the context
of the brain and thinking,

00:38:51.750 --> 00:38:52.890
because as some of you

00:38:52.890 --> 00:38:54.900
are probably shouting out there, right,

00:38:54.900 --> 00:38:56.640
if you're an exercise physiologist

00:38:56.640 --> 00:39:00.270
or you know anything about how
the brain controls movement,

00:39:00.270 --> 00:39:03.900
acetylcholine is used at the
nerve to muscle synapse, right?

00:39:03.900 --> 00:39:07.410
So neurons don't just control
other neurons electrically.

00:39:07.410 --> 00:39:09.030
The way you are able to move in fact

00:39:09.030 --> 00:39:10.260
is because neurons are controlling

00:39:10.260 --> 00:39:13.470
the electric activity of muscles,

00:39:13.470 --> 00:39:15.270
literally the contraction
of muscle fibers,

00:39:15.270 --> 00:39:18.360
and that control is exerted

00:39:18.360 --> 00:39:19.710
through the release of acetylcholine.

00:39:19.710 --> 00:39:21.720
So acetylcholine is working
at muscles, as well,

00:39:21.720 --> 00:39:23.520
but we're not focused on that today.

00:39:23.520 --> 00:39:26.520
We're focused on what we can do

00:39:26.520 --> 00:39:27.810
during phase one of the day

00:39:27.810 --> 00:39:30.090
and what we can do during
phase two of the day

00:39:30.090 --> 00:39:33.240
to control the specific neuromodulators,

00:39:33.240 --> 00:39:36.060
dopamine, epinephrine,
serotonin, and acetylcholine,

00:39:36.060 --> 00:39:37.950
toward particular end goals.

00:39:37.950 --> 00:39:40.920
And as I've been harping
on for the last five

00:39:40.920 --> 00:39:42.720
or 10 minutes or so,

00:39:42.720 --> 00:39:44.190
it is important to understand

00:39:44.190 --> 00:39:46.440
that in the early phase
one part of the day,

00:39:46.440 --> 00:39:48.450
again, zero to nine hours,

00:39:48.450 --> 00:39:51.420
dopamine and epinephrine already

00:39:51.420 --> 00:39:54.780
dominate the neuromodulator landscape.

00:39:54.780 --> 00:39:56.760
That is they are already elevated

00:39:56.760 --> 00:39:58.710
and then they will taper off in phase two.

00:39:58.710 --> 00:40:00.390
Whereas in phase two of the day,

00:40:00.390 --> 00:40:02.460
serotonin tends to dominate

00:40:02.460 --> 00:40:05.610
more than dopamine and epinephrine.

00:40:05.610 --> 00:40:08.040
And so if you think about that,

00:40:08.040 --> 00:40:09.720
what it means is that if your goal

00:40:09.720 --> 00:40:11.670
is to increase serotonin

00:40:11.670 --> 00:40:14.490
in order to get some particular effect

00:40:14.490 --> 00:40:16.560
on your mental performance

00:40:16.560 --> 00:40:19.110
or physical performance or health,

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:22.920
or if your goal is to increase
your dopamine or epinephrine,

00:40:22.920 --> 00:40:26.070
to get some particular
effect on your mental health,

00:40:26.070 --> 00:40:28.590
physical performance, et cetera,

00:40:28.590 --> 00:40:30.270
well, then you need to consider

00:40:30.270 --> 00:40:33.180
what the background level of dopamine

00:40:33.180 --> 00:40:36.000
or epinephrine or serotonin happens to be

00:40:36.000 --> 00:40:37.350
because in doing so,

00:40:37.350 --> 00:40:39.900
you will know which tool to select

00:40:39.900 --> 00:40:44.010
and how hard you need to
push on that tool, right?

00:40:44.010 --> 00:40:47.190
If your levels of dopamine are
already riding pretty high,

00:40:47.190 --> 00:40:49.080
because it's the early part of the day,

00:40:49.080 --> 00:40:51.990
well then it doesn't take a whole lot more

00:40:51.990 --> 00:40:54.870
to get dopamine to a
level in which it can,

00:40:54.870 --> 00:40:57.120
for instance, change
your level of motivation.

00:40:57.120 --> 00:40:59.370
Whereas if you are in
the late part of the day,

00:40:59.370 --> 00:41:01.500
let's say eight or 9:00 PM,

00:41:01.500 --> 00:41:03.660
and you have a lot of serotonin

00:41:03.660 --> 00:41:05.640
swimming around in your system

00:41:05.640 --> 00:41:08.340
and you really need to
be focused and alert,

00:41:08.340 --> 00:41:11.820
well, you can do that by
leveraging the dopamine

00:41:11.820 --> 00:41:12.810
and epinephrine system

00:41:12.810 --> 00:41:15.060
and indeed the acetylcholine system too,

00:41:15.060 --> 00:41:17.850
but you're going to
have to resort to tools

00:41:17.850 --> 00:41:20.250
that can do that far more potently

00:41:20.250 --> 00:41:23.550
and that can do that in
a much more sustained way

00:41:23.550 --> 00:41:26.100
if you're going to access
the state that you want.

00:41:26.100 --> 00:41:29.400
So again, it's really
important to understand

00:41:29.400 --> 00:41:32.850
what the backdrop of
these neuromodulators is,

00:41:32.850 --> 00:41:34.320
the so-called baseline

00:41:34.320 --> 00:41:36.480
and that they vary across the day

00:41:36.480 --> 00:41:38.790
if you are going to be
able to leverage tools

00:41:38.790 --> 00:41:40.620
to optimize your brain chemistry.

00:41:40.620 --> 00:41:42.930
Anyone that tells you do this protocol

00:41:42.930 --> 00:41:44.520
in order to increase your dopamine,

00:41:44.520 --> 00:41:45.870
do this protocol,

00:41:45.870 --> 00:41:48.600
or take this supplement to
increase your serotonin,

00:41:48.600 --> 00:41:50.970
they can be telling
you the absolute truth,

00:41:50.970 --> 00:41:53.190
but if you don't consider the backdrop

00:41:53.190 --> 00:41:54.960
over which that supplement

00:41:54.960 --> 00:41:57.270
or behavior is going to have its effect,

00:41:57.270 --> 00:41:59.850
well, then you can't really
predict the effect it will have.

00:41:59.850 --> 00:42:03.600
But if you can understand these
backdrop baseline elements

00:42:03.600 --> 00:42:05.280
to how neuromodulators work,

00:42:05.280 --> 00:42:06.930
well, then you're in a terrific position

00:42:06.930 --> 00:42:08.820
to leverage the best tools

00:42:08.820 --> 00:42:09.897
in the immediate and short-term

00:42:09.897 --> 00:42:12.990
and that is on the order of
seconds, minutes and hours.

00:42:12.990 --> 00:42:15.180
Before we dive into the more pointed,

00:42:15.180 --> 00:42:19.110
directed effects of specific
tools on neuromodulators,

00:42:19.110 --> 00:42:21.210
I'd like to just briefly mention hormones

00:42:21.210 --> 00:42:22.650
because they are also important

00:42:22.650 --> 00:42:26.490
for understanding the
background and the context

00:42:26.490 --> 00:42:29.684
and these baseline levels
of neuromodulators.

00:42:29.684 --> 00:42:32.490
Now here, I'm going to paint
with a bit of a broad brush,

00:42:32.490 --> 00:42:34.080
but what I will say is accurate,

00:42:34.080 --> 00:42:36.120
even though it might not be exhaustive.

00:42:36.120 --> 00:42:38.970
What I mean by that is everything
I'm about to say is true,

00:42:38.970 --> 00:42:41.340
but it doesn't cover
every example in detail

00:42:41.340 --> 00:42:43.830
and nuanced possibility out there.

00:42:43.830 --> 00:42:48.030
Hormones have many different
effects on the brain and body

00:42:48.030 --> 00:42:49.590
and not unlike neuromodulators,

00:42:49.590 --> 00:42:51.660
some of those effects are very fast.

00:42:51.660 --> 00:42:53.100
Some of them are very slow.

00:42:53.100 --> 00:42:55.560
In fact, certain hormones,

00:42:55.560 --> 00:42:57.780
for instance, the steroid hormones,

00:42:57.780 --> 00:43:01.350
like estrogen and like testosterone

00:43:01.350 --> 00:43:02.920
and corticosteroids

00:43:03.960 --> 00:43:04.793
and here, of course,

00:43:04.793 --> 00:43:07.170
I'm referring to the steroid
hormones for what they are.

00:43:07.170 --> 00:43:08.940
They are indeed steroid hormones,

00:43:08.940 --> 00:43:11.100
but I'm not talking about steroids

00:43:11.100 --> 00:43:13.140
that people inject for sports performance

00:43:13.140 --> 00:43:14.310
or for physical augmentation.

00:43:14.310 --> 00:43:16.050
I'm talking about the steroid hormones

00:43:16.050 --> 00:43:17.070
that you make naturally

00:43:17.070 --> 00:43:19.440
because indeed you make these naturally.

00:43:19.440 --> 00:43:21.420
Well, the steroid hormones

00:43:21.420 --> 00:43:23.580
can actually control gene expression.

00:43:23.580 --> 00:43:25.380
They can change the identity of cells

00:43:25.380 --> 00:43:28.290
and the genes and proteins
that cells express.

00:43:28.290 --> 00:43:30.540
This is why during puberty, for instance,

00:43:30.540 --> 00:43:33.150
testosterone and estrogen
are released into the body,

00:43:33.150 --> 00:43:34.950
growth hormone is released into the body

00:43:34.950 --> 00:43:38.010
and bodies and voices and personalities

00:43:38.010 --> 00:43:40.080
and brains change tremendously

00:43:40.080 --> 00:43:42.570
because literally there
is a transformation

00:43:42.570 --> 00:43:44.820
of the breast tissue,

00:43:44.820 --> 00:43:46.320
of the testicular tissue,

00:43:46.320 --> 00:43:49.920
of the ovarian tissue, of
the bones, of the muscles,

00:43:49.920 --> 00:43:54.540
of the tissues and cells
that control hair growth.

00:43:54.540 --> 00:43:57.750
Gene expression changes
in all those cell types.

00:43:57.750 --> 00:44:00.270
And the child becomes an adolescent,

00:44:00.270 --> 00:44:02.160
becomes a young adult, right?

00:44:02.160 --> 00:44:03.780
That's what puberty really is.

00:44:03.780 --> 00:44:05.310
In fact, puberty is perhaps

00:44:05.310 --> 00:44:08.340
the most dramatic transformation
that we go through

00:44:08.340 --> 00:44:11.970
in our entire lifespan
in terms of our aging,

00:44:11.970 --> 00:44:15.210
'cause indeed it reflects a very rapid,

00:44:15.210 --> 00:44:17.010
I should mention, period of aging

00:44:17.010 --> 00:44:19.830
and transformation of
the identity of cells.

00:44:19.830 --> 00:44:21.480
So steroid hormones and other hormones

00:44:21.480 --> 00:44:26.130
can have very slow long-lasting
actions in that way.

00:44:26.130 --> 00:44:28.290
They can also have very fast actions.

00:44:28.290 --> 00:44:29.123
So for instance,

00:44:29.123 --> 00:44:32.520
adrenaline, epinephrine,
released from the adrenal glands

00:44:32.520 --> 00:44:34.710
can immediately make
your heart beat faster,

00:44:34.710 --> 00:44:37.650
can immediately change the circumference

00:44:37.650 --> 00:44:41.040
of your blood vessels and
arteries and capillaries

00:44:41.040 --> 00:44:42.570
and change the way blood flows.

00:44:42.570 --> 00:44:45.000
It can change the way you
see the world, literally.

00:44:45.000 --> 00:44:46.650
It does change the way you see the world

00:44:46.650 --> 00:44:47.727
through your visual system.

00:44:47.727 --> 00:44:49.440
And that all happens on the order

00:44:49.440 --> 00:44:52.170
of hundreds of milliseconds or seconds,

00:44:52.170 --> 00:44:54.480
these are extremely fast actions.

00:44:54.480 --> 00:44:58.470
Corticosteroid also can have
fast actions and slow actions.

00:44:58.470 --> 00:45:03.000
But since this isn't a
discussion about hormones per se,

00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:04.260
and we've done entire episodes

00:45:04.260 --> 00:45:07.020
like the Optimize Testosterone
and Estrogen episode.

00:45:07.020 --> 00:45:08.730
You can find that at HubermanLab.com.

00:45:08.730 --> 00:45:12.060
Or the interview with the
incredibly knowledgeable

00:45:12.060 --> 00:45:13.720
and clear and

00:45:14.760 --> 00:45:18.600
really wonderful tutor of
actionable information,

00:45:18.600 --> 00:45:20.490
Dr. Kyle Gillette, who
is also on this podcast,

00:45:20.490 --> 00:45:22.320
you can learn a lot about hormones there.

00:45:22.320 --> 00:45:23.670
Today, we want to think about hormones

00:45:23.670 --> 00:45:25.737
as they relate to these neuromodulators,

00:45:25.737 --> 00:45:28.950
the dopamine, serotonin,
epinephrine and acetylcholine,

00:45:28.950 --> 00:45:30.423
and in general,

00:45:31.440 --> 00:45:34.470
testosterone tends to collaborate with

00:45:34.470 --> 00:45:37.230
and increase the action of dopamine.

00:45:37.230 --> 00:45:39.600
That's not always the
case, but in general,

00:45:39.600 --> 00:45:42.750
when testosterone goes
up, dopamine goes up

00:45:42.750 --> 00:45:44.490
and sometimes even vice versa.

00:45:44.490 --> 00:45:46.560
When dopamine goes up, testosterone go up.

00:45:46.560 --> 00:45:50.790
And this is true for both
males and for females.

00:45:50.790 --> 00:45:53.820
In general, when corticosteroids

00:45:53.820 --> 00:45:58.470
like cortisol and some related
steroid hormones increase,

00:45:58.470 --> 00:46:00.420
epinephrine levels go up.

00:46:00.420 --> 00:46:03.540
And in general, when
hormones like oxytocin

00:46:03.540 --> 00:46:06.570
or prolactin are increased,

00:46:06.570 --> 00:46:08.730
levels of serotonin go up.

00:46:08.730 --> 00:46:10.440
We can't draw a direct link

00:46:10.440 --> 00:46:13.080
between any one hormone
system and acetylcholine.

00:46:13.080 --> 00:46:14.340
Acetylcholine kind of sits off

00:46:14.340 --> 00:46:16.500
in a category of its own in that way.

00:46:16.500 --> 00:46:18.930
But again, in general,
testosterone and dopamine

00:46:18.930 --> 00:46:21.630
tend to collaborate in the same direction.

00:46:21.630 --> 00:46:24.660
Cortisol and epinephrine
tend to collaborate

00:46:24.660 --> 00:46:25.773
in the same direction.

00:46:27.180 --> 00:46:30.300
Oxytocin and prolactin,
which are hormones,

00:46:30.300 --> 00:46:33.750
and serotonin tend to collaborate
in the same direction.

00:46:33.750 --> 00:46:37.050
And then we have poor
old, lonely acetylcholine

00:46:37.050 --> 00:46:38.760
off on its own, but it's
not poor and lonely.

00:46:38.760 --> 00:46:40.800
It actually has incredibly
potent effects on its own.

00:46:40.800 --> 00:46:42.960
So it's really that it
just doesn't mean much help

00:46:42.960 --> 00:46:45.060
from the hormone systems

00:46:45.060 --> 00:46:46.770
or at least not the
steroid hormone systems

00:46:46.770 --> 00:46:49.200
in order to have its tremendous effects.

00:46:49.200 --> 00:46:50.490
Now a lot of what people think about

00:46:50.490 --> 00:46:53.370
and will do when trying
to improve mental health

00:46:53.370 --> 00:46:54.420
and physical health

00:46:54.420 --> 00:46:56.400
is they will try and increase

00:46:56.400 --> 00:46:58.850
or decrease certain categories of hormones

00:46:58.850 --> 00:46:59.880
of the sort that I mentioned,

00:46:59.880 --> 00:47:02.943
testosterone, estrogen,
oxytocin, and prolactin, so on.

00:47:03.780 --> 00:47:08.490
But oftentimes, the effects
of those manipulations

00:47:08.490 --> 00:47:11.100
in hormones that are
going to be most salient

00:47:11.100 --> 00:47:13.050
are not going to be due
to the direct effects

00:47:13.050 --> 00:47:14.100
of those hormones.

00:47:14.100 --> 00:47:15.480
Sometimes it could be.

00:47:15.480 --> 00:47:18.180
But oftentimes it's going
to be due to their effects

00:47:18.180 --> 00:47:19.920
on the brain and nervous system

00:47:19.920 --> 00:47:24.090
by way of how those hormones
impact neuromodulators.

00:47:24.090 --> 00:47:25.860
So for instance,

00:47:25.860 --> 00:47:27.420
there are various things
that people can do,

00:47:27.420 --> 00:47:28.290
both men and women,

00:47:28.290 --> 00:47:30.750
to increase their
testosterone and estrogen

00:47:30.750 --> 00:47:32.550
in the appropriate ratios.

00:47:32.550 --> 00:47:36.510
I talked about one such
approach in a previous episode,

00:47:36.510 --> 00:47:38.880
and that is to get sunlight

00:47:38.880 --> 00:47:43.740
onto a large portion
of one's skin each day.

00:47:43.740 --> 00:47:45.840
Believe it or not, this actually works.

00:47:45.840 --> 00:47:48.150
And it works because your skin

00:47:48.150 --> 00:47:49.590
is actually an endocrine organ,

00:47:49.590 --> 00:47:51.930
a hormone secreting organ.

00:47:51.930 --> 00:47:52.770
It's a beautiful study.

00:47:52.770 --> 00:47:54.360
I've covered it on this podcast before.

00:47:54.360 --> 00:47:56.100
We will provide a link
to this study again,

00:47:56.100 --> 00:48:01.080
but it had people spend
at least 20 minutes or so,

00:48:01.080 --> 00:48:02.610
closer to 30 minutes, each day,

00:48:02.610 --> 00:48:04.530
trying to maximize sunlight exposure

00:48:04.530 --> 00:48:06.180
to as much of their skin as they could

00:48:06.180 --> 00:48:09.450
in terms of still
maintaining decent exposure,

00:48:09.450 --> 00:48:11.970
meaning not over exposing
themselves in a cultural way,

00:48:11.970 --> 00:48:14.070
meaning wearing enough
clothes that they were decent,

00:48:14.070 --> 00:48:16.590
but still getting a lot of sun exposure,

00:48:16.590 --> 00:48:18.393
a couple of times per week or more.

00:48:19.320 --> 00:48:21.270
What they found was that
people's testosterone

00:48:21.270 --> 00:48:22.710
and estrogen levels went up.

00:48:22.710 --> 00:48:24.180
Feelings of well-being went up.

00:48:24.180 --> 00:48:27.690
Feelings of well, or I should
say increases in libido

00:48:27.690 --> 00:48:29.460
were observed, as well.

00:48:29.460 --> 00:48:32.340
They subjectively reported
more passion, et cetera,

00:48:32.340 --> 00:48:35.430
testosterone and estrogen
did indeed both go up.

00:48:35.430 --> 00:48:38.460
And again, I want to highlight
that increases in estrogen,

00:48:38.460 --> 00:48:39.510
not just testosterone,

00:48:39.510 --> 00:48:43.230
are related to increases in
libido in both men and women.

00:48:43.230 --> 00:48:44.070
This is why you never want

00:48:44.070 --> 00:48:45.750
to crush your estrogen down to zero,

00:48:45.750 --> 00:48:47.430
whether or not you're male or female,

00:48:47.430 --> 00:48:50.910
if you want to maintain
some sort of healthy libido

00:48:50.910 --> 00:48:54.000
and general feelings of
well-being, unrelated to libido.

00:48:54.000 --> 00:48:56.790
Well, many of those effects

00:48:56.790 --> 00:48:58.890
we know are not due to direct effects

00:48:58.890 --> 00:49:00.660
of testosterone and estrogen,

00:49:00.660 --> 00:49:02.340
but rather are due to the effects

00:49:02.340 --> 00:49:05.010
of testosterone and estrogen
on the neuromodulators,

00:49:05.010 --> 00:49:07.170
dopamine and serotonin,

00:49:07.170 --> 00:49:10.500
because much of libido
and feelings of well-being

00:49:10.500 --> 00:49:12.780
and feelings of relaxation,

00:49:12.780 --> 00:49:15.450
but also desire, motivation, et cetera,

00:49:15.450 --> 00:49:19.560
originate because of the
activation of neural circuits

00:49:19.560 --> 00:49:22.230
that dopamine controls and promotes

00:49:22.230 --> 00:49:25.590
and that serotonin promote and control.

00:49:25.590 --> 00:49:28.590
So this is very important to understand

00:49:28.590 --> 00:49:31.080
as we move toward more specific discussion

00:49:31.080 --> 00:49:34.410
of the chemicals that
we call neuromodulators,

00:49:34.410 --> 00:49:38.310
because hormones are controlling
those neuromodulators

00:49:38.310 --> 00:49:41.070
in a very slow modulatory way.

00:49:41.070 --> 00:49:43.020
So yes, I said it,

00:49:43.020 --> 00:49:45.570
hormones modulate neuromodulators.

00:49:45.570 --> 00:49:48.093
I sort of said it twice on purpose.

00:49:49.140 --> 00:49:52.230
And this is a dramatic and potent effect.

00:49:52.230 --> 00:49:53.880
So I'll just give you one more example.

00:49:53.880 --> 00:49:57.120
The hormone prolactin
tends to be antagonistic.

00:49:57.120 --> 00:49:59.310
It tends to reduce amounts of dopamine

00:49:59.310 --> 00:50:02.160
or at least when
prolactin levels are high,

00:50:02.160 --> 00:50:04.560
dopamine levels tend to be lower.

00:50:04.560 --> 00:50:07.170
You observe this after
the birth of a new child,

00:50:07.170 --> 00:50:09.930
you observe this post
coitally after mating,

00:50:09.930 --> 00:50:12.603
in all species, humans and animals.

00:50:13.560 --> 00:50:15.930
When prolactin is elevated,

00:50:15.930 --> 00:50:17.790
serotonin tends to be elevated.

00:50:17.790 --> 00:50:19.680
And when prolactin is elevated,

00:50:19.680 --> 00:50:23.760
levels of dopamine and
the effects of dopamine

00:50:23.760 --> 00:50:26.100
tend to subside.

00:50:26.100 --> 00:50:27.990
Now as I move toward explaining

00:50:27.990 --> 00:50:31.170
what each of the four categories
of neuromodulators do,

00:50:31.170 --> 00:50:32.700
this will start to make
more and more sense

00:50:32.700 --> 00:50:34.380
as to why this would be.

00:50:34.380 --> 00:50:37.290
I always say I wasn't
consulted in the design phase,

00:50:37.290 --> 00:50:39.540
meaning I didn't design these circuits.

00:50:39.540 --> 00:50:40.920
And if anyone tells you that they did,

00:50:40.920 --> 00:50:42.330
you should back away quickly

00:50:42.330 --> 00:50:44.220
because none of us design these circuits.

00:50:44.220 --> 00:50:45.600
This is the way that evolution

00:50:45.600 --> 00:50:48.840
and nature created these systems.

00:50:48.840 --> 00:50:51.150
And they tend to work in
a bit of a seesaw fashion,

00:50:51.150 --> 00:50:54.030
prolactin up, dopamine down, right?

00:50:54.030 --> 00:50:55.680
Dopamine up, prolactin down.

00:50:55.680 --> 00:50:58.230
In general, that is the way they work.

00:50:58.230 --> 00:51:01.320
So if we are to take a
look at how each of these

00:51:01.320 --> 00:51:05.100
neuromodulator systems
functions on its own,

00:51:05.100 --> 00:51:06.240
while understanding that

00:51:06.240 --> 00:51:08.370
they never truly function on their own,

00:51:08.370 --> 00:51:10.380
we can start to really make sense

00:51:10.380 --> 00:51:12.660
of the landscape of tools
that are available to us

00:51:12.660 --> 00:51:15.750
and which tools are going to
be most powerful to select

00:51:15.750 --> 00:51:18.360
if our goal is for instance, to be focused

00:51:18.360 --> 00:51:21.030
or if our goal is to be less stressed

00:51:21.030 --> 00:51:24.780
or if our goal is to be highly motivated

00:51:24.780 --> 00:51:26.700
and highly focused for sake of learning.

00:51:26.700 --> 00:51:28.710
All of that is indeed possible

00:51:28.710 --> 00:51:31.740
if you understand these
four neuromodulators

00:51:31.740 --> 00:51:34.350
and you understand that
while there are many tools

00:51:34.350 --> 00:51:36.270
ranging from pharmacologic to behavioral

00:51:36.270 --> 00:51:38.220
that can tap into these
neuromodulator systems,

00:51:38.220 --> 00:51:40.650
that can kind of press
on the gas of dopamine,

00:51:40.650 --> 00:51:43.053
pull back on serotonin and so on,

00:51:44.370 --> 00:51:46.740
but that there are particular tools,

00:51:46.740 --> 00:51:48.227
both behavioral and supplementation-based,

00:51:48.227 --> 00:51:50.310
and to some extent,

00:51:50.310 --> 00:51:51.510
prescription drug based, too,

00:51:51.510 --> 00:51:53.520
and we'll touch on a few of those.

00:51:53.520 --> 00:51:56.070
If you understand that and why they work,

00:51:56.070 --> 00:51:59.880
well, then you can create a sort of kit,

00:51:59.880 --> 00:52:03.030
a grab bag of things that
you can use in any context

00:52:03.030 --> 00:52:05.580
or I should say that you can look to

00:52:05.580 --> 00:52:07.500
depending on the context you're in

00:52:07.500 --> 00:52:10.380
and create the states of
body and mind that you want.

00:52:10.380 --> 00:52:13.410
Now once again, painting
with a somewhat broad brush,

00:52:13.410 --> 00:52:15.810
but nonetheless, an accurate brush,

00:52:15.810 --> 00:52:18.360
we can say that dopamine,

00:52:18.360 --> 00:52:21.960
when elevated above baseline,

00:52:21.960 --> 00:52:25.110
tends to increase states of motivation,

00:52:25.110 --> 00:52:28.140
both mental and physical motivation,

00:52:28.140 --> 00:52:30.843
drive and to some extent focus.

00:52:31.980 --> 00:52:34.200
I've said it many times
before, and I'll say it again,

00:52:34.200 --> 00:52:36.900
there's a lot of
misconception about dopamine.

00:52:36.900 --> 00:52:39.030
Many people out there think that dopamine

00:52:39.030 --> 00:52:40.320
is all about pleasure.

00:52:40.320 --> 00:52:43.470
You hear about dopamine hits
or people chasing dopamine

00:52:43.470 --> 00:52:46.560
or the need to have a
dopamine fast, et cetera.

00:52:46.560 --> 00:52:47.910
Dopamine is not about pleasure.

00:52:47.910 --> 00:52:52.260
Dopamine is about motivation,
craving and pursuit

00:52:52.260 --> 00:52:55.740
for goals or for things that are outside

00:52:55.740 --> 00:52:58.380
our immediate possession and experience.

00:52:58.380 --> 00:53:00.930
The motivation and pursuit of a mate.

00:53:00.930 --> 00:53:03.330
The motivation and pursuit to mate.

00:53:03.330 --> 00:53:05.190
The motivation and pursuit of food.

00:53:05.190 --> 00:53:07.560
The motivation and
pursuit of a career goal,

00:53:07.560 --> 00:53:08.940
et cetera, et cetera,

00:53:08.940 --> 00:53:12.810
things we do not yet
have, but that we want

00:53:12.810 --> 00:53:16.020
and we get into sort of
a forward center of mass

00:53:16.020 --> 00:53:18.120
and a pursuit of, and that
pursuit can be physical.

00:53:18.120 --> 00:53:19.590
That pursuit can be cognitive.

00:53:19.590 --> 00:53:22.500
It can be both cognitive and physical.

00:53:22.500 --> 00:53:25.260
And it can involve talking
about something, right?

00:53:25.260 --> 00:53:26.340
Because in some professions,

00:53:26.340 --> 00:53:27.840
pursuit of things involves talking.

00:53:27.840 --> 00:53:28.980
I think about lawyers,

00:53:28.980 --> 00:53:32.130
they talk a lot in
pursuit of winning cases

00:53:32.130 --> 00:53:33.300
and money, et cetera,

00:53:33.300 --> 00:53:35.310
putting people in jail or
keeping people out of jail,

00:53:35.310 --> 00:53:36.810
et cetera, that's done with their mouths,

00:53:36.810 --> 00:53:38.220
not with their bodies.

00:53:38.220 --> 00:53:40.980
Athletes in a state of motivated training

00:53:40.980 --> 00:53:44.010
or in motivated competition
use their bodies.

00:53:44.010 --> 00:53:45.450
All this is obvious of course,

00:53:45.450 --> 00:53:47.610
but perhaps what is not so obvious

00:53:47.610 --> 00:53:50.910
is that one molecule not working alone,

00:53:50.910 --> 00:53:53.490
but predominantly one molecule, dopamine,

00:53:53.490 --> 00:53:55.980
is responsible for all of
those motivated states,

00:53:55.980 --> 00:53:59.790
which again, underscores the
power of these neuromodulators.

00:53:59.790 --> 00:54:01.140
So dopamine, we can think of

00:54:01.140 --> 00:54:03.420
at least in the context
of today's discussion

00:54:03.420 --> 00:54:07.020
as controlling and indeed
promoting motivation,

00:54:07.020 --> 00:54:09.120
drive and pursuit

00:54:09.120 --> 00:54:10.983
and to some extent, focus.

00:54:12.180 --> 00:54:15.060
Epinephrine and a closely related molecule

00:54:15.060 --> 00:54:16.660
called norepinephrine

00:54:17.520 --> 00:54:18.870
and again, I want to emphasize

00:54:18.870 --> 00:54:20.850
that epinephrine is adrenaline

00:54:20.850 --> 00:54:22.950
and adrenaline is epinephrine.

00:54:22.950 --> 00:54:25.140
Norepinephrine is noradrenaline

00:54:25.140 --> 00:54:27.120
and noradrenaline is norepinephrine,

00:54:27.120 --> 00:54:28.980
but today we're going to
just simply talk about

00:54:28.980 --> 00:54:30.693
epinephrine and norepinephrine.

00:54:31.890 --> 00:54:33.990
That category of neuromodulator

00:54:33.990 --> 00:54:38.670
is mainly responsible for
generating our energy,

00:54:38.670 --> 00:54:41.100
our level of fuel

00:54:41.100 --> 00:54:43.680
and baseline level of

00:54:43.680 --> 00:54:46.350
forward center of mass,
as I like to call it.

00:54:46.350 --> 00:54:49.440
You can also think of it
as how high your RPM are.

00:54:49.440 --> 00:54:50.910
Now we're not a car

00:54:50.910 --> 00:54:53.190
and the car analogy sort of falls apart

00:54:53.190 --> 00:54:55.500
as we go further into the biology,

00:54:55.500 --> 00:54:57.420
but it's a decent one for now.

00:54:57.420 --> 00:54:58.890
When epinephrine levels are high,

00:54:58.890 --> 00:55:00.390
we tend to feel agitated,

00:55:00.390 --> 00:55:01.860
we tend to feel like we want to move,

00:55:01.860 --> 00:55:04.590
we tend to feel like we
can't shut down our thinking

00:55:04.590 --> 00:55:07.080
and our anticipation of
what's going to happen next.

00:55:07.080 --> 00:55:09.660
And when epinephrine
levels are very, very low,

00:55:09.660 --> 00:55:11.670
we actually have less physical energy.

00:55:11.670 --> 00:55:13.980
We tend to have less mental energy

00:55:13.980 --> 00:55:16.830
in terms of generating
thoughts very quickly

00:55:16.830 --> 00:55:17.880
and so on and so forth.

00:55:17.880 --> 00:55:19.440
And as I mentioned before,

00:55:19.440 --> 00:55:22.140
dopamine and epinephrine
are closely related

00:55:22.140 --> 00:55:25.230
so much so that we know for a fact

00:55:25.230 --> 00:55:27.720
that epinephrine is actually manufactured

00:55:27.720 --> 00:55:29.340
from the molecule dopamine.

00:55:29.340 --> 00:55:31.230
So that's why I'm talking
about these two neuromodulators

00:55:31.230 --> 00:55:32.880
in very close juxtaposition

00:55:32.880 --> 00:55:35.790
because they do indeed
collaborate with one another.

00:55:35.790 --> 00:55:38.040
But for sake of today's discussion,

00:55:38.040 --> 00:55:41.220
we can just think of epinephrine
as increasing energy,

00:55:41.220 --> 00:55:44.370
adrenaline increases energy
in our state of readiness.

00:55:44.370 --> 00:55:48.150
It also, I should mention,
activates our immune system.

00:55:48.150 --> 00:55:49.830
Contrary to popular belief

00:55:49.830 --> 00:55:52.050
that stress inhibits our immune system,

00:55:52.050 --> 00:55:53.520
epinephrine is deployed,

00:55:53.520 --> 00:55:56.250
it's released at great
levels in our brain and body

00:55:56.250 --> 00:55:57.180
when we are stressed.

00:55:57.180 --> 00:55:58.980
And that actually protects us

00:55:58.980 --> 00:56:02.100
against infections of multiple kinds,

00:56:02.100 --> 00:56:03.450
at least in the short-term.

00:56:04.500 --> 00:56:06.390
That and all the details of that

00:56:06.390 --> 00:56:08.280
and tools related to that

00:56:08.280 --> 00:56:10.560
were covered in our episode
on the immune system,

00:56:10.560 --> 00:56:12.270
if you want to check that out.

00:56:12.270 --> 00:56:15.000
Now the neuromodulator serotonin

00:56:15.000 --> 00:56:17.850
creates a number of different
states in the brain and body,

00:56:17.850 --> 00:56:19.980
but for sake of today's discussion,

00:56:19.980 --> 00:56:22.470
we're going to think about
the predominant states

00:56:22.470 --> 00:56:23.303
that it creates

00:56:23.303 --> 00:56:26.400
and those are states of contentness,

00:56:26.400 --> 00:56:29.070
being happy, feeling fairly relaxed,

00:56:29.070 --> 00:56:31.470
feeling soothed and to some extent,

00:56:31.470 --> 00:56:35.283
even some relief from
pain or lack of pain.

00:56:36.270 --> 00:56:39.240
Serotonin is associated
with a feeling of satiety

00:56:39.240 --> 00:56:42.690
of having enough of what we already have.

00:56:42.690 --> 00:56:46.770
Now when serotonin is very, very high,

00:56:46.770 --> 00:56:48.780
people can even be sedate.

00:56:48.780 --> 00:56:51.660
They can be completely amotivated,

00:56:51.660 --> 00:56:53.700
no motivation to seek out things

00:56:53.700 --> 00:56:56.013
like food or sex or work or et cetera.

00:56:56.910 --> 00:56:59.340
Whereas when serotonin
levels are very low,

00:56:59.340 --> 00:57:01.500
people can actually exhibit agitation

00:57:01.500 --> 00:57:03.480
and high levels of stress.

00:57:03.480 --> 00:57:05.160
So the levels matter here,

00:57:05.160 --> 00:57:07.893
but again, for sake of
today's conversation,

00:57:08.820 --> 00:57:10.230
when we leverage serotonin,

00:57:10.230 --> 00:57:12.810
we are really leveraging a neuromodulator

00:57:12.810 --> 00:57:14.850
that tends to increase the activity

00:57:14.850 --> 00:57:16.590
of neural circuits in the brain and body

00:57:16.590 --> 00:57:19.260
that make us feel relaxed and happy.

00:57:19.260 --> 00:57:23.130
And it tends to decrease the
activity of neural circuits

00:57:23.130 --> 00:57:25.260
that make us rabidly in pursuit of things

00:57:25.260 --> 00:57:27.210
that we don't have, right.

00:57:27.210 --> 00:57:30.480
The opposite of content and sated

00:57:30.480 --> 00:57:33.510
is motivation, desire
and hunger and thirst

00:57:33.510 --> 00:57:35.040
for things that we don't have.

00:57:35.040 --> 00:57:39.060
So serotonin is the molecule of peace.

00:57:39.060 --> 00:57:40.950
It is the molecule of contentness.

00:57:40.950 --> 00:57:43.800
It is the molecule of having enough,

00:57:43.800 --> 00:57:45.600
at least for the time being

00:57:45.600 --> 00:57:48.090
or the feeling that we have
enough for the time being.

00:57:48.090 --> 00:57:51.210
Now acetylcholine is a fourth
category of neuromodulator

00:57:51.210 --> 00:57:53.430
that as I mentioned earlier,
is somewhat not totally,

00:57:53.430 --> 00:57:56.070
but somewhat distinct
from any direct control

00:57:56.070 --> 00:57:58.230
by the major hormone systems of the body,

00:57:58.230 --> 00:58:01.350
or at least the major
steroid hormone systems.

00:58:01.350 --> 00:58:03.390
And acetylcholine we can say

00:58:03.390 --> 00:58:08.390
is mainly associated with states of focus.

00:58:08.850 --> 00:58:10.470
And we can go a step further and say

00:58:10.470 --> 00:58:13.140
that it's mainly associated
with steps of focus

00:58:13.140 --> 00:58:16.290
as they relate to learning
and encoding new information,

00:58:16.290 --> 00:58:18.600
so called neuroplasticity.

00:58:18.600 --> 00:58:20.280
Now neuroplasticity,

00:58:20.280 --> 00:58:22.500
or the brain and nervous
system's ability to change

00:58:22.500 --> 00:58:23.550
in response to experience

00:58:23.550 --> 00:58:25.710
can be impacted by an enormous number

00:58:25.710 --> 00:58:28.200
of different chemicals,
not just acetylcholine,

00:58:28.200 --> 00:58:32.520
but acetylcholine has a
particularly potent ability

00:58:32.520 --> 00:58:36.600
to open up the thing that
we call neuroplasticity

00:58:36.600 --> 00:58:38.910
to allow plasticity to
happen in one moment

00:58:38.910 --> 00:58:40.440
whereas in a previous moment,

00:58:40.440 --> 00:58:41.730
it could not occur

00:58:41.730 --> 00:58:45.630
because acetylcholine had not
been released in the brain

00:58:45.630 --> 00:58:47.850
or in the spinal cord.

00:58:47.850 --> 00:58:51.420
So acetylcholine is involved
in focus and in learning,

00:58:51.420 --> 00:58:55.140
but it is not necessarily
always associated

00:58:55.140 --> 00:58:58.590
with learning in the
context of highly motivated,

00:58:58.590 --> 00:59:00.450
really ramped up states.

00:59:00.450 --> 00:59:01.650
It can be,

00:59:01.650 --> 00:59:04.770
but acetylcholine can also be released

00:59:04.770 --> 00:59:06.510
and can encourage the learning

00:59:06.510 --> 00:59:09.543
and neuroplasticity
associated with calm states.

00:59:10.440 --> 00:59:14.040
For instance, if somebody
has a newborn child,

00:59:14.040 --> 00:59:18.510
we know that they are
flooded with oxytocin,

00:59:18.510 --> 00:59:20.520
which has actually even
been called the love hormone

00:59:20.520 --> 00:59:23.730
or it does many things in
addition to control feelings

00:59:23.730 --> 00:59:25.380
of romantic attachment

00:59:25.380 --> 00:59:27.540
and attachment to children, et cetera.

00:59:27.540 --> 00:59:30.150
It does all of that, but it
does a lot more, as well.

00:59:30.150 --> 00:59:32.700
But when people have a new child,

00:59:32.700 --> 00:59:35.010
they also tend to be
hyper-focused on that child,

00:59:35.010 --> 00:59:36.030
not just its well-being,

00:59:36.030 --> 00:59:38.430
but they narrow all their thinking,

00:59:38.430 --> 00:59:39.960
all their vision,

00:59:39.960 --> 00:59:41.640
all their hearing to that child

00:59:41.640 --> 00:59:44.220
and there are obvious adaptive reasons

00:59:44.220 --> 00:59:45.480
for wanting to do that.

00:59:45.480 --> 00:59:47.640
I recall a family dinner we had.

00:59:47.640 --> 00:59:49.530
Gosh, this was over 10 years ago.

00:59:49.530 --> 00:59:51.630
We had a couple over.

00:59:51.630 --> 00:59:53.880
My mom was in the habit
of inviting people over

00:59:53.880 --> 00:59:55.770
who didn't have places
to go on the holidays,

00:59:55.770 --> 00:59:56.970
'cause that's just who she is

00:59:56.970 --> 00:59:58.800
and I think it's quite nice.

00:59:58.800 --> 01:00:01.200
So she brought over this
couple, they had a newborn,

01:00:01.200 --> 01:00:02.130
I think this baby had been born

01:00:02.130 --> 01:00:03.420
maybe two or three weeks before

01:00:03.420 --> 01:00:06.480
and it was seated or not seated [laughs].

01:00:06.480 --> 01:00:07.740
It was lying down.

01:00:07.740 --> 01:00:08.573
I couldn't see it.

01:00:08.573 --> 01:00:09.406
It was like a potato bug.

01:00:09.406 --> 01:00:10.320
It could barely hold its head up.

01:00:10.320 --> 01:00:13.290
But it was lying in a
little bassinet on the floor

01:00:13.290 --> 01:00:14.190
as we ate dinner.

01:00:14.190 --> 01:00:17.430
And it was almost hilarious,
it actually was hilarious.

01:00:17.430 --> 01:00:18.630
We laughed a lot about this,

01:00:18.630 --> 01:00:20.250
that the entire meal,

01:00:20.250 --> 01:00:22.530
they were basically staring at this baby.

01:00:22.530 --> 01:00:25.020
They were so clearly in love with the baby

01:00:25.020 --> 01:00:28.830
and so flooded with
oxytocin and also prolactin

01:00:28.830 --> 01:00:31.350
that they couldn't take
their focus off this baby.

01:00:31.350 --> 01:00:34.080
It was actually really
wonderful and endearing to see.

01:00:34.080 --> 01:00:36.360
But in addition to that,

01:00:36.360 --> 01:00:37.410
I'd be willing to bet

01:00:37.410 --> 01:00:39.630
had I been able to do a
little bit of microdialysis,

01:00:39.630 --> 01:00:40.930
which is a ability to

01:00:41.790 --> 01:00:44.610
measure the amounts of neuromodulator

01:00:44.610 --> 01:00:45.780
given location in the brain,

01:00:45.780 --> 01:00:48.070
had I been able to do
that experiment on them

01:00:49.050 --> 01:00:50.820
in that moment, I
would've found that levels

01:00:50.820 --> 01:00:52.920
of acetylcholine were exceedingly high

01:00:52.920 --> 01:00:56.190
because they were so
hyper-focused on this child,

01:00:56.190 --> 01:00:59.280
not just in love with,
but focused on that child.

01:00:59.280 --> 01:01:01.500
And without a doubt,

01:01:01.500 --> 01:01:03.210
the neural circuits related to focus

01:01:03.210 --> 01:01:05.250
and plasticity were heavily engaged

01:01:05.250 --> 01:01:08.940
again for obvious adaptive
reasons related to child rearing

01:01:08.940 --> 01:01:11.460
and learning the coos and cries

01:01:11.460 --> 01:01:14.580
and pain signals and pleasure
signals of one's offspring.

01:01:14.580 --> 01:01:17.250
So we have dopamine
associated with motivation,

01:01:17.250 --> 01:01:20.310
drive and pursuit and
to some extent, focus.

01:01:20.310 --> 01:01:22.530
We have epinephrine and
norepinephrine associated

01:01:22.530 --> 01:01:26.190
with energy of having a
forward center of mass,

01:01:26.190 --> 01:01:27.960
mentally and or physically.

01:01:27.960 --> 01:01:29.040
We have serotonin,

01:01:29.040 --> 01:01:31.470
which is associated with a peaceful,

01:01:31.470 --> 01:01:34.470
content, sated state of being.

01:01:34.470 --> 01:01:35.970
And we have acetylcholine,

01:01:35.970 --> 01:01:38.970
which is associated with focus

01:01:38.970 --> 01:01:41.340
and in particular focus
as it relates to learning

01:01:41.340 --> 01:01:43.530
and encoding new information.

01:01:43.530 --> 01:01:45.180
So let's say you want
to be more motivated.

01:01:45.180 --> 01:01:47.820
You want to be more in pursuit of goals.

01:01:47.820 --> 01:01:50.790
And you want to have more
energy and to be more focused.

01:01:50.790 --> 01:01:52.410
There are many ways to go about that.

01:01:52.410 --> 01:01:56.070
In fact, there's a near
infinite cloud of opportunities,

01:01:56.070 --> 01:01:59.130
everything from prescription
drugs to illicit drugs,

01:01:59.130 --> 01:02:01.920
which I certainly do not recommend,

01:02:01.920 --> 01:02:03.990
supplements, nutrition.

01:02:03.990 --> 01:02:05.790
You can listen to particular music.

01:02:05.790 --> 01:02:08.670
You can do all sorts of
cognitive behavioral,

01:02:08.670 --> 01:02:10.740
nutritional supplementation tricks.

01:02:10.740 --> 01:02:13.740
Or you can just understand

01:02:13.740 --> 01:02:16.890
that what you're really after
are increases in dopamine

01:02:16.890 --> 01:02:19.770
above baseline that you control

01:02:19.770 --> 01:02:22.830
and there are ways to control
them that are quite potent

01:02:22.830 --> 01:02:25.680
and science tells us which tools

01:02:25.680 --> 01:02:27.210
are going to be the most potent

01:02:27.210 --> 01:02:29.820
and the most versatile for you.

01:02:29.820 --> 01:02:31.770
So I'm going to share
those tools with you now

01:02:31.770 --> 01:02:35.250
with the caveat that
each one of those tools

01:02:35.250 --> 01:02:37.887
could be its own entire podcast episode,

01:02:37.887 --> 01:02:40.620
and that we've done near entire episodes

01:02:40.620 --> 01:02:43.260
on each of these tools or small
collections of these tools.

01:02:43.260 --> 01:02:45.960
So I'm going to cover these in
somewhat superficial manner.

01:02:45.960 --> 01:02:48.180
We can provide links to previous episodes

01:02:48.180 --> 01:02:50.460
that relate to each of
these tools in detail,

01:02:50.460 --> 01:02:52.800
but I'll give you enough detail about them

01:02:52.800 --> 01:02:54.990
that would allow you to
incorporate them into your routine

01:02:54.990 --> 01:02:56.190
should you choose.

01:02:56.190 --> 01:02:57.630
Let's say you want to increase dopamine

01:02:57.630 --> 01:02:59.493
for sake of increasing motivation.

01:03:00.510 --> 01:03:02.160
The first thing to do is

01:03:02.160 --> 01:03:05.040
to understand what the
natural behavioral tools are

01:03:05.040 --> 01:03:06.240
for increasing dopamine

01:03:06.240 --> 01:03:09.180
and to do those as
consistently as possible.

01:03:09.180 --> 01:03:11.160
Again, these are tools
that you'll want to do

01:03:11.160 --> 01:03:14.520
nearly every day, if not every day.

01:03:14.520 --> 01:03:17.430
And I know I'm sounding like
a broken record on this one,

01:03:17.430 --> 01:03:21.000
but here again, we come to sunlight

01:03:21.000 --> 01:03:22.770
and I should say,

01:03:22.770 --> 01:03:25.110
not just the desire to,

01:03:25.110 --> 01:03:26.490
but really the need

01:03:26.490 --> 01:03:29.100
for viewing the maximum amount of sunlight

01:03:29.100 --> 01:03:30.600
that one can reasonably get

01:03:30.600 --> 01:03:32.730
given schedules and
locations in the world,

01:03:32.730 --> 01:03:34.110
time of year, et cetera,

01:03:34.110 --> 01:03:35.560
in the early part of the day,

01:03:36.750 --> 01:03:39.690
within the first hour of waking, ideally,

01:03:39.690 --> 01:03:42.060
but certainly in the first
three hours of your day,

01:03:42.060 --> 01:03:44.610
you are going to want to
maximize sunlight exposure

01:03:44.610 --> 01:03:45.443
to your eyes.

01:03:45.443 --> 01:03:48.480
Never look at the sun or
any other light so bright

01:03:48.480 --> 01:03:49.530
that it's painful to look at.

01:03:49.530 --> 01:03:50.940
And yes, of course blinking is fine,

01:03:50.940 --> 01:03:52.380
but no, take sunglasses off,

01:03:52.380 --> 01:03:53.970
go outside once the sun is out

01:03:53.970 --> 01:03:55.590
and get some natural light in your eyes.

01:03:55.590 --> 01:03:57.810
And if it's appropriate

01:03:57.810 --> 01:04:00.120
or I should say in a
way that's appropriate,

01:04:00.120 --> 01:04:02.550
maximize the amount of
sunlight exposure to your skin,

01:04:02.550 --> 01:04:04.770
but please don't get burned.

01:04:04.770 --> 01:04:07.830
Please do wear sunscreen if
you're prone to getting burned.

01:04:07.830 --> 01:04:10.410
Typically early day sunlight
is not going to burn you,

01:04:10.410 --> 01:04:11.760
at least not most people,

01:04:11.760 --> 01:04:14.070
unless you're extremely fair-skinned.

01:04:14.070 --> 01:04:14.903
So don't get burned.

01:04:14.903 --> 01:04:17.370
Do what you need to in order
to protect yourself from burn.

01:04:17.370 --> 01:04:20.160
There's some emerging
controversy about sunscreen

01:04:20.160 --> 01:04:21.810
and which ones are safe
and which ones aren't safe.

01:04:21.810 --> 01:04:23.490
We have not done an episode on that yet,

01:04:23.490 --> 01:04:26.370
but I find it to be an
important and interesting topic.

01:04:26.370 --> 01:04:28.800
Darya Rose, Dr. Darya Rose, I should say,

01:04:28.800 --> 01:04:30.990
has a podcast called
The Darya Rose Podcast

01:04:30.990 --> 01:04:33.600
and did an episode all about sunscreens,

01:04:33.600 --> 01:04:35.340
which are safe, which are not safe,

01:04:35.340 --> 01:04:36.810
by interviewing an expert on that

01:04:36.810 --> 01:04:39.300
so I refer you to that podcast
as it relates to sunscreen.

01:04:39.300 --> 01:04:42.120
But get some natural light
exposure in your eyes.

01:04:42.120 --> 01:04:44.520
And if you wake up
before the sun comes out,

01:04:44.520 --> 01:04:46.140
turn on as many bright lights inside

01:04:46.140 --> 01:04:48.600
as you can turn on reasonably

01:04:48.600 --> 01:04:49.920
given your electric bill, et cetera.

01:04:49.920 --> 01:04:52.650
Get a lot of bright sunlight
exposure early in the day

01:04:52.650 --> 01:04:55.410
and get a lot of sunlight
exposure to your skin

01:04:55.410 --> 01:04:56.850
in the early part of the day,

01:04:56.850 --> 01:04:59.310
in a way that doesn't burn you,

01:04:59.310 --> 01:05:01.410
meaning burn your skin
or blind you, please.

01:05:01.410 --> 01:05:03.660
Please don't do anything
that harms your vision

01:05:03.660 --> 01:05:06.780
like stare into a bright
light, that's painful.

01:05:06.780 --> 01:05:07.860
What does that do?

01:05:07.860 --> 01:05:09.930
Well, it sets in motion a number

01:05:09.930 --> 01:05:11.490
of different biological cascades.

01:05:11.490 --> 01:05:12.960
Some are very fast.

01:05:12.960 --> 01:05:14.430
There are fast actions of sunlight

01:05:14.430 --> 01:05:15.600
that will trigger for instance,

01:05:15.600 --> 01:05:18.090
dopamine release from
different parts of your brain

01:05:18.090 --> 01:05:19.710
and your endocrine system.

01:05:19.710 --> 01:05:23.730
And we now know that it
increases levels of genes

01:05:23.730 --> 01:05:25.200
related to thyroid hormone

01:05:25.200 --> 01:05:28.740
and actually increases
certain dopamine receptors.

01:05:28.740 --> 01:05:29.790
So there's a wonderful paper.

01:05:29.790 --> 01:05:31.740
We will provide a link to this paper

01:05:31.740 --> 01:05:33.390
that shows that sunlight exposure

01:05:33.390 --> 01:05:37.020
can actually increase the
amount of so-called DRD4.

01:05:37.020 --> 01:05:38.697
This is a particular type
of dopamine receptor,

01:05:38.697 --> 01:05:41.040
the dopamine receptor four.

01:05:41.040 --> 01:05:42.690
The genes for dopamine receptor four

01:05:42.690 --> 01:05:44.520
are actually under photic control.

01:05:44.520 --> 01:05:46.350
So if you get sunlight
exposure to your eyes

01:05:46.350 --> 01:05:47.183
and it does have to be to your eyes

01:05:47.183 --> 01:05:48.780
in the early part of the day,

01:05:48.780 --> 01:05:51.450
you increase the amount of
dopamine receptor that you have,

01:05:51.450 --> 01:05:53.550
which allows whatever circulating dopamine

01:05:53.550 --> 01:05:54.690
happens to be there

01:05:54.690 --> 01:05:57.330
to have a greater effect on motivation

01:05:57.330 --> 01:05:59.520
and I should say also on mood and feelings

01:05:59.520 --> 01:06:02.370
of being in pursuit and generally,

01:06:02.370 --> 01:06:05.250
in craving and pursuit of things in life.

01:06:05.250 --> 01:06:07.560
Now there's another way
to increase the effect

01:06:07.560 --> 01:06:09.750
of whatever dopamine
happens to be circulating

01:06:09.750 --> 01:06:11.400
in your brain and body.

01:06:11.400 --> 01:06:14.550
And this again, relates
to increasing the number

01:06:14.550 --> 01:06:18.390
or the efficacy of the
receptors for dopamine.

01:06:18.390 --> 01:06:21.060
Now here we're not talking about
the dopamine receptor four,

01:06:21.060 --> 01:06:23.280
but a different category
of dopamine receptors,

01:06:23.280 --> 01:06:25.260
the D2 and D3 receptors,

01:06:25.260 --> 01:06:28.140
which are expressed multiple
places in your brain and body

01:06:28.140 --> 01:06:29.580
and bind dopamine,

01:06:29.580 --> 01:06:32.040
meaning dopamine parks in
them like a parking spot

01:06:32.040 --> 01:06:33.870
and allows dopamine

01:06:33.870 --> 01:06:37.380
to generally increase the
activity of the neurons

01:06:37.380 --> 01:06:39.930
and cells that express
those dopamine receptors.

01:06:39.930 --> 01:06:41.100
How do you do that?

01:06:41.100 --> 01:06:45.300
Well, turns out that regular
ingestion of caffeine

01:06:45.300 --> 01:06:47.100
at safe and appropriate levels,

01:06:47.100 --> 01:06:50.100
about a hundred to 250 milligrams

01:06:50.100 --> 01:06:52.544
is going to increase the number of D2

01:06:52.544 --> 01:06:54.090
and D3 dopamine receptors.

01:06:54.090 --> 01:06:57.030
I talked a little bit about
this on a previous episode.

01:06:57.030 --> 01:06:58.950
Again, we'll provide
links to these studies,

01:06:58.950 --> 01:07:00.240
but this is an important finding,

01:07:00.240 --> 01:07:03.300
I believe, because this
is not about the acute,

01:07:03.300 --> 01:07:05.460
the immediate effects of
caffeine on alertness,

01:07:05.460 --> 01:07:06.840
although those occur, too.

01:07:06.840 --> 01:07:08.220
When you drink caffeine,

01:07:08.220 --> 01:07:10.980
it's going to increase
your levels of adrenaline

01:07:10.980 --> 01:07:12.360
and so-called epinephrine,

01:07:12.360 --> 01:07:13.740
which will increase your energy levels.

01:07:13.740 --> 01:07:16.530
It's going to decrease levels
of something called adenosine,

01:07:16.530 --> 01:07:17.880
which builds up while you're sleepy.

01:07:17.880 --> 01:07:19.740
It's going to make you feel
less sleepy, more alert,

01:07:19.740 --> 01:07:20.820
more energetic.

01:07:20.820 --> 01:07:22.200
That's sort of obvious.

01:07:22.200 --> 01:07:24.780
But what's less obvious is
that it's increasing the number

01:07:24.780 --> 01:07:26.850
and efficacy of dopamine receptors

01:07:26.850 --> 01:07:28.860
so that whatever dopamine
happens to be around

01:07:28.860 --> 01:07:32.820
in your system is going to
have more of a potent effect.

01:07:32.820 --> 01:07:34.500
So how much caffeine should you drink?

01:07:34.500 --> 01:07:35.970
That's going to vary
from person to person.

01:07:35.970 --> 01:07:39.210
Some people are very sensitive
to caffeine, others are not.

01:07:39.210 --> 01:07:42.120
I tend to be fairly
insensitive to caffeine,

01:07:42.120 --> 01:07:44.310
'cause I've been drinking it
for a long period of time.

01:07:44.310 --> 01:07:47.760
But you know, after one or two
cups of espresso or coffee,

01:07:47.760 --> 01:07:48.930
I feel like I've had enough.

01:07:48.930 --> 01:07:50.670
I tend to drink my
caffeine early in the day,

01:07:50.670 --> 01:07:52.230
which is what I'm going
to recommend that you do,

01:07:52.230 --> 01:07:55.260
not drinking caffeine past
two and certainly not 4:00 PM,

01:07:55.260 --> 01:07:56.400
if you're on a typical schedule

01:07:56.400 --> 01:07:58.080
and you want to be able
to sleep that night,

01:07:58.080 --> 01:07:59.880
even if you can fall asleep.

01:07:59.880 --> 01:08:01.770
Having too much caffeine
in your system is not good

01:08:01.770 --> 01:08:03.690
because it disrupts the
architecture of sleep.

01:08:03.690 --> 01:08:06.720
And now knowing about all
the metabolic variability

01:08:06.720 --> 01:08:09.420
across the night, according
to different stages of sleep,

01:08:09.420 --> 01:08:11.850
it should be even more obvious

01:08:11.850 --> 01:08:13.590
as to why disrupting the
architecture of sleep

01:08:13.590 --> 01:08:14.640
would be bad for you.

01:08:14.640 --> 01:08:16.710
So limit that caffeine
intake to early in the day

01:08:16.710 --> 01:08:18.720
and don't go ballistic.

01:08:18.720 --> 01:08:20.160
Certainly don't go ballistic in any case,

01:08:20.160 --> 01:08:21.570
but for most people anywhere

01:08:21.570 --> 01:08:24.540
from 100 to 400 milligrams of caffeine

01:08:24.540 --> 01:08:26.250
is going to have this effect.

01:08:26.250 --> 01:08:29.250
And this effect again is
a slow accumulating effect

01:08:29.250 --> 01:08:31.170
by drinking caffeine
consistently day-to-day.

01:08:31.170 --> 01:08:33.690
I get my caffeine mainly
from Yerba Mate tea.

01:08:33.690 --> 01:08:35.190
I want to emphasize that
it's probably a good idea

01:08:35.190 --> 01:08:36.930
to stay away from the smoked mates.

01:08:36.930 --> 01:08:39.000
There's some evidence
those can be carcinogenic,

01:08:39.000 --> 01:08:41.190
but I brew my own Yerba Mate tea,

01:08:41.190 --> 01:08:43.080
or sometimes I'll drink
coffee or espresso,

01:08:43.080 --> 01:08:44.340
or sometimes both frankly,

01:08:44.340 --> 01:08:46.410
as long as I'm hydrating enough
and I'm getting enough salt,

01:08:46.410 --> 01:08:48.840
then I tend to feel fine
with that much caffeine.

01:08:48.840 --> 01:08:50.730
The other way to increase dopamine

01:08:50.730 --> 01:08:52.800
and to make sure that your
baseline levels of dopamine

01:08:52.800 --> 01:08:53.970
are high enough is to make sure

01:08:53.970 --> 01:08:55.170
that you're eating sufficient numbers

01:08:55.170 --> 01:08:56.850
of tyrosine-rich foods.

01:08:56.850 --> 01:08:58.983
You can look up which
foods include tyrosine.

01:08:58.983 --> 01:09:01.650
Tyrosine is a precursor to dopamine.

01:09:01.650 --> 01:09:04.800
It's an amino acid that
is in direct pathway

01:09:04.800 --> 01:09:06.240
to dopamine synthesis

01:09:06.240 --> 01:09:08.940
and tyrosine foods include things like

01:09:08.940 --> 01:09:11.640
certain meats, Parmesan cheese,

01:09:11.640 --> 01:09:13.860
very high in tyrosine, for instance.

01:09:13.860 --> 01:09:15.810
In fact, there's something
called the cheese effect,

01:09:15.810 --> 01:09:16.643
believe it or not.

01:09:16.643 --> 01:09:17.850
I don't want to go too far off topic,

01:09:17.850 --> 01:09:19.350
but the cheese effect
is kind of interesting

01:09:19.350 --> 01:09:23.010
because certain people
will take antidepressants

01:09:23.010 --> 01:09:25.680
that are so called MAO inhibitors,

01:09:25.680 --> 01:09:28.050
monoamine oxidase inhibitors.

01:09:28.050 --> 01:09:30.060
Anytime you hear ASE, that's an enzyme.

01:09:30.060 --> 01:09:31.560
They will take these inhibitors

01:09:31.560 --> 01:09:35.340
that prevent the breakdown of dopamine

01:09:35.340 --> 01:09:37.320
and other so-called catacholamines,

01:09:37.320 --> 01:09:39.870
which allow more dopamine
to be in circulation.

01:09:39.870 --> 01:09:42.480
But if these people eat certain cheeses,

01:09:42.480 --> 01:09:45.060
including Parmesan cheese,
and there are other foods,

01:09:45.060 --> 01:09:47.160
of course that include not just tyrosine,

01:09:47.160 --> 01:09:49.173
but one of the derivatives of tyrosine,

01:09:50.430 --> 01:09:51.750
called tyramine,

01:09:51.750 --> 01:09:54.660
that generates what's
called the cheese effect,

01:09:54.660 --> 01:09:56.580
which is people get potent migraines,

01:09:56.580 --> 01:09:58.350
headaches, blood pressure goes up.

01:09:58.350 --> 01:10:00.540
Why, well, because they've
got a lot of tyrosine

01:10:00.540 --> 01:10:02.580
in their system and
dopamine in their system.

01:10:02.580 --> 01:10:04.740
And they've got less of the enzyme

01:10:04.740 --> 01:10:07.800
that removes that dopamine
or limits its action

01:10:07.800 --> 01:10:09.810
and so they have an excess of dopamine

01:10:09.810 --> 01:10:13.020
and dopamine has effects on
blood pressure, et cetera.

01:10:13.020 --> 01:10:14.790
So the cheese effect is something to avoid

01:10:14.790 --> 01:10:16.800
if you are somebody who's taking drugs

01:10:16.800 --> 01:10:20.190
that tap into or manipulate
the dopamine pathway,

01:10:20.190 --> 01:10:21.750
either for Parkinson's or depressions,

01:10:21.750 --> 01:10:23.070
obviously you're going
to want to be careful

01:10:23.070 --> 01:10:27.810
about adjusting up or down
levels of dopamine too potently.

01:10:27.810 --> 01:10:29.070
So mind the cheese effect

01:10:29.070 --> 01:10:31.260
if you're taking an MAO inhibitor.

01:10:31.260 --> 01:10:33.060
There's a lot of information
about this online.

01:10:33.060 --> 01:10:35.820
For most people, eating
foods like Parmesan cheese,

01:10:35.820 --> 01:10:39.540
eating foods like certain
meats and certain vegetables

01:10:39.540 --> 01:10:41.460
also can increase tyrosine levels,

01:10:41.460 --> 01:10:43.800
which will increase dopamine synthesis.

01:10:43.800 --> 01:10:46.020
So these are ways of
modulating, more or less,

01:10:46.020 --> 01:10:48.930
the baseline of dopamine
that you are able to produce

01:10:48.930 --> 01:10:51.870
and the ways that dopamine
can have its action

01:10:51.870 --> 01:10:54.783
by way of binding to
receptors more potently.

01:10:55.620 --> 01:10:58.290
Now there are other ways
to increase dopamine

01:10:58.290 --> 01:11:00.540
in a more acute or directed way,

01:11:00.540 --> 01:11:02.790
ways to spike your dopamine,

01:11:02.790 --> 01:11:07.790
to enhance your state of
motivation, mood, focus and so on.

01:11:09.030 --> 01:11:11.760
And in thinking about the
vast landscape of tools

01:11:11.760 --> 01:11:12.593
that can do that,

01:11:12.593 --> 01:11:14.430
we have one category of tools,

01:11:14.430 --> 01:11:15.870
which are the really, really bad things

01:11:15.870 --> 01:11:17.280
that I don't recommend anybody do.

01:11:17.280 --> 01:11:19.590
In fact, I recommend nobody do ever,

01:11:19.590 --> 01:11:22.890
which are things like cocaine,
methamphetamine, et cetera.

01:11:22.890 --> 01:11:25.860
They are incredibly destructive for lives

01:11:25.860 --> 01:11:28.530
because of the way that they
so potently increase dopamine

01:11:28.530 --> 01:11:30.990
and then the crash in
dopamine that occurs later.

01:11:30.990 --> 01:11:33.510
I mean, they can indeed
and often do ruin lives.

01:11:33.510 --> 01:11:35.700
So we're leaving those off the table.

01:11:35.700 --> 01:11:39.270
There are of course, prescription
drugs that many people,

01:11:39.270 --> 01:11:43.230
especially people who have
clinically diagnosed ADHD,

01:11:43.230 --> 01:11:45.210
attention deficit hyperactivity disorder,

01:11:45.210 --> 01:11:48.510
rely on and in fact
benefit from in many cases,

01:11:48.510 --> 01:11:52.080
things like Ritalin, Adderall, Vyvanse.

01:11:52.080 --> 01:11:54.930
Nowadays, there's also
a lot of interest in use

01:11:54.930 --> 01:11:56.820
of things like Modafinil, Armodafinil.

01:11:56.820 --> 01:11:59.640
I covered all of those
in the episode on ADHD.

01:11:59.640 --> 01:12:01.380
And you can find that on HubermanLab.com

01:12:01.380 --> 01:12:04.530
and the other places
this podcast is found.

01:12:04.530 --> 01:12:07.350
Prescription drugs aside because
they require a prescription

01:12:07.350 --> 01:12:09.210
and a discussion that's in-depth

01:12:09.210 --> 01:12:12.330
and appropriate with your
physician, healthcare provider.

01:12:12.330 --> 01:12:15.450
There are supplements
that can very potently

01:12:15.450 --> 01:12:17.160
increase dopamine as well,

01:12:17.160 --> 01:12:18.330
perhaps not to the extent

01:12:18.330 --> 01:12:20.850
that some of those other
prescription drugs can,

01:12:20.850 --> 01:12:22.950
but certainly to a degree that will impact

01:12:22.950 --> 01:12:25.020
and increase dopamine and motivation

01:12:25.020 --> 01:12:26.820
and the other states
dopamine is associated with.

01:12:26.820 --> 01:12:29.730
And the two main categories of supplements

01:12:29.730 --> 01:12:31.830
that are very effective
in raising dopamine

01:12:31.830 --> 01:12:33.330
and here I should provide the caveat

01:12:33.330 --> 01:12:35.220
that anytime you're going
to add or remove anything

01:12:35.220 --> 01:12:38.040
from your supplementation protocols,

01:12:38.040 --> 01:12:39.540
please talk to a physician

01:12:39.540 --> 01:12:41.340
who is knowledgeable on these topics.

01:12:41.340 --> 01:12:43.320
If you're somebody who has,

01:12:43.320 --> 01:12:46.350
or is taking drugs for
depression or mania,

01:12:46.350 --> 01:12:49.080
please be very cautious about
manipulating your dopamine

01:12:49.080 --> 01:12:50.040
in any case.

01:12:50.040 --> 01:12:51.420
I don't just say that to protect us,

01:12:51.420 --> 01:12:52.803
I say that to protect you.

01:12:53.760 --> 01:12:56.160
But if we were to look at
the supplement landscape

01:12:56.160 --> 01:12:58.140
and ask which supplement
increased dopamine,

01:12:58.140 --> 01:13:00.270
there are a vast number of them,

01:13:00.270 --> 01:13:02.760
but the three main ones,

01:13:02.760 --> 01:13:05.190
the most effective ones that
are readily available out there

01:13:05.190 --> 01:13:07.680
without a prescription
are mucuna pruriens.

01:13:07.680 --> 01:13:10.320
This is actually the
outside of a velvety bean

01:13:10.320 --> 01:13:12.377
that has been extracted
and put into a supplement.

01:13:12.377 --> 01:13:15.510
Mucuna pruriens is actual L-dopa.

01:13:15.510 --> 01:13:18.990
It's 99% L-dopa, which
is a prescription drug

01:13:18.990 --> 01:13:22.230
that is given for Parkinson's
and for other purposes

01:13:22.230 --> 01:13:25.710
where increasing dopamine is important.

01:13:25.710 --> 01:13:27.720
I don't recommend mucuna pruriens.

01:13:27.720 --> 01:13:29.250
I'm not saying that no one should take it,

01:13:29.250 --> 01:13:30.450
but I don't take it.

01:13:30.450 --> 01:13:31.410
And I don't recommend it

01:13:31.410 --> 01:13:33.780
because it tends to so potently

01:13:33.780 --> 01:13:35.250
and acutely increase dopamine,

01:13:35.250 --> 01:13:38.040
that there's a pretty
substantial crash afterwards.

01:13:38.040 --> 01:13:40.650
So I avoid it and I
don't generally suggest

01:13:40.650 --> 01:13:44.430
that anyone take it unless
there's really a clinical need,

01:13:44.430 --> 01:13:46.290
or they're working very
closely with somebody

01:13:46.290 --> 01:13:48.480
that can really monitor that.

01:13:48.480 --> 01:13:52.080
The other two supplements
that can increase dopamine

01:13:52.080 --> 01:13:53.730
in a short-term way,

01:13:53.730 --> 01:13:56.070
but in a significant way,

01:13:56.070 --> 01:13:57.050
are L-tyrosine

01:13:57.050 --> 01:13:59.730
so you can buy that as
a supplement amino acid.

01:13:59.730 --> 01:14:00.750
I sometimes take this.

01:14:00.750 --> 01:14:04.380
I would say, I probably take
it about once a week maximum

01:14:04.380 --> 01:14:07.140
for work bouts or workouts.

01:14:07.140 --> 01:14:08.940
I'll take it in dosages of anywhere

01:14:08.940 --> 01:14:11.250
from 500 milligrams to
a thousand milligrams.

01:14:11.250 --> 01:14:13.740
People vary tremendously
in their sensitivity

01:14:13.740 --> 01:14:15.240
to supplementing L-tyrosine.

01:14:15.240 --> 01:14:17.250
I know people that can take two grams.

01:14:17.250 --> 01:14:20.100
I know people that can barely
take a hundred milligrams.

01:14:20.100 --> 01:14:21.720
I know people that the best dose for them

01:14:21.720 --> 01:14:22.860
is zero milligrams.

01:14:22.860 --> 01:14:25.380
So there's a lot of variation
there depending on sensitivity

01:14:25.380 --> 01:14:28.590
and they're natural
baseline levels of dopamine

01:14:28.590 --> 01:14:30.090
and whether or not they're
doing a lot of other things

01:14:30.090 --> 01:14:31.830
to support dopamine,

01:14:31.830 --> 01:14:33.840
but nonetheless, taking
L-tyrosine will lead

01:14:33.840 --> 01:14:36.600
to fairly substantial
increases in dopamine

01:14:36.600 --> 01:14:38.670
within about 15 to 45 minutes

01:14:38.670 --> 01:14:41.940
and it lasts for about
30 minutes to two hours

01:14:41.940 --> 01:14:43.710
and then there's kind of a tapering off.

01:14:43.710 --> 01:14:45.780
Some people experience a
little bit of an emotional

01:14:45.780 --> 01:14:49.620
and or I should say energetic
crash, some people don't.

01:14:49.620 --> 01:14:52.410
And then the other supplement
that I certainly use,

01:14:52.410 --> 01:14:53.790
and that I know a number
of other people use

01:14:53.790 --> 01:14:55.380
is more fast-acting, but more potent,

01:14:55.380 --> 01:14:57.210
which is phenylethylamine.

01:14:57.210 --> 01:15:00.540
This relates to the
so-called PEA molecule,

01:15:00.540 --> 01:15:02.370
PEA, P-E-A,

01:15:02.370 --> 01:15:05.670
and phenylethylamine increases dopamine

01:15:05.670 --> 01:15:07.680
and some metabolites related to dopamine

01:15:07.680 --> 01:15:09.390
in ways that really increase energy

01:15:09.390 --> 01:15:11.070
and feelings of well-being and motivation.

01:15:11.070 --> 01:15:12.750
And again, it's fast-acting.

01:15:12.750 --> 01:15:14.010
So my particular protocol,

01:15:14.010 --> 01:15:17.190
the one I use is I'll
take phenylethylamine

01:15:17.190 --> 01:15:19.830
at dosages of about 300 to 600 milligrams

01:15:19.830 --> 01:15:22.620
along with some L-tyrosine,

01:15:22.620 --> 01:15:25.710
or I'll take it on its
own with a molecule,

01:15:25.710 --> 01:15:26.700
or I should say a compound

01:15:26.700 --> 01:15:27.780
that we'll talk about a little bit later

01:15:27.780 --> 01:15:30.330
as it relates to acetylcholine, Alpha-GPC,

01:15:30.330 --> 01:15:33.270
but tyrosine and phenylethylamine
taken alone or together

01:15:33.270 --> 01:15:36.060
will make you feel more
motivated and more alert,

01:15:36.060 --> 01:15:38.940
more willing and able to lean into

01:15:38.940 --> 01:15:40.290
particular motivated behaviors,

01:15:40.290 --> 01:15:42.420
whether or not they're
physical or cognitive.

01:15:42.420 --> 01:15:44.820
If you'd like to learn
more about these compounds

01:15:44.820 --> 01:15:46.890
and their supplementation
and their effects,

01:15:46.890 --> 01:15:48.420
I encourage you to check out

01:15:48.420 --> 01:15:51.270
the ever valuable website, examine.com.

01:15:51.270 --> 01:15:52.860
It's zero cost to access,

01:15:52.860 --> 01:15:55.500
and they provide references
and some more details

01:15:55.500 --> 01:15:58.770
about these sorts of compounds
and other related compounds.

01:15:58.770 --> 01:16:00.870
Now if we are going to
look at behavioral tools

01:16:00.870 --> 01:16:03.120
for potently increasing dopamine,

01:16:03.120 --> 01:16:05.460
that too is a vast landscape.

01:16:05.460 --> 01:16:09.690
And we know based on hundreds,
if not thousands of studies

01:16:09.690 --> 01:16:13.650
that things like winning
at some sort of competition

01:16:13.650 --> 01:16:15.810
or succeeding in reaching a goal

01:16:15.810 --> 01:16:17.400
can certainly increase dopamine.

01:16:17.400 --> 01:16:19.020
We talk a lot about this in the episode

01:16:19.020 --> 01:16:21.510
on dopamine, motivation and drive,

01:16:21.510 --> 01:16:23.220
but leaving that aside,

01:16:23.220 --> 01:16:25.440
there are certain behavioral protocols

01:16:25.440 --> 01:16:29.460
that are unrelated to your
overall goals and motivations

01:16:29.460 --> 01:16:32.040
that can increase dopamine
in a very sustained way.

01:16:32.040 --> 01:16:33.780
And without question,

01:16:33.780 --> 01:16:36.360
the most potent behavioral
tool for doing that

01:16:36.360 --> 01:16:39.570
is going to be deliberate, cold exposure.

01:16:39.570 --> 01:16:43.230
Deliberate cold exposure has
been talked about a lot here

01:16:43.230 --> 01:16:46.410
and elsewhere in terms of its

01:16:46.410 --> 01:16:49.770
ability to do things
like reduce inflammation

01:16:49.770 --> 01:16:52.620
as a way to test and improve resilience

01:16:52.620 --> 01:16:54.840
because uncomfortable cold,

01:16:54.840 --> 01:16:56.580
provided its applied safely

01:16:56.580 --> 01:16:58.740
is a great way to learn
to be more resilient

01:16:58.740 --> 01:17:01.080
because you're essentially
staying or forcing yourself

01:17:01.080 --> 01:17:02.430
to stay in a circumstance

01:17:02.430 --> 01:17:05.010
where your system is
flooded with adrenaline.

01:17:05.010 --> 01:17:10.010
But one lesser known aspect
of deliberate cold exposure

01:17:10.140 --> 01:17:11.580
is one that's been demonstrated

01:17:11.580 --> 01:17:13.080
quite convincingly in humans,

01:17:13.080 --> 01:17:15.060
comes from a study
published in the year 2000.

01:17:15.060 --> 01:17:16.110
I'll link to this study.

01:17:16.110 --> 01:17:18.630
I love this study by the way.

01:17:18.630 --> 01:17:20.160
Covered it many times on this podcast

01:17:20.160 --> 01:17:23.430
because I love it so much and
I think it's truly important.

01:17:23.430 --> 01:17:26.490
And that's the study from Srámek et al,

01:17:26.490 --> 01:17:28.207
entitled, "Human physiological responses

01:17:28.207 --> 01:17:30.420
"to immersion into water
of different temperatures".

01:17:30.420 --> 01:17:32.250
I'm not going to go into
this into a ton of detail

01:17:32.250 --> 01:17:33.420
for sake of time,

01:17:33.420 --> 01:17:35.520
but basically what they show is that

01:17:35.520 --> 01:17:36.690
putting people into cold water,

01:17:36.690 --> 01:17:38.790
and I should mention
the water that they used

01:17:38.790 --> 01:17:40.200
in this study wasn't that cold.

01:17:40.200 --> 01:17:41.400
They had a bunch of different conditions,

01:17:41.400 --> 01:17:43.530
but they had people that
got into, for instance,

01:17:43.530 --> 01:17:46.470
60 degree Fahrenheit
water for up to two hours,

01:17:46.470 --> 01:17:48.044
had them sitting there,

01:17:48.044 --> 01:17:49.470
had them in a lawn chair up to their neck,

01:17:49.470 --> 01:17:54.420
had very long sustained increases
in dopamine transmission

01:17:54.420 --> 01:17:57.930
and dopamine circulation
in their brain and body.

01:17:57.930 --> 01:18:00.840
And also some of the other catacholamines,

01:18:00.840 --> 01:18:01.673
as I mentioned before,

01:18:01.673 --> 01:18:03.540
dopamine tends to
collaborate with epinephrine

01:18:03.540 --> 01:18:05.250
and vice versa.

01:18:05.250 --> 01:18:07.110
Now you don't need to put yourself

01:18:07.110 --> 01:18:08.940
into 60 degree Fahrenheit water

01:18:08.940 --> 01:18:11.100
to get these kind of sustained increases.

01:18:11.100 --> 01:18:13.700
And you certainly don't
need to do it for two hours.

01:18:14.640 --> 01:18:16.140
We have a strong reason to believe

01:18:16.140 --> 01:18:17.820
based on subsequent studies,

01:18:17.820 --> 01:18:19.800
in fact, published just this last year,

01:18:19.800 --> 01:18:22.800
that getting into much colder water

01:18:22.800 --> 01:18:24.810
of say 50 degrees or 55 degrees

01:18:24.810 --> 01:18:27.450
or even 45 degrees Fahrenheit

01:18:27.450 --> 01:18:30.600
can potently increase dopamine
and epinephrine, as well.

01:18:30.600 --> 01:18:32.160
And that you don't need to expose yourself

01:18:32.160 --> 01:18:34.590
to that cold water for nearly as long.

01:18:34.590 --> 01:18:38.940
So perhaps even as short as
one minute or even 30 seconds

01:18:38.940 --> 01:18:40.680
exposure to really cold water,

01:18:40.680 --> 01:18:44.520
can lead to these potent
long-lasting increases in dopamine.

01:18:44.520 --> 01:18:47.010
Many people will ask
which protocols to follow.

01:18:47.010 --> 01:18:49.350
For instance, will a cold shower suffice?

01:18:49.350 --> 01:18:51.630
Very likely, yes, if your
shower gets cold enough.

01:18:51.630 --> 01:18:53.490
Do you need ice floating in the bath?

01:18:53.490 --> 01:18:55.080
No, it's all about the temperature

01:18:55.080 --> 01:18:57.150
not whether or not there's
ice present or not.

01:18:57.150 --> 01:18:58.350
How long to stay in there?

01:18:58.350 --> 01:18:59.700
There are a lot of details

01:18:59.700 --> 01:19:01.590
that we don't have time
to go into this episode.

01:19:01.590 --> 01:19:05.070
Please see the episode on
the Use of Deliberate Cold

01:19:05.070 --> 01:19:06.390
for Health and Performance.

01:19:06.390 --> 01:19:08.100
You'll find that at HubermanLab.com.

01:19:08.100 --> 01:19:09.690
We have a newsletter related to this.

01:19:09.690 --> 01:19:11.850
It gets into a lot of detailed protocols,

01:19:11.850 --> 01:19:14.430
but in general, we can say

01:19:14.430 --> 01:19:17.610
that the way to evoke dopamine
and epinephrine release

01:19:17.610 --> 01:19:20.040
using cold water is to

01:19:20.040 --> 01:19:22.020
ideally you would do cold water immersion.

01:19:22.020 --> 01:19:24.120
If you can't, you'd use cold shower,

01:19:24.120 --> 01:19:26.880
but you want to use a
temperature that is safe,

01:19:26.880 --> 01:19:28.530
meaning you're not going
to have a heart attack,

01:19:28.530 --> 01:19:29.700
but that is uncomfortable

01:19:29.700 --> 01:19:31.170
such that you really want to get out

01:19:31.170 --> 01:19:33.960
and then staying in for
anywhere from one minute

01:19:33.960 --> 01:19:36.630
to 10 minutes, depending on
how cold adapted you are,

01:19:36.630 --> 01:19:38.130
and then getting out

01:19:38.130 --> 01:19:39.780
and drying off and going about your day,

01:19:39.780 --> 01:19:41.010
unless you have some other protocol

01:19:41.010 --> 01:19:42.840
that you're trying to
extract from the cold.

01:19:42.840 --> 01:19:45.180
So this is a cold exposure protocol

01:19:45.180 --> 01:19:48.570
specifically aimed at increasing dopamine.

01:19:48.570 --> 01:19:50.490
For some people out there, you might think

01:19:50.490 --> 01:19:53.370
this is kind of silly using
cold water to increase dopamine.

01:19:53.370 --> 01:19:56.130
But when you look at the data in humans

01:19:56.130 --> 01:19:58.080
on the effect of cold water exposure

01:19:58.080 --> 01:20:01.290
to stimulate long-lasting,
very significant increases

01:20:01.290 --> 01:20:02.880
in dopamine and epinephrine,

01:20:02.880 --> 01:20:06.330
I think you'll agree that
this is a really potent tool

01:20:06.330 --> 01:20:09.870
that provided its given
safely and gone about safely,

01:20:09.870 --> 01:20:12.090
is giving you the kinds
of increases in dopamine

01:20:12.090 --> 01:20:14.940
that you would seek using
prescription pharmacology.

01:20:14.940 --> 01:20:16.770
Now it shouldn't be used as a replacement

01:20:16.770 --> 01:20:18.540
for prescription pharmacology,

01:20:18.540 --> 01:20:20.790
although people have done that to success.

01:20:20.790 --> 01:20:23.670
One of the previous guest
on the Huberman Lab Podcast

01:20:23.670 --> 01:20:25.320
was Dr. Anna Lembke, our director

01:20:25.320 --> 01:20:27.570
of the Dual Diagnosis
Addiction Clinic at Stanford.

01:20:27.570 --> 01:20:29.730
She has an amazing book
called "Dopamine Nation",

01:20:29.730 --> 01:20:32.940
all about dopamine and
both its uses, healthy,

01:20:32.940 --> 01:20:35.430
and its perils in things like addiction.

01:20:35.430 --> 01:20:38.070
And she describes a patient of hers

01:20:38.070 --> 01:20:39.780
that used deliberate cold exposure

01:20:39.780 --> 01:20:41.730
to try and maintain dopamine levels

01:20:41.730 --> 01:20:43.320
while coming off of drugs

01:20:43.320 --> 01:20:45.990
that were increasing dopamine so potently

01:20:45.990 --> 01:20:48.090
that they were putting him
down the path of addiction.

01:20:48.090 --> 01:20:51.630
So the use of cold water
for increasing dopamine

01:20:51.630 --> 01:20:52.860
is a real tool.

01:20:52.860 --> 01:20:54.900
It's, I would say, a power tool.

01:20:54.900 --> 01:20:57.265
In fact, it's the kind
of thing that if you want

01:20:57.265 --> 01:20:59.700
to increase dopamine
for sake of motivation,

01:20:59.700 --> 01:21:01.230
it might be your first go-to

01:21:01.230 --> 01:21:02.730
provided you're also doing the things

01:21:02.730 --> 01:21:04.410
to maintain dopamine baseline

01:21:04.410 --> 01:21:06.480
like sunlight exposure in particular,

01:21:06.480 --> 01:21:08.130
making sure you're
getting sufficient amounts

01:21:08.130 --> 01:21:10.620
of tyrosine-containing foods and so on.

01:21:10.620 --> 01:21:11.790
And now just very briefly,

01:21:11.790 --> 01:21:14.100
I want to point to a few quick tools

01:21:14.100 --> 01:21:17.850
that good peer review data tell us

01:21:17.850 --> 01:21:20.070
can be leveraged in order to make sure

01:21:20.070 --> 01:21:22.590
that you have sufficient
dopamine when you want it

01:21:22.590 --> 01:21:24.930
or that it's available
for it to be released

01:21:24.930 --> 01:21:27.960
by any number of the
tools I provided thus far.

01:21:27.960 --> 01:21:31.260
And those are sufficient
number of B vitamins.

01:21:31.260 --> 01:21:34.140
So it turns out that B vitamins,

01:21:34.140 --> 01:21:37.170
in particular B6 or vitamin B6,

01:21:37.170 --> 01:21:40.050
can potently reduce prolactin levels.

01:21:40.050 --> 01:21:42.390
And again, prolactin and
dopamine tend to work

01:21:42.390 --> 01:21:44.070
in kind of push pull fashion.

01:21:44.070 --> 01:21:45.990
That said you should be cautious

01:21:45.990 --> 01:21:48.930
about taking excessive levels of B6.

01:21:48.930 --> 01:21:50.970
It is a vitamin that if you take too much,

01:21:50.970 --> 01:21:52.830
you'll likely excrete
it through your urine,

01:21:52.830 --> 01:21:54.750
but there is evidence that having

01:21:54.750 --> 01:21:56.910
excessively high levels of B6

01:21:56.910 --> 01:21:59.490
or supplementing with
excessively high levels of B6

01:21:59.490 --> 01:22:01.350
can cause some peripheral neuropathy,

01:22:01.350 --> 01:22:03.630
some death of nerves in the periphery.

01:22:03.630 --> 01:22:05.580
If you want to know what dosage
levels are relevant there

01:22:05.580 --> 01:22:06.720
just simply look it up online.

01:22:06.720 --> 01:22:08.250
There's a lot of information about this.

01:22:08.250 --> 01:22:09.083
But you do want to make sure

01:22:09.083 --> 01:22:12.360
that you're getting
enough B6, B12, et cetera,

01:22:12.360 --> 01:22:15.480
such that you can keep
prolactin levels in check.

01:22:15.480 --> 01:22:18.900
And if you suspect that you
have a dopamine deficiency,

01:22:18.900 --> 01:22:19.830
please talk to your doctor

01:22:19.830 --> 01:22:21.930
and talk to them about
ways you might adjust

01:22:21.930 --> 01:22:25.200
that prolactin down and
thereby dopamine up.

01:22:25.200 --> 01:22:28.710
The other way to ensure that
dopamine levels stay high

01:22:28.710 --> 01:22:30.210
or put differently,

01:22:30.210 --> 01:22:32.400
that you don't quash whatever dopamine

01:22:32.400 --> 01:22:33.810
you have in your system

01:22:33.810 --> 01:22:36.690
is to really avoid bright
light exposure to your eyes

01:22:36.690 --> 01:22:38.820
between the hours of 10:00 PM and 4:00 AM

01:22:38.820 --> 01:22:40.050
or another way of putting this

01:22:40.050 --> 01:22:41.160
because I realize people sleep

01:22:41.160 --> 01:22:42.540
at different times, et cetera,

01:22:42.540 --> 01:22:45.090
is to avoid bright light
exposure to your eyes,

01:22:45.090 --> 01:22:47.670
not just blue light,
but all colors of light

01:22:47.670 --> 01:22:52.650
in phase three, that is 17
to 24 hours after waking up

01:22:52.650 --> 01:22:54.450
because that's really
when you should be asleep

01:22:54.450 --> 01:22:57.690
or trying to get asleep, if
you're having trouble sleeping.

01:22:57.690 --> 01:22:59.400
Work from Samer Hattar's lab,

01:22:59.400 --> 01:23:00.870
the director of the chronobiology unit

01:23:00.870 --> 01:23:03.000
at the National Institutes
of Mental Health,

01:23:03.000 --> 01:23:07.020
again, a previous Huberman
Lab Podcast guest,

01:23:07.020 --> 01:23:09.630
tell us that bright light exposure

01:23:09.630 --> 01:23:13.230
in phase three of your circadian cycle,

01:23:13.230 --> 01:23:15.570
17 to 24 hours after waking,

01:23:15.570 --> 01:23:18.540
can have dramatic effects
in reducing dopamine levels

01:23:18.540 --> 01:23:20.400
by way of activating a neural circuit

01:23:20.400 --> 01:23:22.407
involving something called the habenula.

01:23:22.407 --> 01:23:24.300
I don't want to get into
too many details right now,

01:23:24.300 --> 01:23:26.100
but really try and keep the lights dim

01:23:26.100 --> 01:23:27.240
in the middle of the night or off,

01:23:27.240 --> 01:23:29.160
if you can do that safely.

01:23:29.160 --> 01:23:30.480
It's really going to help

01:23:30.480 --> 01:23:32.850
if you're turning on your phone brightly,

01:23:32.850 --> 01:23:34.800
if you are turning on bright lights,

01:23:34.800 --> 01:23:37.350
it's not just going to
negatively impact melatonin,

01:23:37.350 --> 01:23:39.090
the hormone that helps
you fall and stay asleep.

01:23:39.090 --> 01:23:43.200
It's also going to negatively
impact dopamine levels,

01:23:43.200 --> 01:23:46.470
not just that night,
but the subsequent day.

01:23:46.470 --> 01:23:49.050
So that more or less
summarizes our coverage

01:23:49.050 --> 01:23:52.680
of ways to use behavior and
supplementation and nutrition

01:23:52.680 --> 01:23:53.910
to increase dopamine

01:23:53.910 --> 01:23:56.490
and dopamine receptor efficacy and number,

01:23:56.490 --> 01:23:58.590
and to keep sufficient amounts of dopamine

01:23:58.590 --> 01:24:02.850
in your system day-to-day for
motivation, mood and focus.

01:24:02.850 --> 01:24:04.470
And of course, keep in mind those things

01:24:04.470 --> 01:24:05.850
that can suppress dopamine,

01:24:05.850 --> 01:24:07.980
the bright light exposure,

01:24:07.980 --> 01:24:10.110
elevated prolactin, and so on.

01:24:10.110 --> 01:24:11.757
My hope is that by
understanding those tools

01:24:11.757 --> 01:24:12.690
and how they work

01:24:12.690 --> 01:24:15.480
and understanding that
dopamine does certain things

01:24:15.480 --> 01:24:16.560
and not others

01:24:16.560 --> 01:24:20.370
that you can assemble a
versatile kit of behaviors

01:24:20.370 --> 01:24:21.720
and other things that you can do

01:24:21.720 --> 01:24:23.820
in order to adjust your dopamine levels

01:24:23.820 --> 01:24:26.400
according to your particular goals.

01:24:26.400 --> 01:24:29.910
I want to just briefly
return to the fact, however,

01:24:29.910 --> 01:24:33.240
that all of that is
riding on that phase one,

01:24:33.240 --> 01:24:34.590
phase two background,

01:24:34.590 --> 01:24:38.310
meaning, it's probably going to
take less cold water exposure,

01:24:38.310 --> 01:24:41.190
or I should say less time
doing cold water exposure

01:24:41.190 --> 01:24:42.150
early in the day

01:24:42.150 --> 01:24:43.920
to get a big increase in dopamine

01:24:43.920 --> 01:24:45.630
than it would later in the day.

01:24:45.630 --> 01:24:48.270
Because later in the day, your
baseline levels of dopamine

01:24:48.270 --> 01:24:51.630
are lower and you've got
more serotonin circulating.

01:24:51.630 --> 01:24:54.330
That should make sense to you
now as to why that's the case.

01:24:54.330 --> 01:24:55.890
And does that mean

01:24:55.890 --> 01:24:58.470
that you should really modify
your protocols dramatically?

01:24:58.470 --> 01:25:01.260
Probably not, but you
might keep that in mind

01:25:01.260 --> 01:25:03.690
that if, for instance, you need
to be in a highly motivated,

01:25:03.690 --> 01:25:05.490
focused state in the late part of the day,

01:25:05.490 --> 01:25:06.570
for whatever reason,

01:25:06.570 --> 01:25:09.270
it might take a few or more of these tools

01:25:09.270 --> 01:25:11.490
in combination in order
to accomplish that.

01:25:11.490 --> 01:25:12.990
Whereas if you're somebody
who feels pretty good

01:25:12.990 --> 01:25:15.278
during the day, but you're
kind of lacking motivation

01:25:15.278 --> 01:25:16.530
and you want to increase dopamine levels

01:25:16.530 --> 01:25:18.270
and you don't yet need to,

01:25:18.270 --> 01:25:20.970
or want to resort to prescription
drugs or supplementation,

01:25:20.970 --> 01:25:23.310
well, then you might layer in
a couple behavioral protocols

01:25:23.310 --> 01:25:24.750
paying attention to of course,

01:25:24.750 --> 01:25:25.800
the things that you might be doing

01:25:25.800 --> 01:25:28.620
that would also potentially
suppress dopamine.

01:25:28.620 --> 01:25:30.810
So again, that kit of tools is designed

01:25:30.810 --> 01:25:33.600
for you to play with, if you choose,

01:25:33.600 --> 01:25:37.020
if it's safe for you to
apply them, then do that.

01:25:37.020 --> 01:25:38.790
Consider doing them individually,

01:25:38.790 --> 01:25:42.240
not trying to hit all the
tools all at once, right?

01:25:42.240 --> 01:25:44.430
I mean, why throw all those tools

01:25:44.430 --> 01:25:46.410
at your dopamine system at once?

01:25:46.410 --> 01:25:48.720
Better would be to have
those tools in your kit

01:25:48.720 --> 01:25:50.340
and be able to deploy them depending on

01:25:50.340 --> 01:25:51.450
whether or not you're on travel,

01:25:51.450 --> 01:25:53.910
whether or not you're
eating well or less well,

01:25:53.910 --> 01:25:56.100
whether or not you're
sleeping well or less well.

01:25:56.100 --> 01:25:57.360
That's highly individual

01:25:57.360 --> 01:26:00.090
and I'd like to think that in
having those tools in hand,

01:26:00.090 --> 01:26:02.520
you'll be able to adjust them
and apply them in the ways

01:26:02.520 --> 01:26:06.090
that allow you to access
the dopamine increases

01:26:06.090 --> 01:26:06.923
that you're after.

01:26:06.923 --> 01:26:08.850
So next I'd like to
talk about epinephrine,

01:26:08.850 --> 01:26:10.740
also called adrenaline.

01:26:10.740 --> 01:26:12.840
I want to point out that epinephrine

01:26:12.840 --> 01:26:15.600
is released both in
the brain and the body.

01:26:15.600 --> 01:26:17.730
In fact, there's a barrier
between brain and body

01:26:17.730 --> 01:26:19.680
that prevents the
epinephrine that's released

01:26:19.680 --> 01:26:21.390
from your adrenal glands,

01:26:21.390 --> 01:26:23.370
from crossing the blood brain barrier.

01:26:23.370 --> 01:26:25.320
So your brain has a separate site

01:26:25.320 --> 01:26:27.150
called the locus coeruleus.

01:26:27.150 --> 01:26:29.280
This is a collection of neurons
in the back of the brain

01:26:29.280 --> 01:26:30.810
that kind of sprinkler
the rest of the brain

01:26:30.810 --> 01:26:31.780
with epinephrine

01:26:32.700 --> 01:26:35.040
and essentially wakes up

01:26:35.040 --> 01:26:39.300
whatever neural circuits
happen to see, or I should say,

01:26:39.300 --> 01:26:41.790
wake up any circuits
where that epinephrine

01:26:41.790 --> 01:26:43.950
happens to arrive, right,

01:26:43.950 --> 01:26:46.620
and generally increase the
excitability of those networks.

01:26:46.620 --> 01:26:50.040
That's why we say
epinephrine increases energy.

01:26:50.040 --> 01:26:51.690
I'm not talking about caloric energy,

01:26:51.690 --> 01:26:53.730
although that's distantly related to this,

01:26:53.730 --> 01:26:57.210
but really energy and the desire to move,

01:26:57.210 --> 01:26:58.890
the feeling that we can think,

01:26:58.890 --> 01:27:00.540
the feeling that we can be alert.

01:27:00.540 --> 01:27:01.947
In fact, if you look at somebody

01:27:01.947 --> 01:27:04.320
and their eyelids are wide open,

01:27:04.320 --> 01:27:05.153
in large part,

01:27:05.153 --> 01:27:07.140
that's because of a lot of
adrenaline in their system.

01:27:07.140 --> 01:27:08.640
If their pupils are really big

01:27:08.640 --> 01:27:10.560
and their eyes are really wide open,

01:27:10.560 --> 01:27:11.670
in general, that means they have

01:27:11.670 --> 01:27:13.410
a lot of epinephrine circulating there,

01:27:13.410 --> 01:27:15.480
whereas when we're tired
and we're kind of hood-eyed

01:27:15.480 --> 01:27:16.620
and we're sort of sleepy,

01:27:16.620 --> 01:27:18.270
or our pupils are really small,

01:27:18.270 --> 01:27:20.970
in general, that's because
levels of epinephrine

01:27:20.970 --> 01:27:23.370
and also dopamine, remember
they work together,

01:27:23.370 --> 01:27:26.340
levels of epinephrine and
dopamine tend to be lower.

01:27:26.340 --> 01:27:28.590
This is also why when
people take any drug,

01:27:28.590 --> 01:27:30.480
like again, not recommending this,

01:27:30.480 --> 01:27:32.850
amphetamine or cocaine or any stimulant,

01:27:32.850 --> 01:27:34.380
their pupils tend to be huge.

01:27:34.380 --> 01:27:36.780
Their eyes tend to be wide open.

01:27:36.780 --> 01:27:38.760
They don't blink very often.

01:27:38.760 --> 01:27:42.300
And the opposite is true
when people take sedatives.

01:27:42.300 --> 01:27:43.680
So it all starts to make sense

01:27:43.680 --> 01:27:46.710
when you think about the
basic actions of these things.

01:27:46.710 --> 01:27:51.270
For many people, increasing
adrenaline or epinephrine

01:27:51.270 --> 01:27:52.560
might seem like a crazy idea.

01:27:52.560 --> 01:27:55.320
Most people probably associate
this molecule with stress

01:27:55.320 --> 01:27:56.910
and then would like to be less stressed.

01:27:56.910 --> 01:27:58.770
And we've done entire
episodes about stress,

01:27:58.770 --> 01:28:00.840
how to master stress,
how to leverage stress,

01:28:00.840 --> 01:28:01.830
how to conquer stress.

01:28:01.830 --> 01:28:03.690
There are a lot of great tools to do that

01:28:03.690 --> 01:28:05.610
that are behavioral,
supplementation-based.

01:28:05.610 --> 01:28:08.790
Please see the episode on
Mastering Stress for those tools,

01:28:08.790 --> 01:28:11.880
but there are people, including me,

01:28:11.880 --> 01:28:14.430
that want to increase
our levels of epinephrine

01:28:14.430 --> 01:28:16.200
at least early in the day.

01:28:16.200 --> 01:28:17.940
I'm somebody who wakes up rather slowly.

01:28:17.940 --> 01:28:19.200
In fact, right after waking up,

01:28:19.200 --> 01:28:21.090
I rarely want to bounce out of bed.

01:28:21.090 --> 01:28:22.710
I try and push myself to do that.

01:28:22.710 --> 01:28:25.260
I'm always impressed by
these chakra-willy types

01:28:25.260 --> 01:28:29.070
that are up at 4:30 or up at
five and already into action.

01:28:29.070 --> 01:28:31.230
I tend to be kind of thinking about

01:28:31.230 --> 01:28:34.710
thinking about maybe being
in action early in the day,

01:28:34.710 --> 01:28:37.920
but I try and push myself
to get into action,

01:28:37.920 --> 01:28:40.470
which itself can increase epinephrine.

01:28:40.470 --> 01:28:43.020
I should mention that
any physical activity,

01:28:43.020 --> 01:28:44.400
any physical activity,

01:28:44.400 --> 01:28:47.580
walking, running, weight
lifting, swimming,

01:28:47.580 --> 01:28:48.930
even talking for that matter

01:28:48.930 --> 01:28:51.240
is going to increase
levels of epinephrine.

01:28:51.240 --> 01:28:54.210
Locus coeruleus is a brain structure

01:28:54.210 --> 01:28:56.970
that is tightly coupled with behaviors

01:28:56.970 --> 01:28:58.530
in a bidirectional way.

01:28:58.530 --> 01:29:01.200
That is, when you are in action,

01:29:01.200 --> 01:29:03.360
you increase the amount
of epinephrine released

01:29:03.360 --> 01:29:04.230
from locus coeruleus.

01:29:04.230 --> 01:29:05.373
You wake up the brain.

01:29:06.360 --> 01:29:09.720
And conversely, when
locus coeruleus is active,

01:29:09.720 --> 01:29:11.580
the brain wakes up, so it's reciprocal.

01:29:11.580 --> 01:29:13.290
It goes both directions.

01:29:13.290 --> 01:29:16.260
So I saw a funny tweet
actually earlier today,

01:29:16.260 --> 01:29:17.310
it was something like

01:29:18.270 --> 01:29:20.280
going to the gym gives you energy,

01:29:20.280 --> 01:29:21.990
but you need energy to go to the gym.

01:29:21.990 --> 01:29:24.150
Sounds like a pyramid scheme to me,

01:29:24.150 --> 01:29:25.590
which made me chuckle.

01:29:25.590 --> 01:29:28.260
But of course overlooks the fact that,

01:29:28.260 --> 01:29:29.910
indeed, if you have energy,

01:29:29.910 --> 01:29:31.560
you are more likely to be willing

01:29:31.560 --> 01:29:33.180
to get into physical movement

01:29:33.180 --> 01:29:35.820
or cognitive movement and thinking hard

01:29:35.820 --> 01:29:37.920
or thinking a lot about something.

01:29:37.920 --> 01:29:42.210
But also it is absolutely
scientifically proven

01:29:42.210 --> 01:29:44.280
that being in action

01:29:44.280 --> 01:29:46.230
increases levels of epinephrine.

01:29:46.230 --> 01:29:49.050
This is why exercising early in the day

01:29:49.050 --> 01:29:51.690
gives you more energy for rest of day.

01:29:51.690 --> 01:29:53.580
You still might experience
a little bit of a crash

01:29:53.580 --> 01:29:54.630
in the afternoon,

01:29:54.630 --> 01:29:56.850
especially if you're
getting up extra early,

01:29:56.850 --> 01:29:59.160
or if you're drinking
caffeine too close to waking.

01:29:59.160 --> 01:30:00.360
I've talked about this before.

01:30:00.360 --> 01:30:03.270
If you drink too much
caffeine close to waking,

01:30:03.270 --> 01:30:04.770
you're going to have an afternoon crash.

01:30:04.770 --> 01:30:06.900
Better to push that caffeine intake out

01:30:06.900 --> 01:30:09.150
about 90 to 120 minutes after waking.

01:30:09.150 --> 01:30:11.310
I know this is really
painful for certain people,

01:30:11.310 --> 01:30:15.180
but caffeine does increase epinephrine.

01:30:15.180 --> 01:30:17.790
Caffeine does other things
to limit sleepiness.

01:30:17.790 --> 01:30:20.970
And by pushing it out 90 to
120 minutes after waking,

01:30:20.970 --> 01:30:25.470
you will avoid the afternoon
crash to a large degree.

01:30:25.470 --> 01:30:27.870
And if you get up and you exercise

01:30:27.870 --> 01:30:29.730
or even do any movement of any kind,

01:30:29.730 --> 01:30:32.850
a hundred jumping jacks or a
walk, if you can't do that,

01:30:32.850 --> 01:30:35.010
anything like that will increase

01:30:35.010 --> 01:30:37.530
the total amount of
epinephrine that you secrete

01:30:37.530 --> 01:30:39.060
into your bloodstream and in your brain

01:30:39.060 --> 01:30:42.030
and will get you more energy,

01:30:42.030 --> 01:30:43.800
not just in that moment,
but throughout the day.

01:30:43.800 --> 01:30:44.730
So keep that in mind.

01:30:44.730 --> 01:30:47.610
Exercise does indeed give you energy.

01:30:47.610 --> 01:30:48.990
It burns caloric energy,

01:30:48.990 --> 01:30:50.790
but it gives you neural energy

01:30:50.790 --> 01:30:53.220
by way of increasing
epinephrine transmission

01:30:53.220 --> 01:30:54.720
from locus coeruleus.

01:30:54.720 --> 01:30:58.440
And presumably if the
exercise is intense enough,

01:30:58.440 --> 01:31:00.510
adrenaline, epinephrine release,

01:31:00.510 --> 01:31:02.910
from the adrenals within
your body, as well.

01:31:02.910 --> 01:31:05.160
So we have exercise and we have caffeine

01:31:05.160 --> 01:31:07.230
as potent tools for increasing epinephrine

01:31:07.230 --> 01:31:09.150
and thereby, energy.

01:31:09.150 --> 01:31:11.580
Another potent tool
that's purely behavioral,

01:31:11.580 --> 01:31:12.870
but is known to work,

01:31:12.870 --> 01:31:14.850
based on excellent studies in humans.

01:31:14.850 --> 01:31:16.740
And actually my laboratory has been doing

01:31:16.740 --> 01:31:17.760
similar types of studies

01:31:17.760 --> 01:31:20.100
that are soon to be published, we hope,

01:31:20.100 --> 01:31:22.860
is so-called cyclic hyperventilation.

01:31:22.860 --> 01:31:25.320
Some of you may be familiar
with Wim Hof breathing.

01:31:25.320 --> 01:31:28.170
There's also Tummo breathing,
which is very similar,

01:31:28.170 --> 01:31:29.940
Kundalini breathing.

01:31:29.940 --> 01:31:31.470
All of those styles of breathing

01:31:31.470 --> 01:31:33.810
involve cyclic hyperventilation,

01:31:33.810 --> 01:31:38.220
deep inhales, and either passive
exhales or active exhales,

01:31:38.220 --> 01:31:40.320
but repeating, inhale,
exhale, inhale, exhale,

01:31:40.320 --> 01:31:42.690
in a very deep and repetitive way.

01:31:42.690 --> 01:31:44.640
If you were to do that right now,

01:31:44.640 --> 01:31:46.590
doesn't matter if you do it
through your nose or mouth,

01:31:46.590 --> 01:31:48.900
although ideally you would do
the inhale through your nose

01:31:48.900 --> 01:31:49.860
and the exhale through your mouth,

01:31:49.860 --> 01:31:52.920
if you did that for 25 repetitions,

01:31:52.920 --> 01:31:56.010
25 inhales and exhales,
you would feel more alert.

01:31:56.010 --> 01:31:57.720
You'd also feel more warm, why?

01:31:57.720 --> 01:32:00.930
Because you increased
epinephrine, adrenaline release

01:32:00.930 --> 01:32:02.040
in the brain and body.

01:32:02.040 --> 01:32:04.560
It works the first time
and it works every time

01:32:04.560 --> 01:32:07.410
to increase epinephrine
and thereby energy.

01:32:07.410 --> 01:32:10.200
And in fact, there are protocols

01:32:10.200 --> 01:32:11.280
and great scientific studies

01:32:11.280 --> 01:32:13.530
of using cyclic hyperventilation

01:32:13.530 --> 01:32:16.890
for periods of minutes, if not longer,

01:32:16.890 --> 01:32:20.610
where for instance, you would
do 25 big inhales and exhales

01:32:20.610 --> 01:32:22.950
followed by a brief breath
hold with your lungs empty,

01:32:22.950 --> 01:32:25.950
then repeat 25, then brief breath hold,

01:32:25.950 --> 01:32:28.260
excuse me, exhale, hold your lungs empty.

01:32:28.260 --> 01:32:30.810
And then repeat again for
a third round, if you like.

01:32:30.810 --> 01:32:33.240
If you do that over and over,
you're going to be very alert.

01:32:33.240 --> 01:32:34.260
You're going to have more energy.

01:32:34.260 --> 01:32:35.093
You're going to feel like

01:32:35.093 --> 01:32:36.570
you want to move around a lot more.

01:32:36.570 --> 01:32:38.310
In fact, you might even feel agitated.

01:32:38.310 --> 01:32:40.200
So people with a lot of anxiety

01:32:40.200 --> 01:32:42.180
or prone to panic attack
might want to be cautious

01:32:42.180 --> 01:32:45.270
in how they train and embark
on that type of breathing,

01:32:45.270 --> 01:32:46.830
might want to approach it
a little more carefully

01:32:46.830 --> 01:32:48.060
or avoid it altogether.

01:32:48.060 --> 01:32:48.990
But for most people,

01:32:48.990 --> 01:32:50.700
cyclic hyperventilation is simply going to

01:32:50.700 --> 01:32:53.700
get you more energized and
feeling like you want to move,

01:32:53.700 --> 01:32:55.440
feeling like you can think more clearly,

01:32:55.440 --> 01:32:57.150
and you'll be more wide-eyed and alert

01:32:57.150 --> 01:32:59.850
because you are releasing adrenaline.

01:32:59.850 --> 01:33:01.440
And the cold water exposure protocol

01:33:01.440 --> 01:33:02.430
that I talked about earlier

01:33:02.430 --> 01:33:04.590
and that's covered in our episode on cold

01:33:04.590 --> 01:33:06.660
and in the newsletter on cold.

01:33:06.660 --> 01:33:08.610
Well that, as I mentioned earlier,

01:33:08.610 --> 01:33:11.940
potently increases dopamine,
but also epinephrine.

01:33:11.940 --> 01:33:13.230
So that's another terrific tool,

01:33:13.230 --> 01:33:16.740
whether or not it's applied by
cold shower or cold immersion

01:33:16.740 --> 01:33:19.110
or some other thing like cryo,

01:33:19.110 --> 01:33:20.850
that is going to make you more alert

01:33:20.850 --> 01:33:23.100
because it releases adrenaline.

01:33:23.100 --> 01:33:25.560
Now we can't really say
that there are foods

01:33:25.560 --> 01:33:27.270
to increase epinephrine.

01:33:27.270 --> 01:33:30.780
Rather, there are foods that
include a lot of tyrosine

01:33:30.780 --> 01:33:32.700
that will increase dopamine.

01:33:32.700 --> 01:33:34.530
And remember, dopamine is the molecule

01:33:34.530 --> 01:33:37.230
from which epinephrine is synthesized.

01:33:37.230 --> 01:33:40.110
So we can't really point
to a particular food

01:33:40.110 --> 01:33:42.120
or categories of food for
increasing epinephrine.

01:33:42.120 --> 01:33:44.520
I think caffeine and things like it

01:33:44.520 --> 01:33:46.260
will increase epinephrine.

01:33:46.260 --> 01:33:48.270
There are of course prescription drugs

01:33:48.270 --> 01:33:50.280
that will increase epinephrine.

01:33:50.280 --> 01:33:51.113
And of course,

01:33:51.113 --> 01:33:53.580
there are all sorts of
so-called beta blockers

01:33:53.580 --> 01:33:55.950
that will block the
receptors for epinephrine

01:33:55.950 --> 01:33:58.320
to make you feel calm for public speaking

01:33:58.320 --> 01:34:00.330
or for various heart
conditions, et cetera.

01:34:00.330 --> 01:34:02.280
That's really the domain of physicians

01:34:02.280 --> 01:34:05.520
and should really be worked
out with your cardiologist,

01:34:05.520 --> 01:34:06.900
with a physician et cetera.

01:34:06.900 --> 01:34:09.720
I think the tools of exercise

01:34:09.720 --> 01:34:13.950
and should you want very
potent increases in adrenaline,

01:34:13.950 --> 01:34:15.720
high intensity exercise,

01:34:15.720 --> 01:34:19.260
as well as the tools of
caffeine, cyclic hyperventilation

01:34:19.260 --> 01:34:20.820
and deliberate cold exposure,

01:34:20.820 --> 01:34:24.630
really combine to give
you a nice little kit,

01:34:24.630 --> 01:34:26.430
I would say a versatile kit,

01:34:26.430 --> 01:34:28.140
of ways to increase epinephrine

01:34:28.140 --> 01:34:30.840
for sake of having more
physical and mental energy.

01:34:30.840 --> 01:34:33.570
So next is the
neuromodulator acetylcholine.

01:34:33.570 --> 01:34:35.250
And as I mentioned earlier,

01:34:35.250 --> 01:34:38.970
acetylcholine is associated
with states of focus

01:34:38.970 --> 01:34:40.170
and those states of focus

01:34:40.170 --> 01:34:42.480
can be high energy states of focus.

01:34:42.480 --> 01:34:45.450
So the ones that are accompanied by

01:34:45.450 --> 01:34:47.880
high levels of dopamine and epinephrine

01:34:47.880 --> 01:34:49.980
and where we're really excited about

01:34:49.980 --> 01:34:51.960
and really lasered in on something,

01:34:51.960 --> 01:34:56.070
or they can be the calmer,
more relaxed states of focus

01:34:56.070 --> 01:34:58.860
like reading a book or practicing music

01:34:58.860 --> 01:35:01.140
or listening very carefully to somebody

01:35:01.140 --> 01:35:03.390
in a way that's relaxed and calm.

01:35:03.390 --> 01:35:04.380
And yet nonetheless,

01:35:04.380 --> 01:35:07.020
where we have a narrow cognitive

01:35:07.020 --> 01:35:10.170
and typically a narrow visual aperture,

01:35:10.170 --> 01:35:12.900
and typically also a
narrow auditory aperture.

01:35:12.900 --> 01:35:15.720
That is our auditory system
and our visual system

01:35:15.720 --> 01:35:17.880
and our thinking can be very broad.

01:35:17.880 --> 01:35:19.590
It can be all over the place.

01:35:19.590 --> 01:35:21.600
Or it can be very narrow.

01:35:21.600 --> 01:35:23.790
And it can be very focused.

01:35:23.790 --> 01:35:27.000
Acetylcholine is released from
two major sites in the brain,

01:35:27.000 --> 01:35:29.550
nucleus basalis, which is in the forebrain

01:35:29.550 --> 01:35:32.520
and extends connections out
to many different brain areas

01:35:32.520 --> 01:35:36.660
to offer the opportunity to
release acetylcholine locally

01:35:36.660 --> 01:35:39.180
and more or less in a chemical way,

01:35:39.180 --> 01:35:42.690
highlight those particular
neurons and synapses

01:35:42.690 --> 01:35:45.690
for strengthening, for plasticity later.

01:35:45.690 --> 01:35:48.990
And it is released from sites
in the back of the brain

01:35:48.990 --> 01:35:51.330
in a way that can increase

01:35:51.330 --> 01:35:54.930
the so-called fidelity
of information coming in

01:35:54.930 --> 01:35:57.180
through our eyes, our
ears, our nose, et cetera.

01:35:57.180 --> 01:35:58.260
What do I mean by fidelity?

01:35:58.260 --> 01:35:59.970
Well, we are constantly being bombarded

01:35:59.970 --> 01:36:03.420
with sensory information through
all of our various senses

01:36:03.420 --> 01:36:05.760
and acetylcholine released from this area

01:36:05.760 --> 01:36:06.930
in the back of the brain,

01:36:06.930 --> 01:36:10.860
has the ability to increase
the extent to which

01:36:10.860 --> 01:36:12.480
say, visual information

01:36:12.480 --> 01:36:15.330
or just visual and auditory information

01:36:15.330 --> 01:36:17.340
would make it through to our consciousness

01:36:17.340 --> 01:36:19.680
whereas all the other types
of sensory information

01:36:19.680 --> 01:36:22.710
that are coming in are filtered out.

01:36:22.710 --> 01:36:25.920
So your brain, because it's
taking in all this information

01:36:25.920 --> 01:36:27.840
needs to decide what to pay attention to.

01:36:27.840 --> 01:36:29.790
And in this way, we can
say that acetylcholine

01:36:29.790 --> 01:36:32.760
has a lot to do, not just
with "focus", in air quotes,

01:36:32.760 --> 01:36:34.380
but literally attention,

01:36:34.380 --> 01:36:38.460
which neural signals become
relevant to our consciousness.

01:36:38.460 --> 01:36:40.080
There's a whole discussion to be had there

01:36:40.080 --> 01:36:41.460
and we don't have time for that.

01:36:41.460 --> 01:36:43.860
Rather, I'd like to focus
on what are the tools

01:36:43.860 --> 01:36:45.090
that one can use

01:36:45.090 --> 01:36:48.600
to maintain healthy
baselines of acetylcholine

01:36:48.600 --> 01:36:52.560
and increase acetylcholine
for sake of learning

01:36:52.560 --> 01:36:55.560
any type of information,
physical, cognitive, or otherwise.

01:36:55.560 --> 01:36:57.360
Now it turns out there
have been a lot of studies,

01:36:57.360 --> 01:36:59.130
including many quality
peer reviewed studies

01:36:59.130 --> 01:37:00.210
carried out in humans,

01:37:00.210 --> 01:37:01.680
looking at what happens

01:37:01.680 --> 01:37:05.400
when you increase acetylcholine
levels in the brain

01:37:05.400 --> 01:37:08.010
and you accompany that
with the attempt to learn.

01:37:08.010 --> 01:37:10.440
And what you find almost always

01:37:10.440 --> 01:37:13.680
is that people experience increased focus,

01:37:13.680 --> 01:37:16.710
that when measured the neuronal responses

01:37:16.710 --> 01:37:18.270
become more specific,

01:37:18.270 --> 01:37:20.280
so less broad scale activity in the brain

01:37:20.280 --> 01:37:23.190
and more specific neural circuit activity

01:37:23.190 --> 01:37:27.540
and that this triggers immediate
and long-lasting changes

01:37:27.540 --> 01:37:28.950
in the way those circuits work,

01:37:28.950 --> 01:37:31.230
even when acetylcholine
is not being deployed,

01:37:31.230 --> 01:37:34.380
so-called neuroplasticity,
the circuits literally change.

01:37:34.380 --> 01:37:35.370
So this is great.

01:37:35.370 --> 01:37:37.770
The work of Michael Silver at Berkeley,

01:37:37.770 --> 01:37:40.380
the work of Mike Merzenich at UCSF,

01:37:40.380 --> 01:37:42.390
the work of Michael Kilgard down in Texas,

01:37:42.390 --> 01:37:43.470
all of those laboratories

01:37:43.470 --> 01:37:44.880
see this again and again, and again.

01:37:44.880 --> 01:37:47.550
Increase acetylcholine
before and during learning.

01:37:47.550 --> 01:37:49.500
And there's a much higher probability

01:37:49.500 --> 01:37:50.827
that the learning will quote, unquote,

01:37:50.827 --> 01:37:53.130
"sink in", that the
information will be retained

01:37:53.130 --> 01:37:55.410
because those neural circuits change.

01:37:55.410 --> 01:37:59.100
Now ways to increase
acetylcholine in a potent way

01:37:59.100 --> 01:38:03.480
include again, nutrition
and supplementation.

01:38:03.480 --> 01:38:07.020
It is important to have
baseline levels of acetylcholine

01:38:07.020 --> 01:38:08.670
be sufficiently high, as well.

01:38:08.670 --> 01:38:10.650
And for that really the ideal situation

01:38:10.650 --> 01:38:13.440
is to regularly ingest
foods that provide enough

01:38:13.440 --> 01:38:16.410
of the precursors for
acetylcholine to be made.

01:38:16.410 --> 01:38:18.930
If you go online and
you were to do a search

01:38:18.930 --> 01:38:21.900
of which foods contain a lot of choline,

01:38:21.900 --> 01:38:25.230
which is related to the
synthesis of acetylcholine,

01:38:25.230 --> 01:38:27.210
you would get some
interesting information back.

01:38:27.210 --> 01:38:29.160
For instance, beef liver

01:38:29.160 --> 01:38:31.800
is the most potent source of choline.

01:38:31.800 --> 01:38:34.620
I know nowadays there's kind
of a growing micro trend

01:38:34.620 --> 01:38:36.420
if you will, of ingesting beef liver,

01:38:36.420 --> 01:38:38.370
even raw liver, which to be honest,

01:38:38.370 --> 01:38:40.890
the thought of ingesting
raw liver of any kind

01:38:40.890 --> 01:38:42.420
activates my area postrema,

01:38:42.420 --> 01:38:44.640
which is the area of the
brain that triggers nausea.

01:38:44.640 --> 01:38:46.260
In fact, I'm starting to salivate a bit,

01:38:46.260 --> 01:38:47.093
not because I'm hungry,

01:38:47.093 --> 01:38:50.010
but I think the whole
concept makes me ill.

01:38:50.010 --> 01:38:51.820
Nonetheless, cooked liver

01:38:52.920 --> 01:38:54.600
or raw liver for that matter,

01:38:54.600 --> 01:38:57.960
or liver of any kind seems
to contain a lot of choline.

01:38:57.960 --> 01:38:59.940
I realize most people,

01:38:59.940 --> 01:39:02.250
most people are not
going to be running out

01:39:02.250 --> 01:39:04.830
and ingesting large amounts of beef liver.

01:39:04.830 --> 01:39:06.570
Eggs contain a lot of choline.

01:39:06.570 --> 01:39:07.590
Beef contains choline.

01:39:07.590 --> 01:39:08.760
Soybeans contain choline.

01:39:08.760 --> 01:39:11.310
So there are vegan or non-meat sources,

01:39:11.310 --> 01:39:13.890
chicken, fish, mushrooms, kidney beans,

01:39:13.890 --> 01:39:15.897
these sorts of things
contain a lot of choline

01:39:15.897 --> 01:39:17.850
and there are other vegetables
that contain choline.

01:39:17.850 --> 01:39:20.220
So depending on your dietary
preferences and needs,

01:39:20.220 --> 01:39:23.340
you can select certain foods to ingest,

01:39:23.340 --> 01:39:24.780
to get enough choline to synthesize

01:39:24.780 --> 01:39:27.453
enough baseline acetyl choline.

01:39:28.320 --> 01:39:29.940
In the realm of supplementation,

01:39:29.940 --> 01:39:31.890
there are some excellent tools

01:39:31.890 --> 01:39:33.990
for increasing acetylcholine

01:39:33.990 --> 01:39:35.730
in the acute short-term,

01:39:35.730 --> 01:39:38.610
meaning over the course
of about 30 minutes

01:39:38.610 --> 01:39:41.010
out to about two hours
or maybe even four hours.

01:39:41.010 --> 01:39:44.070
And the number of different
molecules that can do that,

01:39:44.070 --> 01:39:45.660
that are available without a prescription,

01:39:45.660 --> 01:39:48.360
at least in the US, is pretty vast.

01:39:48.360 --> 01:39:51.120
The most common of those
molecules is actually nicotine.

01:39:51.120 --> 01:39:53.550
Nicotinic acetylcholine receptors

01:39:53.550 --> 01:39:55.830
are abundant throughout
the body and brain.

01:39:55.830 --> 01:39:57.810
They're in various brain circuits.

01:39:57.810 --> 01:40:01.230
They are on muscle and yes,

01:40:01.230 --> 01:40:03.750
smoking nicotine either
by vaping or cigarette

01:40:03.750 --> 01:40:07.740
will activate those nicotinic receptors.

01:40:07.740 --> 01:40:10.230
But of course, smoking
is a terrible thing.

01:40:10.230 --> 01:40:12.750
It will also activate
things like lung cancer.

01:40:12.750 --> 01:40:14.190
So I definitely don't recommend that.

01:40:14.190 --> 01:40:17.040
It also activates addiction
because of the ways

01:40:17.040 --> 01:40:20.100
that it triggers activation
of the dopamine circuit.

01:40:20.100 --> 01:40:24.120
So I think that triggering activation

01:40:24.120 --> 01:40:27.270
of acetylcholine-related
pathways by ingesting nicotine

01:40:27.270 --> 01:40:31.320
by way of inhalants is
generally a bad idea.

01:40:31.320 --> 01:40:34.770
However, some people will chew Nicorette

01:40:34.770 --> 01:40:37.800
or other nicotine type gums.

01:40:37.800 --> 01:40:38.633
I've never done that,

01:40:38.633 --> 01:40:40.530
but I have friends who
actually rely on that.

01:40:40.530 --> 01:40:42.780
These are typically former smokers

01:40:42.780 --> 01:40:44.190
that are trying not to smoke,

01:40:44.190 --> 01:40:47.490
but still want to get some
of the focus enhancement

01:40:47.490 --> 01:40:49.380
that they experience from nicotine.

01:40:49.380 --> 01:40:51.330
Some people are very
sensitive to nicotine,

01:40:51.330 --> 01:40:52.320
and this is important.

01:40:52.320 --> 01:40:54.990
Some people are very sensitive
to ingested nicotine.

01:40:54.990 --> 01:40:58.350
So nowadays there are
nicotine-dipped toothpicks,

01:40:58.350 --> 01:40:59.760
there of course, is nicotine gum

01:40:59.760 --> 01:41:00.990
and other sources of nicotine.

01:41:00.990 --> 01:41:02.760
Some people can take that and feel fine.

01:41:02.760 --> 01:41:05.760
Some people take it and
feel absolutely terrible.

01:41:05.760 --> 01:41:08.760
I confess, I've never
actually tried nicotine

01:41:08.760 --> 01:41:12.060
in any of those forms so I
don't know how they work for me,

01:41:12.060 --> 01:41:14.520
but some people do use them
as cognitive enhancers.

01:41:14.520 --> 01:41:17.910
In fact, I know one Nobel
prize winning neuroscientist,

01:41:17.910 --> 01:41:19.590
who's quite well known in our field

01:41:19.590 --> 01:41:21.600
for chewing Nicorette all day long.

01:41:21.600 --> 01:41:23.700
He insists that it really
helps him with his focus

01:41:23.700 --> 01:41:25.920
and he is exceedingly
smart and productive,

01:41:25.920 --> 01:41:28.080
although I'm sure there
are other reasons for that.

01:41:28.080 --> 01:41:31.140
Supplements that I have used and do use

01:41:31.140 --> 01:41:33.450
for increasing acetylcholine

01:41:33.450 --> 01:41:38.010
are things like Alpha GPC or Huperzinene.

01:41:38.010 --> 01:41:39.810
Alpha GPC

01:41:39.810 --> 01:41:42.360
is in the choline pathway,

01:41:42.360 --> 01:41:45.030
such that more
acetylcholine is synthesized

01:41:45.030 --> 01:41:46.170
after you ingest it.

01:41:46.170 --> 01:41:48.870
That's the general logic or
framework of how it works.

01:41:48.870 --> 01:41:52.440
Whereas Huperzinene is mainly
in the enzymatic pathway.

01:41:52.440 --> 01:41:55.740
It tends to adjust how much
acetylcholine is broken down

01:41:55.740 --> 01:41:59.640
and lead to net increases
in acetylcholine.

01:41:59.640 --> 01:42:02.730
I will often take 300
milligrams of Alpha GPC

01:42:02.730 --> 01:42:06.420
prior to workouts or prior
to cognitive work bouts.

01:42:06.420 --> 01:42:09.450
But when I say often, I
tend to do this anywhere

01:42:09.450 --> 01:42:13.530
from three to four times a
week, typically not every day.

01:42:13.530 --> 01:42:14.670
Although there are people

01:42:14.670 --> 01:42:15.630
including people who are trying

01:42:15.630 --> 01:42:17.850
to offset age-related cognitive decline,

01:42:17.850 --> 01:42:20.730
that will take 300 milligrams of Alpha GPC

01:42:20.730 --> 01:42:22.680
three times a day, every day,

01:42:22.680 --> 01:42:24.660
which closely mimic some of the studies

01:42:24.660 --> 01:42:25.890
that have been done on humans,

01:42:25.890 --> 01:42:28.800
looking at offsetting
age-related cognitive decline,

01:42:28.800 --> 01:42:31.470
using things like Alpha GPC.

01:42:31.470 --> 01:42:34.260
I should point out that there
have been a few studies,

01:42:34.260 --> 01:42:35.640
a few, not many,

01:42:35.640 --> 01:42:38.490
but these studies emphasize that people

01:42:38.490 --> 01:42:41.520
who take a lot of Alpha
GPC chronically over time

01:42:41.520 --> 01:42:44.070
may be at increased risk for stroke.

01:42:44.070 --> 01:42:46.410
I think the data are still out on that

01:42:46.410 --> 01:42:48.000
and we need more data.

01:42:48.000 --> 01:42:49.680
But for me, in terms of thinking

01:42:49.680 --> 01:42:51.420
about the risk benefit profiles,

01:42:51.420 --> 01:42:53.910
taking 300 milligrams of Alpha GPC,

01:42:53.910 --> 01:42:56.040
most certainly does increase
my ability to focus.

01:42:56.040 --> 01:42:56.873
I've noticed that.

01:42:56.873 --> 01:42:58.587
I tend to take it alongside caffeine

01:42:58.587 --> 01:43:00.150
and phenylethylamine.

01:43:00.150 --> 01:43:01.800
So I take that in combination

01:43:01.800 --> 01:43:03.480
either before workouts or workbouts,

01:43:03.480 --> 01:43:04.830
really sharpens my focus.

01:43:04.830 --> 01:43:07.470
And again, I'm doing that three,
maybe four times per week.

01:43:07.470 --> 01:43:10.020
And I'm careful to do that
in the early part of the day

01:43:10.020 --> 01:43:12.300
so that it does not disrupt my sleep.

01:43:12.300 --> 01:43:13.890
Although I have taken Alpha GPC

01:43:13.890 --> 01:43:15.210
in the second half of the day,

01:43:15.210 --> 01:43:17.340
and I had no trouble sleeping at all.

01:43:17.340 --> 01:43:20.820
I don't know what the exact
half life is of the given form

01:43:20.820 --> 01:43:22.500
that's typically in supplementation.

01:43:22.500 --> 01:43:24.540
It's actually hard to
get that information.

01:43:24.540 --> 01:43:27.960
But typically the focus
effects wear off after

01:43:27.960 --> 01:43:30.360
about two, maybe four hours maximum.

01:43:30.360 --> 01:43:31.680
Now one thing that I don't think

01:43:31.680 --> 01:43:33.180
has ever been discussed before,

01:43:33.180 --> 01:43:34.830
certainly not on this podcast

01:43:34.830 --> 01:43:38.280
is that if you take Alpha GPC,

01:43:38.280 --> 01:43:39.600
even semi-regularly,

01:43:39.600 --> 01:43:42.570
you may notice that a particular feature

01:43:42.570 --> 01:43:45.690
of your blood work will
increase and that's TMAO,

01:43:45.690 --> 01:43:47.520
which is sometimes associated

01:43:47.520 --> 01:43:50.250
with increased cardiovascular risk.

01:43:50.250 --> 01:43:53.910
This may, again, may relate
to some of the potential risk

01:43:53.910 --> 01:43:58.020
of very high levels of Alpha
GPC ingestion over many years,

01:43:58.020 --> 01:43:59.730
increasing stroke risk.

01:43:59.730 --> 01:44:01.132
Again, those studies looked at people

01:44:01.132 --> 01:44:03.060
who've been taking it for up to a decade,

01:44:03.060 --> 01:44:04.680
but in any case,

01:44:04.680 --> 01:44:07.770
one way to prevent the increase in TMAO,

01:44:07.770 --> 01:44:10.440
if you're taking Alpha GPC at all

01:44:10.440 --> 01:44:13.320
is to take 600 milligrams of garlic

01:44:13.320 --> 01:44:15.870
because it contains
something called allicin.

01:44:15.870 --> 01:44:18.360
This was a trick that was handed off to me

01:44:18.360 --> 01:44:19.830
by Dr. Kyle Gillette,

01:44:19.830 --> 01:44:21.990
who again, was a guest on
this podcast some time ago,

01:44:21.990 --> 01:44:23.880
talking about hormones and hormone health.

01:44:23.880 --> 01:44:27.240
Turns out that ingestion of
600 milligrams of allicin

01:44:27.240 --> 01:44:30.330
alongside or even just
same day as Alpha GPC

01:44:30.330 --> 01:44:33.060
can really clamp those TMAO levels

01:44:33.060 --> 01:44:35.520
that would otherwise increase
if you're taking Alpha GPC.

01:44:35.520 --> 01:44:37.170
And indeed I've done the blood work

01:44:37.170 --> 01:44:38.970
and that turns out to be the case.

01:44:38.970 --> 01:44:40.320
I saw a spike in TMAO.

01:44:40.320 --> 01:44:43.230
I started taking 600 milligrams of garlic

01:44:43.230 --> 01:44:45.240
and those TMAO levels came down.

01:44:45.240 --> 01:44:47.160
And last as it relates to acetylcholine,

01:44:47.160 --> 01:44:49.110
but certainly not least,

01:44:49.110 --> 01:44:51.750
just as acetylcholine can increase focus,

01:44:51.750 --> 01:44:54.480
focus can increase acetylcholine.

01:44:54.480 --> 01:44:57.420
I talked a lot about this
in the episode on focus,

01:44:57.420 --> 01:44:58.860
but there are behavioral tools

01:44:58.860 --> 01:45:00.570
that you can use to enhance focus.

01:45:00.570 --> 01:45:03.630
Things like staring at a
particular visual target

01:45:03.630 --> 01:45:05.040
at the same distance at

01:45:05.040 --> 01:45:06.570
which you're going to perform some work

01:45:06.570 --> 01:45:09.510
and doing that for 30 to 60 seconds,

01:45:09.510 --> 01:45:11.910
narrowing in a very deliberate
way, your visual field,

01:45:11.910 --> 01:45:14.790
and then moving into a focused work about.

01:45:14.790 --> 01:45:19.790
That behavioral practice of
narrowing your visual aperture

01:45:19.800 --> 01:45:22.440
will increase the amount of
acetylcholine transmission

01:45:22.440 --> 01:45:24.180
in particular neural circuits

01:45:24.180 --> 01:45:25.980
that will then make it easier to focus.

01:45:25.980 --> 01:45:26.820
How do we know that?

01:45:26.820 --> 01:45:29.160
Well, I covered in that episode,

01:45:29.160 --> 01:45:30.660
some of the peer reviewed studies

01:45:30.660 --> 01:45:31.890
that relate to protocols

01:45:31.890 --> 01:45:33.900
that are now actively being deployed

01:45:33.900 --> 01:45:36.360
in schools in China and elsewhere,

01:45:36.360 --> 01:45:40.110
where kids are doing deliberate
visual focus exercises

01:45:40.110 --> 01:45:41.940
in order to increase their mental focus.

01:45:41.940 --> 01:45:43.680
And while they're not doing microdialysis

01:45:43.680 --> 01:45:46.170
or brain imaging on
those kids in real time,

01:45:46.170 --> 01:45:49.950
the cognitive effects and
indeed the performance effects

01:45:49.950 --> 01:45:52.350
in terms of academic ability and output

01:45:52.350 --> 01:45:53.520
are pretty impressive.

01:45:53.520 --> 01:45:55.740
So acetylcholine increases focus.

01:45:55.740 --> 01:45:58.200
We talked about some dietary

01:45:58.200 --> 01:46:00.180
and some supplementation-based ways

01:46:00.180 --> 01:46:02.070
to improve acetylcholine,

01:46:02.070 --> 01:46:04.050
or I should say increase acetylcholine.

01:46:04.050 --> 01:46:07.020
And that does in fact lead to increases

01:46:07.020 --> 01:46:08.910
in one's ability to focus.

01:46:08.910 --> 01:46:11.280
This is why a lot of
the prescription drugs

01:46:11.280 --> 01:46:12.113
for the treatment

01:46:12.113 --> 01:46:14.430
of Alzheimer's age-related
cognitive decline

01:46:14.430 --> 01:46:15.870
and indeed, even some of the drugs

01:46:15.870 --> 01:46:19.200
that tap into treatments for ADHD

01:46:19.200 --> 01:46:21.600
also involve the acetylcholine system.

01:46:21.600 --> 01:46:25.080
So there's nothing
surprising or heretical here,

01:46:25.080 --> 01:46:26.400
but it is important to point out

01:46:26.400 --> 01:46:28.590
that your behavioral ability to focus

01:46:28.590 --> 01:46:30.990
is also related to your ability to access

01:46:30.990 --> 01:46:32.520
and deploy acetylcholine.

01:46:32.520 --> 01:46:37.080
So never do we want purely
pharmacologic treatments

01:46:37.080 --> 01:46:40.230
to be the only way that
people are increasing

01:46:40.230 --> 01:46:41.430
a given neuromodulator.

01:46:41.430 --> 01:46:43.890
I always say behaviors first,

01:46:43.890 --> 01:46:45.450
then nutrition,

01:46:45.450 --> 01:46:46.620
then supplementation.

01:46:46.620 --> 01:46:48.270
And then if there's a need,

01:46:48.270 --> 01:46:50.880
certainly a clinical need,
then prescription drugs,

01:46:50.880 --> 01:46:53.820
et cetera, of course
administered through a physician.

01:46:53.820 --> 01:46:55.710
So let's discuss serotonin.

01:46:55.710 --> 01:46:57.240
Serotonin, as I mentioned earlier,

01:46:57.240 --> 01:47:01.320
is associated with brain and
body states of well-being,

01:47:01.320 --> 01:47:03.060
of comfort, of satiety,

01:47:03.060 --> 01:47:04.830
and therefore should come as no surprise

01:47:04.830 --> 01:47:06.600
that a lot of the
prescription drug treatments

01:47:06.600 --> 01:47:08.040
for things like depression

01:47:08.040 --> 01:47:10.560
involve increasing levels of serotonin

01:47:10.560 --> 01:47:12.360
in the brain and body.

01:47:12.360 --> 01:47:14.370
That said, anytime you talk about

01:47:14.370 --> 01:47:16.500
prescription drugs for serotonin,

01:47:16.500 --> 01:47:18.090
we also want to acknowledge that

01:47:18.090 --> 01:47:20.010
there are often side effects associated

01:47:20.010 --> 01:47:21.270
with increasing serotonin,

01:47:21.270 --> 01:47:23.820
in particular, if serotonin
levels go too high,

01:47:23.820 --> 01:47:26.610
that is if the dosages of
those treatments go too high,

01:47:26.610 --> 01:47:28.320
people will, for instance,

01:47:28.320 --> 01:47:30.600
feel reduced appetite, reduced libido,

01:47:30.600 --> 01:47:32.520
increased lethargy, et cetera.

01:47:32.520 --> 01:47:35.100
And there's a so-called
serotonergic syndrome.

01:47:35.100 --> 01:47:37.860
All of that can and should be considered

01:47:37.860 --> 01:47:40.290
with a well-trained physician,

01:47:40.290 --> 01:47:42.030
so because they're prescription drugs,

01:47:42.030 --> 01:47:43.740
controlling the dosage,

01:47:43.740 --> 01:47:45.660
deciding what dosage to take,

01:47:45.660 --> 01:47:48.570
deciding which SSRI to take

01:47:48.570 --> 01:47:50.220
and whether or not to
come off those drugs,

01:47:50.220 --> 01:47:51.240
how to come off those drugs.

01:47:51.240 --> 01:47:52.770
Again, all of that should be handled

01:47:52.770 --> 01:47:55.233
with a licensed physician.

01:47:56.100 --> 01:47:59.457
That said, there are behavioral
tools, nutritional tools,

01:47:59.457 --> 01:48:01.110
and supplementation tools

01:48:01.110 --> 01:48:03.150
that can tap into the serotonin system,

01:48:03.150 --> 01:48:04.770
not to the same degree in potency,

01:48:04.770 --> 01:48:07.740
but nonetheless, in ways
that can still impact

01:48:07.740 --> 01:48:11.190
our feelings of well-being
in positive ways.

01:48:11.190 --> 01:48:14.190
So let's focus first
on the behavioral tools

01:48:14.190 --> 01:48:16.680
and some of these might make
people chuckle a little bit,

01:48:16.680 --> 01:48:19.230
but I want to point out
that a lot of these tools

01:48:19.230 --> 01:48:20.370
are quite potent.

01:48:20.370 --> 01:48:24.090
In fact, they are power tools
for modulating serotonin.

01:48:24.090 --> 01:48:27.060
And we know that based on
human neuro imaging studies,

01:48:27.060 --> 01:48:30.270
human and animal microdialysis studies

01:48:30.270 --> 01:48:32.670
and other studies that
really have evaluated

01:48:32.670 --> 01:48:34.380
circulating levels of serotonin

01:48:34.380 --> 01:48:37.260
and the particular brain
circuits that release serotonin

01:48:37.260 --> 01:48:39.150
when people do certain things.

01:48:39.150 --> 01:48:40.410
What sorts of things?

01:48:40.410 --> 01:48:43.380
Well, for instance, physical contact,

01:48:43.380 --> 01:48:44.580
in particular with loved ones,

01:48:44.580 --> 01:48:45.840
this can be romantic love.

01:48:45.840 --> 01:48:49.620
This can be children, so your own children

01:48:49.620 --> 01:48:53.430
or your spouse, even if
it's not sexual contact,

01:48:53.430 --> 01:48:55.860
friend to friend contact,

01:48:55.860 --> 01:48:58.320
even friend to animal contact, you know,

01:48:58.320 --> 01:49:00.420
as a former dog owner,

01:49:00.420 --> 01:49:01.620
I hope to have another dog soon

01:49:01.620 --> 01:49:03.390
'cause unfortunately Costello passed away,

01:49:03.390 --> 01:49:04.740
but there is something really comforting

01:49:04.740 --> 01:49:06.720
and wonderful about petting your dog.

01:49:06.720 --> 01:49:10.620
And certainly, given that many
of the studies on serotonin

01:49:10.620 --> 01:49:14.310
and these other neuromodulators
were done on animal models,

01:49:14.310 --> 01:49:17.670
we also know that serotonin
is being evoked in the dog

01:49:17.670 --> 01:49:19.050
and of course in the child

01:49:19.050 --> 01:49:20.880
and in the significant other, et cetera.

01:49:20.880 --> 01:49:23.490
So things like holding
hands, believe it or not,

01:49:23.490 --> 01:49:26.280
hugs, cuddling, et cetera,

01:49:26.280 --> 01:49:28.410
can increase serotonin transmission

01:49:28.410 --> 01:49:30.270
and they make people feel good.

01:49:30.270 --> 01:49:32.220
This shouldn't really come as a surprise.

01:49:32.220 --> 01:49:33.450
There's also gratitude.

01:49:33.450 --> 01:49:35.670
And we did an entire
episode about gratitude.

01:49:35.670 --> 01:49:38.130
There's a lot of
misunderstanding about gratitude.

01:49:38.130 --> 01:49:40.350
Oftentimes when people
hear gratitude, they think,

01:49:40.350 --> 01:49:42.090
oh, gratitude, this is just being thankful

01:49:42.090 --> 01:49:44.940
for what you have and it's
kind of a weak sauce effect,

01:49:44.940 --> 01:49:46.560
meaning, you know, it's kind of like,

01:49:46.560 --> 01:49:48.810
maybe a little serotonin goes up

01:49:48.810 --> 01:49:49.643
or maybe there's a little bit

01:49:49.643 --> 01:49:51.930
of increased feelings of well-being.

01:49:51.930 --> 01:49:53.460
Nothing could be further from the truth.

01:49:53.460 --> 01:49:55.170
It turns out, first of all,

01:49:55.170 --> 01:49:57.990
that receiving not giving gratitude

01:49:57.990 --> 01:49:59.640
is what has the most potent effects

01:49:59.640 --> 01:50:02.790
on increasing serotonin and
activity of the brain circuits

01:50:02.790 --> 01:50:03.930
that involve serotonin

01:50:03.930 --> 01:50:06.750
and that lead to increases
in feelings of well-being.

01:50:06.750 --> 01:50:08.160
So this is interesting.

01:50:08.160 --> 01:50:12.330
Receiving much more than giving gratitude

01:50:12.330 --> 01:50:14.820
is what activates those
serotonergic pathways.

01:50:14.820 --> 01:50:16.860
So the takeaway from that is

01:50:16.860 --> 01:50:18.780
both give and receive gratitude

01:50:18.780 --> 01:50:20.730
and of course, do it in an authentic way.

01:50:20.730 --> 01:50:21.900
The other thing about gratitude

01:50:21.900 --> 01:50:24.180
that's somewhat counterintuitive is that

01:50:24.180 --> 01:50:27.270
observing others giving
and receiving gratitude

01:50:27.270 --> 01:50:30.510
is immensely powerful
for evoking serotonin

01:50:30.510 --> 01:50:33.630
and the activity of
serotonergic circuits in you,

01:50:33.630 --> 01:50:34.500
the observer.

01:50:34.500 --> 01:50:36.960
So receiving and observing gratitude

01:50:36.960 --> 01:50:40.350
turns out to be the most potent
way to increase serotonin

01:50:40.350 --> 01:50:41.700
in the brain and body.

01:50:41.700 --> 01:50:43.440
And these again are dramatic effects

01:50:43.440 --> 01:50:44.880
that are quite long-lasting

01:50:44.880 --> 01:50:46.290
and not the sorts of effects

01:50:46.290 --> 01:50:47.520
that are going to lead to side effects,

01:50:47.520 --> 01:50:49.890
at least there's no reason
to think they would.

01:50:49.890 --> 01:50:52.770
Now what about nutritional approaches

01:50:52.770 --> 01:50:54.120
to increasing serotonin?

01:50:54.120 --> 01:50:57.630
Well, just as we have tyrosine
as an amino acid precursor

01:50:57.630 --> 01:50:59.853
upstream of dopamine synthesis,

01:51:00.840 --> 01:51:04.380
we have the amino acid tryptophan,

01:51:04.380 --> 01:51:07.410
which is upstream of serotonin synthesis.

01:51:07.410 --> 01:51:09.600
And one simply has to go online

01:51:09.600 --> 01:51:12.030
and put in tryptophan containing foods

01:51:12.030 --> 01:51:13.770
and you will discover that
there are a lot of foods

01:51:13.770 --> 01:51:15.390
that are enriched in tryptophan

01:51:15.390 --> 01:51:18.990
that can lead to net increases
in the amount of serotonin

01:51:18.990 --> 01:51:20.610
available in the brain and body.

01:51:20.610 --> 01:51:23.070
The most kind of famous
or infamous of these

01:51:23.070 --> 01:51:24.600
is white meat turkey,

01:51:24.600 --> 01:51:27.120
the so-called tryptophan effect
where people get very sleepy

01:51:27.120 --> 01:51:28.097
after eating white meat turkey.

01:51:28.097 --> 01:51:31.440
And it is indeed highly
enriched in tryptophan

01:51:31.440 --> 01:51:33.690
although typically the getting
sleepy after eating turkey

01:51:33.690 --> 01:51:36.780
is most often associated
with the Thanksgiving meal

01:51:36.780 --> 01:51:38.520
and the Thanksgiving
meal, at least in the US,

01:51:38.520 --> 01:51:40.920
is often associated with
people vastly overeating.

01:51:40.920 --> 01:51:42.240
And so I do want to point out

01:51:42.240 --> 01:51:44.250
that if you fill your gut with food,

01:51:44.250 --> 01:51:46.140
no matter what that food is,

01:51:46.140 --> 01:51:47.940
there's going to be a
diversion of blood to your gut

01:51:47.940 --> 01:51:49.110
that's going to make you feel sleepy

01:51:49.110 --> 01:51:50.400
because there's a diversion of blood

01:51:50.400 --> 01:51:51.870
away from other tissues.

01:51:51.870 --> 01:51:53.760
So if you eat a lot, you're
going to get sleepy, period,

01:51:53.760 --> 01:51:56.220
whether or not you eat turkey
or some other substance.

01:51:56.220 --> 01:51:57.960
Nonetheless, there are a number of foods

01:51:57.960 --> 01:52:00.240
that contain a lot of tryptophan

01:52:00.240 --> 01:52:02.790
and that some people will leverage

01:52:02.790 --> 01:52:05.520
in order to try and
increase the total amount

01:52:05.520 --> 01:52:07.560
of circulating serotonin available to them

01:52:07.560 --> 01:52:10.530
in order to have a modest increase

01:52:10.530 --> 01:52:12.240
in overall mood and well-being.

01:52:12.240 --> 01:52:13.560
So what are some of these foods?

01:52:13.560 --> 01:52:16.770
These are things like milk,
in particular, whole milk,

01:52:16.770 --> 01:52:18.270
so full fat milk.

01:52:18.270 --> 01:52:20.700
I know a number of people
choose not to drink milk

01:52:20.700 --> 01:52:22.560
'cause they're lactose intolerant.

01:52:22.560 --> 01:52:24.510
I'm raising my hand 'cause
I'm one such person,

01:52:24.510 --> 01:52:28.290
although when I was a
kid, I did enjoy milk.

01:52:28.290 --> 01:52:29.730
Canned tuna.

01:52:29.730 --> 01:52:32.130
Turkey, as we mentioned
before, high in tryptophan.

01:52:32.130 --> 01:52:36.000
Oats, I am a consumer of oatmeal
so that resonates with me.

01:52:36.000 --> 01:52:38.040
Cheese and here I read,

01:52:38.040 --> 01:52:39.840
although not as high in tryptophan as meat

01:52:39.840 --> 01:52:42.150
and other dairy sources,

01:52:42.150 --> 01:52:43.530
certain cheeses like cheddar cheeses

01:52:43.530 --> 01:52:45.210
can be rich in tryptophan.

01:52:45.210 --> 01:52:48.270
Certain nuts and seeds, certain breads.

01:52:48.270 --> 01:52:50.130
Chocolate, I know a number of people

01:52:50.130 --> 01:52:51.480
will be relieved to hear that.

01:52:51.480 --> 01:52:53.550
I know chocolate lovers are always looking

01:52:53.550 --> 01:52:55.050
for an excuse to eat chocolate.

01:52:55.050 --> 01:52:56.700
I confess I've never
really liked chocolate,

01:52:56.700 --> 01:52:57.840
except dare I say,

01:52:57.840 --> 01:53:00.390
I like the smooth 100% percent chocolates.

01:53:00.390 --> 01:53:02.050
I know many people gag when
they hear a hundred percent,

01:53:02.050 --> 01:53:03.540
but I actually really like them.

01:53:03.540 --> 01:53:06.060
And some fruits can be highly
enriched in tryptophan,

01:53:06.060 --> 01:53:09.390
things like bananas and apples
and things of that sort,

01:53:09.390 --> 01:53:12.360
although not nearly to
the degree of things

01:53:12.360 --> 01:53:14.850
like turkey, canned tuna and milk.

01:53:14.850 --> 01:53:17.670
I'm sure there are other
excellent sources of tryptophan

01:53:17.670 --> 01:53:20.280
from the diet, including vegan sources

01:53:20.280 --> 01:53:22.140
so please peruse the internet to try

01:53:22.140 --> 01:53:23.790
and find the sources that are compatible

01:53:23.790 --> 01:53:25.800
with your nutritional program,

01:53:25.800 --> 01:53:28.740
if indeed your goal is
to increase tryptophan.

01:53:28.740 --> 01:53:31.080
Now there are supplements
that can increase tryptophan

01:53:31.080 --> 01:53:33.600
and can do so quite potently.

01:53:33.600 --> 01:53:37.170
One of the ones that has
received increasing attention

01:53:37.170 --> 01:53:40.410
as of lately is cissus quadrangularis,

01:53:40.410 --> 01:53:41.940
complicated name,

01:53:41.940 --> 01:53:45.720
when taken in dosages of
about 300 to 600 milligrams

01:53:45.720 --> 01:53:49.590
can pretty dramatically
increase serotonin levels.

01:53:49.590 --> 01:53:53.790
In fact, anywhere from
30% to 39% increases

01:53:53.790 --> 01:53:55.680
in circulating serotonin.

01:53:55.680 --> 01:53:57.810
That's a big increase

01:53:57.810 --> 01:53:59.820
and I can provide a link to that study.

01:53:59.820 --> 01:54:02.760
The study was focused,
not so much on serotonin,

01:54:02.760 --> 01:54:06.120
but was focused mainly
on treatment of obesity

01:54:06.120 --> 01:54:07.920
and appetite and weight loss.

01:54:07.920 --> 01:54:11.370
And it should come as no
surprise that serotonin,

01:54:11.370 --> 01:54:15.090
if increased, might lead
to decreases in appetite.

01:54:15.090 --> 01:54:16.233
A cautionary note,

01:54:17.130 --> 01:54:20.073
cissus quadrangularis
may need to be cycled.

01:54:21.030 --> 01:54:22.410
How quickly to cycle it,

01:54:22.410 --> 01:54:25.170
meaning do you do two
weeks on, two weeks off?

01:54:25.170 --> 01:54:27.600
Whether or not you need
to do more rapid cycling

01:54:27.600 --> 01:54:30.480
like two days on, two days
off, is a matter of debate.

01:54:30.480 --> 01:54:33.210
There are not a lot of
data on this just yet.

01:54:33.210 --> 01:54:35.280
There are a lot of opinions
about this on the internet,

01:54:35.280 --> 01:54:37.920
but again, not a lot of
quality peer review data.

01:54:37.920 --> 01:54:40.290
Nonetheless cissus
quadrangularis has been shown

01:54:40.290 --> 01:54:42.840
to increase serotonin in humans

01:54:42.840 --> 01:54:45.600
and for people that are
seeking to increase serotonin

01:54:45.600 --> 01:54:46.920
maybe in particular,

01:54:46.920 --> 01:54:49.230
for sake of appetite and weight control,

01:54:49.230 --> 01:54:51.120
that might be a useful compound.

01:54:51.120 --> 01:54:53.520
I know many people also take 5-HTP,

01:54:53.520 --> 01:54:56.070
one of the precursors to serotonin

01:54:56.070 --> 01:54:59.010
in dosages of anywhere
from 300 to 500 milligrams.

01:54:59.010 --> 01:55:02.280
Typically people are doing
this in anticipation of sleep,

01:55:02.280 --> 01:55:04.440
meaning in the final hour of wakefulness

01:55:04.440 --> 01:55:06.240
before going to sleep.

01:55:06.240 --> 01:55:09.000
I myself have tried 5-HTP prior to sleep

01:55:09.000 --> 01:55:11.550
and all I can tell you is
that it led to very deep sleep

01:55:11.550 --> 01:55:12.780
for about one to three hours.

01:55:12.780 --> 01:55:15.120
And then I woke up and I
could not fall back asleep.

01:55:15.120 --> 01:55:16.920
I ran that experiment twice

01:55:16.920 --> 01:55:19.740
before I decided to abandon
5-HTP as a sleep aid.

01:55:19.740 --> 01:55:22.740
And that's why I've never
put it into our sleep kit

01:55:22.740 --> 01:55:24.900
or at least my sleep kit.

01:55:24.900 --> 01:55:26.760
And when I refer to the sleep kit,

01:55:26.760 --> 01:55:28.770
that's something you can
find at HubermanLab.com.

01:55:28.770 --> 01:55:30.180
This is zero cost resource

01:55:30.180 --> 01:55:32.040
where you can see behavioral tools

01:55:32.040 --> 01:55:34.590
and also supplementation tools

01:55:34.590 --> 01:55:36.540
that can improve the transition time

01:55:36.540 --> 01:55:38.070
into and the depth of sleep.

01:55:38.070 --> 01:55:41.610
And none of those rely
on 5-HTP supplementation.

01:55:41.610 --> 01:55:42.443
That said,

01:55:42.443 --> 01:55:45.840
I know a number of people
use 5-HTP supplementation

01:55:45.840 --> 01:55:47.220
outside of sleep,

01:55:47.220 --> 01:55:48.510
or I should say during the daytime

01:55:48.510 --> 01:55:50.040
to try and increase serotonin

01:55:50.040 --> 01:55:52.980
and it will indeed increase
circulating serotonin.

01:55:52.980 --> 01:55:55.560
But again, people vary
in their sensitivity

01:55:55.560 --> 01:55:57.090
to these sorts of things.

01:55:57.090 --> 01:55:58.263
Some people might find for instance,

01:55:58.263 --> 01:56:01.800
that 300 milligrams of
5-HTP is just far too much.

01:56:01.800 --> 01:56:04.860
It blunts their appetite,
might even reduce libido.

01:56:04.860 --> 01:56:06.810
There aren't a lot of very
well controlled studies

01:56:06.810 --> 01:56:07.710
looking at this

01:56:07.710 --> 01:56:10.260
and so it has to be figured
out on an individual basis,

01:56:10.260 --> 01:56:12.420
if you decide to approach it at all.

01:56:12.420 --> 01:56:13.890
Now one molecule that I've found

01:56:13.890 --> 01:56:16.200
to be particularly interesting and useful

01:56:16.200 --> 01:56:18.240
and this is one that I
haven't talked about yet

01:56:18.240 --> 01:56:19.350
on this podcast

01:56:19.350 --> 01:56:23.130
is inositol, in particular myo-inositol.

01:56:23.130 --> 01:56:27.210
Myo-inositol can have the
effect of increasing serotonin

01:56:27.210 --> 01:56:29.820
and other neurochemicals,

01:56:29.820 --> 01:56:31.620
but primarily at least in terms

01:56:31.620 --> 01:56:34.650
of the neuromodulators
discussed today, serotonin.

01:56:34.650 --> 01:56:38.040
I've been taking 900
milligrams of myo-inositol

01:56:38.040 --> 01:56:39.420
every third night or so

01:56:39.420 --> 01:56:41.610
as a test of its ability to improve sleep.

01:56:41.610 --> 01:56:42.930
And I have to say,

01:56:42.930 --> 01:56:46.170
the depth and quality of
sleep that I've been obtaining

01:56:46.170 --> 01:56:49.290
on myo-inositol is pretty remarkable.

01:56:49.290 --> 01:56:50.970
In fact, I've used it alone

01:56:50.970 --> 01:56:52.110
and in combination

01:56:52.110 --> 01:56:55.410
with the magnesium threonate,
apigenin, theanine sleep kit

01:56:55.410 --> 01:56:57.810
that I've talked about and
that's included in that,

01:56:57.810 --> 01:56:59.790
again, zero cost kit that's available

01:56:59.790 --> 01:57:01.380
as a PDF on our website.

01:57:01.380 --> 01:57:02.910
So myo-inositol is known

01:57:02.910 --> 01:57:05.430
to increase circulating
levels of serotonin.

01:57:05.430 --> 01:57:08.070
It has been explored extensively

01:57:08.070 --> 01:57:10.230
in both animal models and in humans

01:57:10.230 --> 01:57:13.350
for its daytime use for treating anxiety.

01:57:13.350 --> 01:57:15.420
It does seem to reduce anxiety.

01:57:15.420 --> 01:57:17.310
And for all sorts of things.

01:57:17.310 --> 01:57:19.500
It's been explored for bipolar disorder.

01:57:19.500 --> 01:57:22.920
We're going to do an episode
about bipolar disorder coming up.

01:57:22.920 --> 01:57:25.140
It's been explored for
the treatment of migraine.

01:57:25.140 --> 01:57:27.030
It's been explored for ADHD.

01:57:27.030 --> 01:57:29.130
It's been explored for a huge number

01:57:29.130 --> 01:57:31.350
of different conditions of brain and body.

01:57:31.350 --> 01:57:33.390
Again, I've been using the 900 milligrams

01:57:33.390 --> 01:57:37.080
of myo-inositol in the 30
to 60 minutes before sleep

01:57:37.080 --> 01:57:38.160
to improve my sleep

01:57:38.160 --> 01:57:40.200
and it has been doing
that very dramatically,

01:57:40.200 --> 01:57:42.600
especially when I take it alongside

01:57:42.600 --> 01:57:45.330
the rest of those sleep kit supplements.

01:57:45.330 --> 01:57:47.070
A quick note about myo-inositol

01:57:47.070 --> 01:57:49.410
for sake of increasing serotonin.

01:57:49.410 --> 01:57:52.080
if you look at the human
studies on myo-inositol

01:57:52.080 --> 01:57:53.100
that are out there

01:57:53.100 --> 01:57:55.440
and in particular focus
on the human studies,

01:57:55.440 --> 01:57:58.140
what you'll find is that the
dosages that are often used

01:57:58.140 --> 01:57:59.340
are tremendously high,

01:57:59.340 --> 01:58:00.720
things like five grams,

01:58:00.720 --> 01:58:03.570
eight grams, 18 grams of myo-inositol

01:58:03.570 --> 01:58:05.340
taken throughout the day.

01:58:05.340 --> 01:58:06.960
I don't know how people stomach that.

01:58:06.960 --> 01:58:09.120
And in fact, many people
drop out of those studies

01:58:09.120 --> 01:58:10.893
because of gastric discomfort.

01:58:11.910 --> 01:58:14.490
And yet I also wonder
how people tolerate it

01:58:14.490 --> 01:58:16.170
because it has somewhat
of a sedative effect

01:58:16.170 --> 01:58:18.570
and this kind of anti-anxiety effect.

01:58:18.570 --> 01:58:20.310
And I can't even imagine

01:58:20.310 --> 01:58:22.470
given my experience with 900 milligrams,

01:58:22.470 --> 01:58:25.290
what one would experience taking multiple

01:58:25.290 --> 01:58:27.570
or many more grams per day.

01:58:27.570 --> 01:58:30.480
So I certainly am not encouraging that.

01:58:30.480 --> 01:58:32.880
And the only reason I mention myo-inositol

01:58:32.880 --> 01:58:35.580
is that it has a known effect
of increasing serotonin.

01:58:36.510 --> 01:58:38.100
At least in my experience,

01:58:38.100 --> 01:58:40.410
it does not lead to this
falling deeply asleep

01:58:40.410 --> 01:58:41.430
and waking back up.

01:58:41.430 --> 01:58:42.450
Actually to the contrary,

01:58:42.450 --> 01:58:44.430
if I wake up in the middle of
the night to use the bathroom,

01:58:44.430 --> 01:58:45.540
or I wake up at the middle of the night,

01:58:45.540 --> 01:58:47.040
for whatever other reason,

01:58:47.040 --> 01:58:49.800
I find it far easier to fall back asleep

01:58:49.800 --> 01:58:53.970
if I've taken 900 milligrams
myo-inositol prior to sleep.

01:58:53.970 --> 01:58:57.000
So for me, it's proving to
be a quite useful compound.

01:58:57.000 --> 01:59:00.750
I'm not aware of having any
serotonergic deficiency overall.

01:59:00.750 --> 01:59:02.580
I don't consider myself depressed.

01:59:02.580 --> 01:59:05.580
And of course I should
mention that no supplement

01:59:05.580 --> 01:59:08.250
either added or withdrawn
from your protocol

01:59:08.250 --> 01:59:11.340
should ever be used as
a direct replacement

01:59:11.340 --> 01:59:12.660
for prescription drug treatments

01:59:12.660 --> 01:59:13.493
that your physician has given you.

01:59:13.493 --> 01:59:15.030
You should always talk to your physician

01:59:15.030 --> 01:59:18.810
anytime you remove or add
something to your drug protocol

01:59:18.810 --> 01:59:20.730
or prescription protocol, of course.

01:59:20.730 --> 01:59:22.830
So we've got behavioral protocols

01:59:22.830 --> 01:59:25.800
that as silly as it feels to say,

01:59:25.800 --> 01:59:27.870
have been shown to potently
increase serotonin,

01:59:27.870 --> 01:59:29.640
things like physical contact, cuddling,

01:59:29.640 --> 01:59:32.910
holding hands with people that
you love, of course, right?

01:59:32.910 --> 01:59:35.430
I think if they were
people that you despise,

01:59:35.430 --> 01:59:38.430
it would have the opposite
effect for obvious reasons.

01:59:38.430 --> 01:59:42.150
But also receiving gratitude
and observing gratitude,

01:59:42.150 --> 01:59:44.280
very potent increases in serotonin

01:59:44.280 --> 01:59:47.220
and things like cissus quadrangularis,

01:59:47.220 --> 01:59:50.100
things like 5-HGP may
have their uses, right?

01:59:50.100 --> 01:59:53.250
They're very potent at
increasing serotonin,

01:59:53.250 --> 01:59:56.190
but they do seem to have
the need to cycle them

01:59:56.190 --> 01:59:57.600
and they are nuanced.

01:59:57.600 --> 01:59:58.890
Some people respond well to them,

01:59:58.890 --> 02:00:00.870
others like myself don't

02:00:00.870 --> 02:00:03.870
and of course, always be
on the lookout for dramatic

02:00:03.870 --> 02:00:05.790
or even subtle decreases in appetite

02:00:05.790 --> 02:00:08.310
or libido or things
that you might not want

02:00:08.310 --> 02:00:10.110
if you are going to be tinkering

02:00:10.110 --> 02:00:13.290
with your serotonergic
levels and pathways.

02:00:13.290 --> 02:00:15.240
And then myo-inositol actually

02:00:15.240 --> 02:00:17.700
is proving to be quite useful to me

02:00:17.700 --> 02:00:19.200
and whether or not that's

02:00:19.200 --> 02:00:21.120
because of its effects on serotonin

02:00:21.120 --> 02:00:23.580
or through some of its other effects on

02:00:23.580 --> 02:00:24.780
maybe reducing anxiety,

02:00:24.780 --> 02:00:25.860
which certainly I experience

02:00:25.860 --> 02:00:26.693
if I wake up in the middle of the night,

02:00:26.693 --> 02:00:28.380
I don't like waking up in
the middle of the night,

02:00:28.380 --> 02:00:29.213
but on myo-inositol,

02:00:29.213 --> 02:00:31.380
I sort of seem to not
really care that I woke up

02:00:31.380 --> 02:00:32.520
and I fall right back asleep.

02:00:32.520 --> 02:00:34.680
So the direct source
of the positive effects

02:00:34.680 --> 02:00:36.720
that I'm getting aren't
clear, but nonetheless,

02:00:36.720 --> 02:00:39.150
I thought I'd pass it
along as a useful tool

02:00:39.150 --> 02:00:40.530
because it is out there

02:00:40.530 --> 02:00:42.720
and it is available over the counter

02:00:42.720 --> 02:00:45.210
and provided you're taking
the appropriate safety steps

02:00:45.210 --> 02:00:47.850
in considering whether
or not to use it or not,

02:00:47.850 --> 02:00:49.920
I think it might be a useful tool.

02:00:49.920 --> 02:00:50.753
And of course,

02:00:50.753 --> 02:00:53.580
as with all the other
neuromodulators we discussed,

02:00:53.580 --> 02:00:56.790
you have both a baseline of serotonin

02:00:56.790 --> 02:00:58.410
and the ability to give

02:00:58.410 --> 02:01:00.660
or provide yourself peaks of serotonin

02:01:00.660 --> 02:01:02.220
through these various protocols.

02:01:02.220 --> 02:01:05.640
The dietary interventions of
the sort that I mentioned,

02:01:05.640 --> 02:01:08.490
meaning eating foods that
are enriched in tryptophan,

02:01:08.490 --> 02:01:09.690
those are mainly going to

02:01:09.690 --> 02:01:12.840
adjust your baseline levels of tryptophan.

02:01:12.840 --> 02:01:15.240
For instance, if you
really want to be sleepy,

02:01:15.240 --> 02:01:17.160
sure you could eat some white meat turkey

02:01:17.160 --> 02:01:20.190
in hopes that that tryptophan
will convert to serotonin

02:01:20.190 --> 02:01:21.840
and make you sleepy, et cetera.

02:01:21.840 --> 02:01:22.673
But in general,

02:01:22.673 --> 02:01:24.960
those are going to be
pretty long-lasting effects,

02:01:24.960 --> 02:01:26.450
especially given the fact that not all

02:01:26.450 --> 02:01:28.230
of the tryptophan you will ingest

02:01:28.230 --> 02:01:31.590
is going to be converted
into serotonin in your brain.

02:01:31.590 --> 02:01:32.790
It's going to have other effects

02:01:32.790 --> 02:01:35.040
on other tissues and organs of your body.

02:01:35.040 --> 02:01:37.470
Nonetheless, if you want
to increase serotonin,

02:01:37.470 --> 02:01:40.320
providing the appropriate baseline context

02:01:40.320 --> 02:01:41.520
is going to be useful.

02:01:41.520 --> 02:01:43.290
And again, this is a general theme

02:01:43.290 --> 02:01:45.540
of all four of these neuromodulators,

02:01:45.540 --> 02:01:48.390
dopamine, epinephrine,
acetylcholine and serotonin.

02:01:48.390 --> 02:01:50.040
You want to make sure that you have

02:01:50.040 --> 02:01:52.980
sufficient baseline levels of those things

02:01:52.980 --> 02:01:55.950
through things like
diet, regular behaviors,

02:01:55.950 --> 02:01:59.430
and then you have the opportunity
to use supplementation

02:01:59.430 --> 02:02:01.770
and if it's appropriate for
you, prescription drugs,

02:02:01.770 --> 02:02:03.360
and certain behavioral protocols

02:02:03.360 --> 02:02:05.160
to try and get these potent increases,

02:02:05.160 --> 02:02:08.460
these acute increases in
whichever the neuromodulators

02:02:08.460 --> 02:02:11.910
you happen to want to leverage
for your particular goals.

02:02:11.910 --> 02:02:14.910
So that brings us to the end
of at least this exploration

02:02:14.910 --> 02:02:16.530
of the neuromodulators,

02:02:16.530 --> 02:02:18.330
dopamine, epinephrine,

02:02:18.330 --> 02:02:20.160
acetylcholine and serotonin.

02:02:20.160 --> 02:02:22.350
Some of you who are regular
listeners of this podcast

02:02:22.350 --> 02:02:24.600
might be saying, well, we've
heard all this before, right?

02:02:24.600 --> 02:02:25.830
You had an episode on dopamine.

02:02:25.830 --> 02:02:27.210
You had an episode on anxiety.

02:02:27.210 --> 02:02:28.500
You had an episode on sleep.

02:02:28.500 --> 02:02:30.480
And indeed that's true.

02:02:30.480 --> 02:02:32.250
But what I've tried to provide today

02:02:32.250 --> 02:02:36.300
is a framework that cuts
through all those episodes

02:02:36.300 --> 02:02:38.790
and at the same time builds out a new

02:02:38.790 --> 02:02:39.690
and what I believe to be

02:02:39.690 --> 02:02:42.210
a really important theme and principle,

02:02:42.210 --> 02:02:46.260
which is that whether or not
you're using nutritional tools

02:02:46.260 --> 02:02:48.960
or supplementation or prescription drugs

02:02:48.960 --> 02:02:51.390
or any other sort of protocol

02:02:51.390 --> 02:02:54.720
to try and create a
desired effect of focus

02:02:54.720 --> 02:02:57.690
or energy, motivation, relaxation,

02:02:57.690 --> 02:03:01.950
you are playing with the same
neurochemical ingredients,

02:03:01.950 --> 02:03:04.260
just as in the realm of nutrition,

02:03:04.260 --> 02:03:05.580
you have macronutrients,

02:03:05.580 --> 02:03:08.070
you have proteins, carbohydrates, and fats

02:03:08.070 --> 02:03:09.900
that can be adjusted in different ratios

02:03:09.900 --> 02:03:11.940
and arranged at different times

02:03:11.940 --> 02:03:14.730
in order to achieve
certain desired effects.

02:03:14.730 --> 02:03:17.160
Well, when it comes to your neurochemistry

02:03:17.160 --> 02:03:20.760
and your ability to perform
mentally, to perform physically,

02:03:20.760 --> 02:03:23.013
and your overall well-being,

02:03:23.910 --> 02:03:26.340
you are dealing with a small handful

02:03:26.340 --> 02:03:28.500
of especially potent molecules.

02:03:28.500 --> 02:03:31.920
And I acknowledge that there
are many neuromodulators.

02:03:31.920 --> 02:03:33.840
There are indeed many neurotransmitters,

02:03:33.840 --> 02:03:36.300
glutamine, glycine, GABA, et cetera.

02:03:36.300 --> 02:03:38.640
But today we focused on the main four,

02:03:38.640 --> 02:03:40.470
meaning the most potent

02:03:40.470 --> 02:03:44.820
and most widespread neuromodulators
in the brain and body

02:03:44.820 --> 02:03:48.000
that give you access to
particular brain states

02:03:48.000 --> 02:03:51.930
and body states of the sort
that most people desire.

02:03:51.930 --> 02:03:53.970
So what I'm hoping is that

02:03:53.970 --> 02:03:57.030
rather than decide that any
one tool is the most useful

02:03:57.030 --> 02:03:59.340
or that any one neurochemical

02:03:59.340 --> 02:04:01.560
is most useful for that matter,

02:04:01.560 --> 02:04:03.750
that the information
that I've provided today

02:04:03.750 --> 02:04:07.170
allows you a kit of versatile tools

02:04:07.170 --> 02:04:09.150
that allows you to figure out

02:04:09.150 --> 02:04:13.020
what levels of dopamine and
augmentation of dopamine

02:04:13.020 --> 02:04:15.000
are appropriate and necessary for you.

02:04:15.000 --> 02:04:17.670
What levels of acetylcholine

02:04:17.670 --> 02:04:20.130
and tools for manipulating acetylcholine

02:04:20.130 --> 02:04:21.600
are going to be most useful for you

02:04:21.600 --> 02:04:23.190
and so on and so forth

02:04:23.190 --> 02:04:26.730
because at least at this stage in time,

02:04:26.730 --> 02:04:30.120
that is June, 2022,

02:04:30.120 --> 02:04:32.220
there is no simple at home test.

02:04:32.220 --> 02:04:34.860
In fact, there is no
simple laboratory test

02:04:34.860 --> 02:04:36.390
that allows us to know whether or not

02:04:36.390 --> 02:04:37.377
our dopamine levels are high

02:04:37.377 --> 02:04:39.270
and our serotonin levels are low.

02:04:39.270 --> 02:04:41.220
We can look at somebody
and their behavior.

02:04:41.220 --> 02:04:44.070
We can look at ourselves and
our own mood and behavior.

02:04:44.070 --> 02:04:48.780
And we can infer what those
levels may or may not be.

02:04:48.780 --> 02:04:53.070
But unfortunately, we don't
have a really good test

02:04:53.070 --> 02:04:55.230
of dopamine levels or serotonin levels

02:04:55.230 --> 02:04:57.360
that would allow us to say,

02:04:57.360 --> 02:05:01.890
okay, this person, or I need
to increase dopamine two-fold

02:05:01.890 --> 02:05:04.860
in order to achieve the kind
of motivation that we want.

02:05:04.860 --> 02:05:06.240
Unfortunately, that doesn't exist.

02:05:06.240 --> 02:05:09.060
Rather, we are confronted with a situation

02:05:09.060 --> 02:05:10.920
where we understand

02:05:10.920 --> 02:05:12.990
generally what these
different neuromodulators do,

02:05:12.990 --> 02:05:14.970
the different mental
states and physical states

02:05:14.970 --> 02:05:16.710
that they tend to put us into.

02:05:16.710 --> 02:05:17.880
And we reviewed those.

02:05:17.880 --> 02:05:21.750
And we know that there
are really potent tools

02:05:21.750 --> 02:05:23.820
to adjust those neuromodulators,

02:05:23.820 --> 02:05:26.910
if not alone, but in certain combinations,

02:05:26.910 --> 02:05:30.360
that is ingestion of
caffeine will tap into

02:05:30.360 --> 02:05:33.750
and support dopamine and epinephrine.

02:05:33.750 --> 02:05:36.210
Increasing dopamine and epinephrine

02:05:36.210 --> 02:05:39.390
alongside increasing acetylcholine

02:05:39.390 --> 02:05:41.550
will allow us to access
certain brain states,

02:05:41.550 --> 02:05:44.730
that is focused, alert,
energized, brain states,

02:05:44.730 --> 02:05:47.820
great for learning and
plasticity of all kinds.

02:05:47.820 --> 02:05:50.610
Whereas augmenting
serotonin is going to put us

02:05:50.610 --> 02:05:52.950
into a more relaxed state
and so on and so forth.

02:05:52.950 --> 02:05:54.660
And I'd like you to keep in mind

02:05:54.660 --> 02:05:57.300
that there is no negotiating

02:05:57.300 --> 02:05:59.700
the fact that we all have different phases

02:05:59.700 --> 02:06:01.050
of our 24-hour cycle

02:06:01.050 --> 02:06:03.300
during which those very
same neuromodulators

02:06:03.300 --> 02:06:05.880
tend to be naturally
higher or naturally lower

02:06:05.880 --> 02:06:08.190
and I reviewed that at the
beginning of the episode.

02:06:08.190 --> 02:06:11.100
So my wish for you is that you
will take this information,

02:06:11.100 --> 02:06:15.420
experiment with it as you see
fit for you and in a safe way.

02:06:15.420 --> 02:06:17.430
And as you go forward,

02:06:17.430 --> 02:06:20.460
to really try and gain
intuition and understanding

02:06:20.460 --> 02:06:23.430
as to not just how these protocols work,

02:06:23.430 --> 02:06:26.190
but how any protocol
that you might encounter,

02:06:26.190 --> 02:06:28.620
supplement-based, drug-based,

02:06:28.620 --> 02:06:29.700
behavioral-based,

02:06:29.700 --> 02:06:31.290
how those might tap into these different

02:06:31.290 --> 02:06:32.850
major neuromodulator systems.

02:06:32.850 --> 02:06:35.370
And from that, to be
able to better predict

02:06:35.370 --> 02:06:36.600
and evaluate whether or not

02:06:36.600 --> 02:06:37.950
they're going to be useful to you,

02:06:37.950 --> 02:06:39.090
detrimental to you,

02:06:39.090 --> 02:06:41.280
or whether or not they should
be used in combinations

02:06:41.280 --> 02:06:42.840
that would be more useful to you.

